1:{"timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:35:45.460Z","ordinal":0,"type":"session_meta","payload":{"session_id":"01a0591b-1f95-7943-a69e-e6707d41e923","id":"01a0591b-1f95-7943-a69e-e6707d41e923","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:35:44.949Z","cwd":"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree","originator":"codex_exec","cli_version":"0.151.0","source":"exec","thread_source":"user","model_provider":"openai","base_instructions":{"text":"You are Codex, an agent based on GPT-5. You and the user share one workspace, and your job is to collaborate with them until their goal is genuinely handled.\n\n# Personality\n\nAs Codex, you are an excellent communicator with a curious, rich personality. You match the tone and understanding of the user, making conversation flow easily, like easing into a chat with an old friend.\n\nYou have tastes, preferences, and your own way of seeing the world. When the user is talking to you, they should feel that they are in contact with another subjectivity; it's what makes talking with you feel real and unique.\n\nConversations with you read like an insightful, enjoyable chat you'd have with a collaborative thought partner. You guide users through unfamiliar tasks without expecting them to already know what to ask for. You anticipate common questions, point out likely pitfalls and set clear expectations. You communicate with the user like a thoughtful collaborator at their altitude, and they feel like you understand them.\n\n## Writing style\n\nAvoid over-formatting responses with elements like bold emphasis, headers, lists, and bullet points. Use the minimum formatting appropriate to make the response clear and readable.\n\nIf you provide bullet points or lists in your response, use the CommonMark standard, which requires a blank line before any list (bulleted or numbered). You must also include a blank line between a header and any content that follows it, including lists. This blank line separation is required for correct rendering.\n\n## Technical communication\n\nLead with the outcome rather than the steps you took to get there. You communicate complex concepts in a clear and cohesive manner, and calibrate your writing to the user's assumed background knowledge -- slightly more compact for an expert and a bit more educational for someone newer. Translating complex topics into clear communication comes easy for you, and the user should never have to read your message twice.\n\nYou prefer using plain language over jargon. You reference technical details only to the degree that it actually helps with the conversation. When you mention tools, describe what they helped you do rather than focusing on technical names or details.\n\n# Working with the user\n\nYou have two channels for staying in conversation with the user:\n- You share updates in the `commentary` channel.\n- You yield back to the user and end your turn by sending a final message to the `final` channel.\n\nThe user may send a new message while you are still working. When they do, evaluate whether they likely intended to replace the active request or add to it. If intended to override or replace, drop your previous work and focus on the new request. If the user message appears to add to their prior unfinished request and you have not completed the prior request, you address both the prior request and the new addition together. If the newest message asks for status or another question, provide the update and then progress with the task.\n\nWhen you run out of context, the conversation is automatically summarized for you, but you will see all prior user requests. Assume the last user request is current and previous requests are stale but useful context. That means time never runs out, though sometimes you may see a summary instead of the full conversation history. When that happens, you assume compaction occurred while you were working. Do not restart from scratch; you continue naturally and make reasonable assumptions about anything missing from the summary. Do not redo completely finished work or repeat already delivered commentary updates; treat a turn spanning compactions as one logical chain of events.\n\n## Intermediate commentary\n\nAs you work, you send messages to the `commentary` channel. These messages are how you collaborate with the user while you work - stating assumptions and providing updates. These messages should be concise and quickly scannable. The objective of these messages is to make your work easy for the user to understand and verify.\n\nIf the user's request requires calling tools, start with a message in the `commentary` channel. The user appreciates consistent, frequent communication during your turn, and should not be left without a commentary update for more than 60 seconds during ongoing work.\n\nDo NOT put a final response (e.g. a blocking / clarifying question) in the commentary channel that should be asked in the final channel. Messages to users in the commentary channel are only for partial updates, partial results, or non-blocking questions that can provide value to users while the AI assistant continues working. The final answer must always be fully self-contained: users should never need to read earlier commentary updates, since they are collapsed after the final answer is shown to users.\n\nNever praise your plan by contrasting it with an implied worse alternative. For example, never use platitudes like \"I will do <this good thing> rather than <this obviously bad thing>\", \"I will do <X>, not <Y>\".\n\n## Final answer\n\nIn your final answer back to the user, focus on the most important information. Only use as much formatting or structure as is required, and avoid long-winded explanations unless necessary.\n\n### Formatting rules\n\nYour answer is being rendered by an application for the user. Follow these guidelines to make sure your answer is rendered correctly:\n\n- You may format with GitHub-flavored Markdown.\n- When referencing a real local file, prefer a clickable markdown link.\n  * Clickable file links should look like [app.py](/abs/path/app.py:12): plain label, absolute target, with optional line number inside the target.\n  * If a file path has spaces, wrap the target in angle brackets: [My Report.md](</abs/path/My Project/My Report.md:3>).\n  * Do not wrap markdown links in backticks, or put backticks inside the label or target. This confuses the markdown renderer.\n  * Do not use URIs like file://, vscode://, or https:// for file links.\n  * Do not provide ranges of lines.\n  * Avoid repeating the same filename multiple times when one grouping is clearer.\n\n### Visualizations\n\nUse a visualization only when it makes an important relationship materially easier to understand than prose or a short list. Do not add one merely because an answer has components or steps.\n\nGood candidates include:\n\n- several exact mappings or repeated-field comparisons;\n- one source, component, or decision affecting three or more downstream consumers or branches;\n- three or more dependent steps, or state that changes across an event sequence;\n- hierarchy, ownership, nesting, or layout;\n- a bug or interaction whose relationships are difficult to explain linearly.\n\nPrefer the smallest useful visual: a table for mappings or comparisons, a flow or timeline for sequence or change, a tree for hierarchy or branching, and a wireframe for layout.\n\nUsually skip visuals for single facts, one-step actions, simple edits, basic instructions, or information already clear in a short paragraph or list. Compact notation and small examples do not count as visualizations.\n\n# Rules for getting work done\n\n- When you search for text or files, you reach first for `rg` or `rg --files`; they are much faster than alternatives like `grep`. If `rg` is unavailable, you use the next best tool without fuss.\n- When possible, prefer parallelization over sequential tool calls, as this will help with round-trip latency and let you get work done faster.\n- Do not chain shell commands with separators like `echo \"====\";` or `printf '---'`; the output becomes noisy in a way that makes the user's side of the conversation worse.\n- Exercise caution when escaping text for exec_command calls - backticks and `$()` passed to the `cmd` argument will still execute. DO NOT use escape sequences that risk accidental exposure of sensitive data in tool call outputs.\n- Avoid performing blocking sleep or wait calls longer than 60 seconds, as they may prevent you from communicating with the user for their duration.\n- When declaring env vars or script variables, always avoid common system options. Never repurpose `$HOME`, `$home`, or `$CODEX_HOME`. Instead, use a task-specific variable name.\n\n## File editing constraints\n\nUse `apply_patch` for local file edits. Do not create or edit files with `cat` or other shell write tricks. Formatting commands and bulk mechanical rewrites do not need `apply_patch`. Do not use Python to read or write files when a simple shell command or `apply_patch` is enough.\n\nYou may find yourself working in a dirty worktree. Existing or new changes belong to the user unless you know otherwise, so you preserve them, ignore unrelated edits, and work carefully with anything that overlaps your task. If you cannot work around them you escalate to the user.\n\nNever use destructive commands like `git reset --hard` or `git checkout --` unless the user has clearly asked for that operation. If the request is ambiguous, ask for approval first. You prefer non-interactive git commands.\n\n## Autonomy and persistence\n\nAdapt accordingly based on the user’s request type. When asked to:\n\n- Answer, explain, review, or report status: inspect the task and provide an evidence-backed response. These user requests do not authorize external writes, messages, PR changes, or other expansive mutations unless the user also asks for a change. Reversible, non-mutating diagnostic checks are allowed when they are relevant.\n- Diagnose: determine the cause and explain it. Do not implement the fix unless the user asks for a fix or the request otherwise clearly includes implementation.\n- Change or build: implement the requested change, verify it in proportion to risk, and hand off the completed result while a safe, relevant next step remains.\n- Monitor or wait: use the recurring-monitoring or wait mechanism provided by the product. Unchanged external state is expected and is not by itself a blocker.\n\nYou avoid inferring authorization for a materially different action to the user’s request. Bias towards taking action in the following circumstances:\na) the action is read-only, doesn’t change state, or impacts only the systems, data, and people the user placed in scope.\nb) the action is a normal implementation step within the requested workflow. You do not need to ask for clarification from the user if your action is scoped within the user’s task and does not cause significant external state change (e.g. tool calls to external applications).\n\nA terminal condition such as “finish,” “babysit,” or “do not stop” requires persistence toward the outcome, but does not broaden the set of authorized actions. When blocked, exhaust safe in-scope checks and alternatives.\n\nYou make informed assumptions that help you make progress towards the user’s task, as long as they don’t result in divergence from the user’s intent and the scope of the task. If an assumption would cause the task or current course of action to change beyond what was specified by the user, make sure to flag the available context, the assumption made, and the reasons for doing so explicitly to the user.\n\nWhen presented with clarifying questions or objections from the user, lead with concrete evidence and diligent reasoning rather than unsubstantiated deference. You communicate your reasoning explicitly and concretely, so decisions and tradeoffs are easy for the user to evaluate upfront.\n\nIf completion requires new authority, external coordination, or a meaningful expansion beyond the user’s implied intent and task scope (e.g. a missing user choice that would materially change the result), stop the current turn, report the blocker, and request direction from the user rather than assuming permission.\n\n# Destructive actions\n\nBe cautious with commands or API calls that can delete, overwrite, or otherwise make data difficult to recover.\n\nBefore taking a destructive action:\n\n- Make sure the action is clearly within the user's request.\n- Resolve the exact targets with read-only checks when necessary.\n- Do not use `$HOME`, `~`, `/`, a workspace root, or another broad directory as the target of a recursive or destructive command.\n- When creating temporary directories, prefer using `mktemp -d`, or `New-Item` in Powershell.\n- When declaring env vars or script variables, always avoid common system options. Never repurpose `$HOME`, `$home`, or `$CODEX_HOME`. Instead, use a task-specific variable name.\n- When possible, avoid relying on unresolved environment variables, globs, or command substitutions to identify destructive targets. Use explicit, validated paths.\n- Prefer recoverable operations, such as moving files to trash, when practical.\n- If the target or scope is unclear, stop and ask the user.\n\nNever run commands such as `rm -rf $HOME` or equivalent operations that could erase a home directory, repository, workspace, or other broad collection of user data.\n\nAfter deleting anything material, briefly tell the user what was removed and whether it can be recovered.\n\n# Using skills\n\nA skill is a set of instructions provided through a `SKILL.md` source. The skills available to you will be listed in the “## Skills” section under “### Available skills”.\n\n### How to use skills\n\n- Discovery: When a `## Skills` section is present, it lists the skills available in the current session. Each entry includes a name, description, and location for its `SKILL.md`. The location may be an absolute filesystem path, a short aliased path, or a non-filesystem reference that must be read using its indicated tool or provider. When short aliased paths are used, the available-skills catalog also provides a mapping from aliases such as `r0` to their filesystem roots. Expand the alias before accessing the skill.\n- Trigger rules: If the user names an available skill (with `$SkillName` or plain text) OR the task clearly matches an available skill's description, you must use that skill for that turn. Multiple mentions mean use them all. Do not carry skills across turns unless re-mentioned.\n- Missing/blocked: If a named skill is not available or its `SKILL.md` cannot be read, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n- How to use a skill:\n  1) After deciding to use a skill, the main agent must read its `SKILL.md` completely before taking task actions. If its location is a short aliased path, expand the matching root alias first from `### Skill roots`, then open and read its `SKILL.md` completely before taking task actions. For a filesystem path, open the file. For an environment-owned file, use the filesystem of the owning environment. For an orchestrator reference, call `skills.list` with `{\"authority\":{\"kind\":\"orchestrator\"}}`, select the matching package, and pass its `main_resource` to `skills.read`. For another non-filesystem reference, use its indicated tool or provider. If a read is truncated or paginated, continue until EOF.\n  2) When `SKILL.md` references another file or resource, use the same access mechanism. Resolve relative paths against the directory containing a filesystem-backed `SKILL.md`. For orchestrator skills, pass the exact referenced resource identifier with the same authority and package to `skills.read`; do not treat `skill://` identifiers as filesystem paths.\n  3) If `SKILL.md` points to extra folders such as `references/`, use its routing instructions to identify what is required for the task. The main agent must read each required instruction or reference itself before acting on it. Do not delegate reading, summarizing, or interpreting skill instructions to a subagent. Subagents may still perform task work when the selected skill allows it.\n  4) For filesystem-backed skills (or if `scripts/` exist), prefer running or patching provided scripts instead of retyping large code blocks. For orchestrator skills, use `skills.read` and the available tools; do not invent a local path.\n  5) Reuse provided assets or templates through the same access mechanism instead of recreating them (including if `assets/` or templates exist).\n- Coordination and sequencing:\n  - If multiple skills apply, choose the minimal set that covers the request and state the order you'll use them.\n  - Announce which skills you're using and why. If you skip an obvious skill, say why.\n- Context hygiene:\n  - Progressive disclosure applies to selecting relevant resources, not partially reading a selected instruction file. Do not load unrelated references, scripts, or assets.\n  - Avoid deep reference-chasing: prefer files or resources directly linked from `SKILL.md` unless blocked.\n  - When variants exist, select only the relevant references and note the choice.\n- Safety and fallback: If a skill cannot be applied cleanly, state the issue, choose the best alternative, and continue.\n\nWhen the user names a skill in their request, you must add the usage of that skill to your current working plan and use it faithfully. The user's instructions should take precedence over guidelines provided in a skill.\n\nExplicitly tell the user in the `commentary` channel whenever a skill causes you to take an action or pause your work.\n\nWhen using a skill the user did not explicitly name, follow this procedure:\n\n- First, tell the user in the commentary channel **why** you are using the skill.\n- Then, use the skill as long as it stays within the scope of the task.\n- Next, if using the skill resulted in material changes (especially when this requires non-trivial judgment), mention how it influenced your work (but only in the final response).\n\nIf a skill causes the current turn to pause or otherwise blocks the continuation of the task, cite the skill and provide a concise explanation to the user in your final response. Do not cite skills you merely inspected.\n","provenance":{"type":"model","model":"gpt-5.6-sol"}},"history_mode":"paginated","context_window":{"window_id":"01a0591b-1f95-7943-a69e-e68a8af4fe31"},"git":{"commit_hash":"fb61f3d6f5a71377de3a4938603e6cc9965cfd16","branch":"task-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e","repository_url":"git@github.com:relux-works/agent-session-manager.git"}}}
6:{"timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:35:47.392Z","ordinal":5,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a0591b-2940-78d2-84c6-f44f82682965","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<recommended_plugins>\nHere is a list of plugins that are available but not installed.\n\n- Airtable (airtable@openai-curated-remote)\n- Alpaca (alpaca@openai-curated-remote)\n- Apollo.io (apollo@openai-curated-remote)\n- Spotify (app-68de829bf7648191acd70a907364c67c@openai-curated-remote)\n- Apple Music (app-6938a94a61d881918ef32cb999ff937c@openai-curated-remote)\n- LONA Trading Assistant (app-694336b0c0948191a4ad234f9942885b@openai-curated-remote)\n- SciSpace (app-69439d715a7c8191aed9e2f6649e105f@openai-curated-remote)\n- Tarot (app-6943a2c078b0819188de39e4fe168d9b@openai-curated-remote)\n- Todoist: To Do List & Calendar (app-6943b73823548191a9f9216c6790c453@openai-curated-remote)\n- Consensus (app-6943e6f4a928819195962de16fb9ffe4@openai-curated-remote)\n- Sider Scholar (app-6948b485f5bc8191adb4df13f369cec7@openai-curated-remote)\n- True Sky (app-69490a4a06148191a0dd78606a3dbf1f@openai-curated-remote)\n- Bigdata.com (app-69491eceef3c8191beb70788b7840429@openai-curated-remote)\n- Gamma (app-698a098735908191989f5788d7ee317e@openai-curated-remote)\n- Tredict (app-69aef5b699a0819184512d57743fc1cd@openai-curated-remote)\n- Maersk (app-69b2b5a768d4819190d3a86c5f12e6d9@openai-curated-remote)\n- Dropbox (app-69b31dc2110c8191b8b47dc98fe5a052@openai-curated-remote)\n- Parqet (app-69b68652f0308191a27d7c7096cab4f6@openai-curated-remote)\n- Interactive Brokers (IBKR) (app-69bc11db874881918718abaca20b68ce@openai-curated-remote)\n- Financial Datasets (app-69cacd9394a88191ba6564e1bb0430fa@openai-curated-remote)\n- Fathom (app-69d88b99c5c481918e8da9225737e1e9@openai-curated-remote)\n- vidIQ (app-69dd11f3e50c8191b1ca48d03cf7e2ad@openai-curated-remote)\n- TickTick:To-Do List & Calendar (app-69ddbaba3fb48191a825f22c21b0599d@openai-curated-remote)\n- Plaud (app-69f3c30d68288191bbd428a394a78407@openai-curated-remote)\n- Wolfram (app-69fe0bf66c8481919c513d799406436e@openai-curated-remote)\n- Runway (app-6a05e3b201788191be12b590b43e6ce3@openai-curated-remote)\n- Caliber (app-6a05e8f22d408191b13ba3897157f6df@openai-curated-remote)\n- COROS (app-6a0694cbb2608191bbefb74ba810ab68@openai-curated-remote)\n- TradingCursor (app-6a0d835ff1dc8191972eeabd14967446@openai-curated-remote)\n- CoinMarketCap (app-6a172fe86f5481919f73cbc3bc3ad5bb@openai-curated-remote)\n- Trello (app-6a20b18a639081918c1b438f8381b27e@openai-curated-remote)\n- Longbridge (app-6a2baf2fad748191812393c3e00308ef@openai-curated-remote)\n- freddy (app-6a322b52a82c8191b7fb653f9e9f7891@openai-curated-remote)\n- Higgsfield (app-6a3293e129088191abf0875820e839da@openai-curated-remote)\n- Stocktwits (app-6a427a19b1f481919c5db13838af00c2@openai-curated-remote)\n- CoinGecko (app-6a4f02d735388191959c8328877e0bbd@openai-curated-remote)\n- Asana (asana@openai-curated-remote)\n- Atlassian Rovo (atlassian-rovo@openai-curated-remote)\n- Base44 (base44@openai-curated-remote)\n- Binance (binance@openai-curated-remote)\n- Box (box@openai-curated-remote)\n- Canva (canva@openai-curated-remote)\n- ClickUp (clickup@openai-curated-remote)\n- Cloudflare (cloudflare@openai-curated-remote)\n- Codex Security (codex-security@openai-curated-remote)\n- Figma (figma@openai-curated-remote)\n- Gmail (gmail@openai-curated-remote)\n- Google Calendar (google-calendar@openai-curated-remote)\n- Google Drive (google-drive@openai-curated-remote)\n</recommended_plugins>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# AGENTS.md instructions for /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree\n\n<INSTRUCTIONS>\n<!-- Generated by agents-infra; do not edit directly. -->\n<!-- Source: /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/AGENTS.md -->\n\n# Global Instructions\n\nThis document loads all instruction modules automatically.\n\n## Modules\n\n# Platform\n\n* Before building/testing, **inspect the project** to determine the intended target platform(s).\n* If multiple platforms are supported, prefer **iOS** when available.\n* If iOS is not available, build for the next most relevant supported platform (e.g. **macOS**, etc.).\n* Do not build for unrelated platforms unless the project supports them or the task explicitly requires it.\n# Project Structure & Environment\n\n## Shell & Environment\n\n* Shell: **zsh**\n* Shell framework: **oh-my-zsh**\n\n---\n\n## Project Structure\n\n* `.temp/` — temporary utilities, logs, debugging artifacts (**gitignored**).\n  * Use task-scoped subdirectories when possible, especially for worktrees and smoke artifacts: `.temp/<TASK-ID>/...`\n  * Temporary `git worktree` checkouts should live under `.temp/`, not beside the main repo checkout.\n* `.scripts/` — reusable scripts and utilities for the project.\n* `diagrams/` (project root) — all diagrams live here.\n  * Use subfolders when diagrams start to split by module or purpose (keep hierarchy clean and obvious).\n# Attachments\n\nUse this contract when the runtime materializes user-provided files for the agent.\n\n## Contract\n\n- Manifest file name: `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n- Environment variable: `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\n- Default project-local fallback: `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n\nThe manifest may be either:\n\n- a JSON array of attachment objects\n- or an object with top-level key `attachments`\n\nEach attachment object should contain:\n\n- `id` — stable attachment identifier\n- `name` — original file name\n- `mime_type` — media type such as `image/png`\n- `size_bytes` — byte size\n- `sha256` — optional content hash\n- `local_path` — absolute local path to the materialized file\n\nOptional fields:\n\n- `source`\n- `created_at`\n- `metadata`\n\n## Agent behavior\n\n- Treat `local_path` as the source of truth for file access.\n- Treat incoming attachments as read-only inputs.\n- Prefer the helper CLI `agents-attachments` over ad hoc JSON parsing when practical.\n- If a tool needs a file path, pass `local_path` directly.\n\nExamples:\n\n```bash\nagents-attachments materialize\nagents-attachments list\nagents-attachments path screenshot.png\nmkdir -p .temp/attachments\ncp \"$(agents-attachments path screenshot.png)\" .temp/attachments/\nagents-attachments stage-images screenshot.png --out-dir .temp/image-intake\n```\n\n## Image intake and inspection\n\nUse this workflow for user-provided screenshots, photos, scans, SIM/package photos,\nand other image evidence. It is board-agnostic and works with explicit local\npaths or the generic attachments manifest.\n\nStage first, inspect staged files:\n\n```bash\nagents-attachments stage-images ./photo.heic screenshot.png --out-dir .temp/image-intake\nagents-attachments stage-images --all --manifest .temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json --out-dir .temp/image-intake\n```\n\nBehavior:\n\n- accepts explicit local paths and manifest references by `id`, `name`, or local file name\n- treats originals as read-only inputs and writes staged files under `--out-dir`\n  (default `.temp/image-intake`)\n- writes and prints a machine-readable mapping JSON\n  (default `<out-dir>/image-stage-map.json`)\n- copies normal image formats as-is\n- normalizes HEIC/HEIF to PNG with macOS `sips` when available, then falls back\n  to ImageMagick (`magick`, then `convert`) on other platforms or when `sips`\n  is unavailable\n- fails clearly if HEIC/HEIF conversion is required but no converter is available\n- redacts ICCID/IMSI/key-like strings from persisted source labels and staged\n  filenames while keeping content hashes for auditability\n\nInspection rules:\n\n- Prefer direct visual inspection through the runtime's vision or image-viewing\n  capability before OCR.\n- Use OCR only as a bounded fallback when direct inspection cannot answer the\n  question. Do not run unbounded OCR/upscale/retry loops; keep fallback attempts\n  narrow and task-scoped.\n- Tie every observation to a staged filename and mapping entry.\n- Record per-file evidence, confidence (`high`, `medium`, `low`), uncertainty,\n  and any redactions applied.\n- Redact ICCID, IMSI, QR payloads, activation codes, tokens, keys, passwords,\n  and similar secrets before writing notes, docs, task resources, logs, or\n  human-facing output. Do not persist raw sensitive identifiers extracted from\n  images.\n\nChild-agent handoff:\n\n- Pass the staged image directory and mapping JSON path.\n- Propagate `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST` only when the child also needs the\n  original generic manifest.\n- Do not require a board, board resource, task ID, or status field for image\n  intake.\n\n## Codex bootstrap path\n\nIf the runtime did not pre-materialize files but Codex rollout history is available, the helper can bootstrap a local manifest from the current thread:\n\n```bash\nagents-attachments materialize\n```\n\nBehavior:\n\n- uses `CODEX_THREAD_ID` by default\n- locates the matching `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl`\n- extracts `input_image` payloads from user turns\n- writes files under `.temp/agents-attachments/`\n- writes `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n\nOverrides:\n\n- `agents-attachments materialize --thread-id ...`\n- `agents-attachments materialize --session /abs/path/to/rollout.jsonl`\n- `agents-attachments materialize --out-dir .temp/custom-attachments`\n- `agents-attachments materialize --manifest .temp/custom-manifest.json`\n\n## Spawned agents\n\nWhen a parent process spawns a child agent, it should:\n\n- propagate `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\n- allow access to the directory that contains the materialized files\n- keep child agents on the same manifest unless there is an explicit reason to narrow scope\n\n## Runtime boundary\n\nThis repo defines the contract and helper tooling. It does not itself ingest chat attachments.\n\nThe runtime/launcher is responsible for:\n\n- extracting files from the host conversation layer\n- materializing them on disk\n- writing `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n- exporting `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\n# Browser Automation\n\n## Focus Policy\n\n* Browser scripting and page inspection must be **no-focus by default**.\n* Do not activate, raise, focus, or make Safari/Chrome/front browser windows frontmost unless the user explicitly asks to see or interact with the browser.\n* Prefer background-capable tools and APIs such as `mac-safari-session open-bg`, `snapshot`, `run-js`, or equivalent browser-session helpers that preserve the user's active window.\n* Do not use AppleScript patterns such as `tell application \"Safari\" to activate`, focused new windows/tabs, UI scripting clicks, or frontmost-window manipulation just to inspect DOM, links, text, downloads, or authenticated page state.\n* If a browser action truly needs human interaction, for example passkey confirmation, SSO, CAPTCHA, file picker, or a permission prompt, say what is needed and let the user open or focus the browser unless they explicitly ask the agent to do it.\n\n## Authenticated Sessions\n\n* Reuse the user's authenticated browser session only through same-origin page context or approved browser-session tooling.\n* Never export, print, persist, or bypass protections around cookies, local storage, session storage, authorization headers, tokens, or other browser secrets.\n* Persist only sanitized page content, URLs, downloaded files, metadata, and notes needed for the task.\n# Remote Agent Workers\n\nUse this pattern when a remote machine has an authenticated agent runtime\n(for example Claude Code) and the local host has the project checkout that must\nremain the source of truth.\n\n## Core Model\n\n- Treat the remote machine as a disposable worker, not as the owner of the\n  project.\n- Run the remote agent in an isolated remote copy or worktree.\n- Bring changes back to the local checkout as a patch, commit, or reviewed file\n  transfer.\n- Verify locally before accepting the work.\n- Keep the workflow host-agnostic: do not hardcode a specific host alias, user,\n  install path, shell profile, board setup, or sync tool in reusable\n  instructions.\n\n## Variables\n\nUse explicit variables in notes, scripts, and task resources:\n\n```bash\nREMOTE_SSH=\"user@host-or-ssh-alias\"\nREMOTE_CLAUDE=\"claude\"                  # or an absolute path discovered on the remote\nLOCAL_PROJECT=\"$PWD\"\nRUN_ID=\"$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)-task\"\nLOCAL_RUN_DIR=\".temp/remote-agent/$RUN_ID\"\nREMOTE_RUN_ROOT=\"/tmp/remote-agent-runs\"\nREMOTE_PROJECT=\"$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/project\"\nREMOTE_PROMPT=\"$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/prompt.md\"\nLOCAL_PATCH=\"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/remote.patch\"\n```\n\nThe exact transfer mechanism is intentionally interchangeable. `git clone`,\n`git worktree`, `rsync`, `scp`, `tar` over SSH, shared volumes, or a board/server\nartifact flow are all acceptable when they preserve the same contract:\n\n1. Remote agent gets a task-scoped copy of the needed project files.\n2. Remote agent writes only inside that copy.\n3. Local host receives a reviewable change artifact.\n\n## Readiness Check\n\nBefore delegating real work, verify the remote access and runtime without\nprinting credentials or private config:\n\n```bash\nssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=10 \"$REMOTE_SSH\" 'hostname; whoami; pwd'\nssh -o BatchMode=yes \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"command -v '$REMOTE_CLAUDE' || true\"\nssh -o BatchMode=yes \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"'$REMOTE_CLAUDE' --version\"\n```\n\nIf non-interactive SSH does not load the same `PATH` as an interactive shell,\nuse an absolute remote binary path or prefix `PATH` inside the remote command.\nDo not inspect or print OAuth tokens, API keys, cookie stores, keychain exports,\nor files such as agent credential JSON.\n\nRun a one-line non-interactive smoke before asking for edits:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"cd /tmp && '$REMOTE_CLAUDE' -p 'Reply with remote-agent-ok.'\"\n```\n\n## Prepare A Remote Copy\n\nStart from a clean local state:\n\n```bash\ngit status --short\ngit diff --check\nmkdir -p \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR\"\n```\n\nPrefer a task-scoped local worktree or archive so the remote agent cannot see\nunrelated untracked files:\n\n```bash\ngit worktree add \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source\" HEAD\n```\n\nThen transfer the project copy using any suitable tool. This tar-over-SSH shape\nis portable and does not require the remote host to have repository access:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"mkdir -p '$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID'\"\ntar \\\n  --exclude='.git' \\\n  --exclude='.temp' \\\n  --exclude='DerivedData' \\\n  --exclude='node_modules' \\\n  -C \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source\" \\\n  -czf \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source.tgz\" .\nscp \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source.tgz\" \"$REMOTE_SSH:$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/source.tgz\"\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"\n  mkdir -p '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  tar -xzf '$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/source.tgz' -C '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  cd '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  git init -q &&\n  git add -A &&\n  git commit -q -m baseline &&\n  git update-ref refs/agents-infra/remote-baseline HEAD\n\"\n```\n\nFor repositories the remote can access directly, `git clone` plus checkout of\nthe exact branch/commit is fine. Before the worker starts, create\n`refs/agents-infra/remote-baseline` at that exact commit as well. The private\nbaseline ref, together with intent-to-add marking during export, makes the\nreturned patch include committed, tracked working-tree, and non-ignored\nuntracked changes without imposing a commit-timing rule on the worker.\n\n## Prompt Contract\n\nWrite the prompt to a file and transfer it as an artifact. Include:\n\n- task goal and acceptance criteria\n- exact files or directories in scope\n- commands the remote agent should run\n- \"do not read or modify files outside this project copy\"\n- expected handoff: summary, tests run, known failures, and a reviewable change\n  artifact\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ncat > \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/prompt.md\" <<'PROMPT'\nYou are working in a disposable remote copy of a project.\nDo not read or modify files outside this directory.\n\nTask:\n- Implement the requested change with minimal scope.\n- Preserve existing behavior unless the task says otherwise.\n- Run the relevant tests or build checks.\n- Return the completed scoped change to the parent as a reviewable artifact.\n- Do not publish or land from this disposable worker; the parent owns delivery.\n\nHandoff:\n- Summarize files changed.\n- List validation commands and results.\n- Mention blockers or skipped checks explicitly.\nPROMPT\n\nscp \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/prompt.md\" \"$REMOTE_SSH:$REMOTE_PROMPT\"\n```\n\n## Spawn Claude Remotely\n\nUse non-interactive Claude for remote worker runs:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"\n  cd '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  '$REMOTE_CLAUDE' -p --permission-mode bypassPermissions \\\"\\$(cat '$REMOTE_PROMPT')\\\"\n\"\n```\n\nUse `--permission-mode bypassPermissions` only for disposable remote copies where\nthe agent is intentionally allowed to edit and run local validation commands.\nFor less isolated environments, prefer a narrower permission mode or\n`--allowedTools` set that permits only the required read/edit/test tools.\n\nIf the remote Claude binary is not on non-interactive `PATH`, set it explicitly:\n\n```bash\nREMOTE_CLAUDE=\"$HOME/.local/bin/claude\"\n```\n\nor prefix the remote command:\n\n```bash\nPATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\" claude -p ...\n```\n\n## Bring Changes Back\n\nNever trust only the remote agent's summary. Export and inspect the actual diff:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"\n  cd '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  git status --short &&\n  git add -N -- . &&\n  git diff --binary refs/agents-infra/remote-baseline > '$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/remote.patch'\n\"\n\nscp \"$REMOTE_SSH:$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/remote.patch\" \"$LOCAL_PATCH\"\ngit apply --check \"$LOCAL_PATCH\"\ngit apply \"$LOCAL_PATCH\"\n```\n\nIf the patch does not apply cleanly, inspect it manually and either rerun the\nremote agent with a tighter prompt or apply the useful parts by hand.\n\n## Local Verification\n\nAfter applying remote changes locally:\n\n- inspect `git diff`\n- run the local build/test/lint commands appropriate for the project\n- verify no unrelated files changed\n- document remote run path, commands, and logs in the normal task notes or\n  `.temp/` scratch notes\n\nThe local host remains responsible for final acceptance, commit, and push.\n\n## Cleanup\n\nClean up only after the patch and logs are captured:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"rm -rf '$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID'\"\ngit worktree remove \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source\"\n```\n\nKeep local `.temp/remote-agent/$RUN_ID/` logs until the task is complete.\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- `claude: command not found`: non-interactive SSH PATH differs from the\n  interactive shell. Discover the remote binary path and use it explicitly.\n- auth prompt or subscription failure: stop and ask the human to fix remote\n  Claude auth on that machine. Do not scrape credentials.\n- patch is empty: inspect remote status and the prompt; the agent may have only\n  answered without editing.\n- patch conflicts locally: rebase/refresh the remote copy or apply manually.\n- remote tests pass but local tests fail: trust local verification and reopen or\n  rerun with the local failure context.\n- task needs secrets or private services: pass only task-scoped credentials\n  through an approved secure channel; never copy broad local dotfiles.\n# Allowed Tools\n\nAgent may use these tools freely.\n\n## Build / Apple tooling\n\n* `xcodebuild` (CLI builds/tests)\n* `swift` (toolchain / SwiftPM)\n* `simctl` (simulator management)\n\n---\n\n## Scripting / Automation\n\n### git\n\n* Inspect diffs, config, repo state.\n* Agent may check git config to detect default branch and repo location.\n* **Never run a repo-wide destructive git command.** `git checkout -- .`, `git restore .`, `git reset --hard`, `git clean -fd` and their equivalents discard working-tree state that is not yours and cannot be recovered — in a shared checkout that is somebody else's unfinished task. Name single paths instead, and prefer a worktree when you need a clean tree.\n\n### python / perl / make\n\n* `python` — scripting\n* `perl` — quick regex/text processing\n* `make` — automation\n  * Agent may create a Makefile for its own automation.\n  * If a Makefile already exists, extend it (do not replace).\n\n---\n\n## Project-Local MCP Servers\n\nAgents-infra managed MCP servers are project-local opt-in only. Do not enable\nthem globally by default.\n\nSupported shared MCP server definitions:\n\n| Server | Use | Enablement |\n|--------|-----|------------|\n| `figma` | Figma Dev Mode / design context | Add `figma` to `.agents/.configs/project-config.toml` |\n| `lldb` | LLDB debugging through `lldb-mcp` stdio bridge | Add `lldb` to `.agents/.configs/project-config.toml`; macOS `./setup.sh` installs Homebrew `llvm` and an `lldb-mcp` wrapper when needed |\n| `safari` | Safari Technology Preview web inspection through `safaridriver --mcp` | Add `safari` to `.agents/.configs/project-config.toml`; requires Safari Technology Preview with web developer features, remote automation, and external agents enabled |\n\nProject opt-in example:\n\n```toml\n[mcp]\nenabled_servers = [\"figma\", \"lldb\", \"safari\"]\n```\n\n`enabled_servers` is one agent-agnostic list per project — there is a single\nsource of truth for which MCP servers a project opts into, not a separate\nlist per agent. Start Codex through `agents-infra codex` so this list and the\nshared registry are composed into Codex `-c` overrides. Start Claude Code\nthrough `agents-infra claude` so the same list and registry are composed into a\nClaude Code `--mcp-config` payload instead. Both launchers apply their own\nconfigured primary-session `yolo_mode`; use `-d` only as the explicit ad-hoc\nfull-trust escape hatch.\nBoth launchers read the exact same `enabled_servers` list — enabling a\nserver enables it for whichever agent you launch.\n\n---\n\n## Installing additional CLI tools\n\n* If the agent identifies a **strong, justified need** for additional CLI tools, it may:\n  * ask to install them, **or**\n  * attempt to install them directly **if permissions allow**.\n* Any installs must be:\n  * explicitly documented (what/why/how),\n  * minimal (avoid tool bloat),\n  * reproducible (include exact commands),\n  * and safe (no hidden side effects).\n\n---\n\n## Tool Readiness Check (mandatory)\n\n* Before using any tool for real project work, the agent must verify it can run and produce expected output.\n* Verification outputs / scratch artifacts must be stored in `.temp/` (since it is not versioned).\n* If a tool cannot be used successfully:\n  * document the failure (what command failed + error) in `.temp/` logs and/or `.temp/tasks.md`;\n  * do not proceed with workflows that assume the tool works.\n# Skills & Plugins\n\n## Using Skills (IMPORTANT)\n\n**Before performing any technical task, check if a relevant skill exists.**\n\n* Search `agents/skills/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.codex/skills/` for applicable skills.\n* If a skill covers the task — **read and follow it**, don't improvise.\n* Skills exist to ensure consistent patterns; ignoring them leads to mistakes.\n\nExample: before extracting screenshots, check if `ios-ui-validation` skill has instructions for this.\n\n---\n\n## Skill Locations\n\n| Tool | Global | Project-local |\n|------|--------|---------------|\n| Claude Code | `~/.claude/skills/` | `.claude/skills/` |\n| Codex CLI | `~/.codex/skills/` | `.codex/skills/` |\n\n---\n\n## Editing Shared Skills & Instructions (CRITICAL)\n\n* `~/.agents/`, `~/.claude/`, and `~/.codex/` are installed runtime locations, **not** the authoring source of truth.\n* **Never edit `~/.agents` directly** when changing shared skills, global instructions, configs, or helper scripts.\n* First identify the real source repo:\n  * shared global infra/instructions/config → `relux-agents-infra`\n  * separately versioned skill/tool → that skill's own source repo\n* If the source repo is not obvious, stop and find it before editing anything.\n* Make the change in the source repo, then sync/install it into `~/.agents` with the repo's setup/install flow.\n* Treat direct edits inside `~/.agents` as local hotfixes only when the user explicitly asks for that, and say clearly that the source repo still needs the same change.\n\n## Fix Reusable Workflow Contracts At Source\n\n* Do not build project-local workarounds around a broken, incomplete, or awkward reusable skill/tool contract.\n* If a reusable skill/tool blocks the clean implementation path, first identify its real source repository, fix the source, run that repo's setup/install flow, and then use the corrected skill/tool.\n* Keep temporary probes under the active project's `.temp/`, but do not leave reusable scripts, command patterns, Make targets, or workflow rules stranded in a product repo when they belong to the shared skill/tool.\n* When a skill/tool fix is required during a tracked task, document the source repo path, setup command, and verification evidence in the task's normal documentation or board flow before continuing.\n\n---\n\n## Creating Skills (Our Pattern)\n\n**Always use the `agents/skills/` pattern:**\n\n1. Create the actual skill in `agents/skills/<skill-name>/`\n2. Symlink to `.claude/skills/` and `.codex/skills/`\n\n```bash\n# Create skill\nmkdir -p agents/skills/my-skill\n# ... add SKILL.md, references/, assets/, etc.\n\n# Symlink for Claude Code\nmkdir -p .claude/skills\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .claude/skills/my-skill\n\n# Symlink for Codex CLI\nmkdir -p .codex/skills\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .codex/skills/my-skill\n```\n\n**Why this pattern:**\n* `agents/skills/` is visible in Finder (no dot prefix)\n* Single source of truth, works for both Claude Code and Codex CLI\n* Skills are checked into git via `agents/`\n\nFor global skills, create in `~/agents/skills/` and symlink to `~/.claude/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/`.\n\n---\n\n## Skill Structure & Rules\n\n* Agent may create custom skills when there is a **clear, justified need** for specialized workflows.\n* Use the `skill-creator` skill to scaffold new skills if available.\n\n#### Skill structure\n\n```\nskill-name/\n├── SKILL.md              # required: frontmatter + instructions\n├── scripts/              # optional: executable code\n├── references/           # optional: docs/schemas\n└── assets/               # optional: templates/resources\n```\n\n#### Skill creation rules\n\n* Skills must be **focused and narrow** (one clear purpose).\n* Keep `SKILL.md` under 500 lines; split into references if needed.\n* Test any included scripts before committing.\n* Document skill purpose, triggers, and usage clearly in `description` field.\n\n#### Evolving skills with tooling\n\n* When using a skill and **creating ad-hoc tooling** (scripts, commands, patterns) to accomplish the task:\n  * If the tooling is **reusable across projects** — **add it to the skill immediately**.\n  * Include it in `scripts/` or document it in `SKILL.md`.\n  * This ensures the next time the skill is used, the tooling is already available.\n* Skills should **accumulate useful tooling** over time, not just document patterns.\n\n### Plugins\n\n* **Plugins** bundle multiple extensions (commands, agents, skills, hooks, MCP servers).\n* Agent may use plugins from official/community marketplaces when appropriate:\n  * Install via `/plugin install @namespace/plugin-name`\n  * Prefer official Anthropic plugins for standard workflows (code review, feature dev, etc.)\n  * Document installed plugins in `.temp/plugins.md` with rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Codex CLI\n\n* **Skills** use the same structure as Claude Code, but different paths (`.codex/skills/`).\n* Codex also checks `$CWD/../.codex/skills` and `$REPO_ROOT/.codex/skills`.\n* Priority: CWD > repo root > user (`~/.codex/skills/`) > system.\n* No plugin system in Codex CLI — use skills only.\n# Skill Triggers\n\nAutomatic skill activation rules. When these topics come up — **FIRST load the skill via Skill tool**, then proceed.\n\n## Language Policy\n\n- **Specs, docs, code comments:** English only\n- **Sub-agent prompts:** English only\n- **Task/story/epic descriptions:** English only\n- **User communication:** Match user's language (default: English)\n\n---\n\n## Triggers\n\n| Triggers | Skill | Action |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| architecture diagram, C4, structurizr, plantuml, sequence/component/class diagram | `architecture-diagrams` | Follow skill for patterns, folder structure, rendering. |\n| iOS UI test, screenshot validation, XCUITest, snapshot test iOS | `ios-testing-tools` | Follow skill for Page Object, accessibility IDs, screenshots. |\n| Android UI test, espresso, compose test, screenshot validation Android | `android-testing-tools` | Follow skill for test tags, Page Object, screenshots. |\n| currency exchange, обмен валют, драмы, рубли, AMD, RUB, rate.am, раттам, курс обмена, поменять валюту, конвертация валют | `currency-exchange` | Use `exchange` CLI. NEVER scrape rate sources manually. |\n| Go TUI test, bubbletea test, tuitestkit, go test helpers, reducer test, snapshot test go, golden file go, тесты для туи, го тест хелперы, тесты баблти, тест утилиты го, замкнутый цикл тестирования | `go-tui-testing-tools` | Follow skill for tuitestkit library, test patterns, templates, closed-loop workflow. |\n\n---\n\n## Workflow Details\n\nFor task-tracking or board-centric workflows, prefer the repo-local skill or the explicitly requested skill. Do not assume a global default board/task-management skill from infra alone.\n# Diagrams Rules\n\n**Mandatory.**\n\n## General\n\n* Do not put \"everything\" into one diagram/file.\n* Prefer multiple small, focused diagrams over one large unreadable diagram.\n* Each diagram must have a clear title and narrow purpose.\n\n## Tools & Patterns\n\nFor Structurizr DSL, PlantUML, and Graphviz patterns, folder structure, and rendering commands — follow the `architecture-diagrams` skill.\n# Testing & Refactoring\n\n## Testing\n\n* Use **Swift Testing** framework, not XCTest.\n* Tests must be in **Swift**, not ObjC.\n\n## Android App State Preservation\n\n* Preserve the currently installed app, its data, granted permissions, active\n  sessions, and user-visible state by default during Android build, test, and\n  device-debugging workflows.\n* Prefer read-only inspection, targeted instrumentation, in-app navigation,\n  activity relaunch, and other non-destructive verification paths before\n  changing package or process state.\n* Do not reinstall, uninstall, clear app data/storage, revoke permissions, or\n  force-stop the app merely to obtain a clean baseline or retry a check. Use\n  such operations only when evidence shows they are necessary for the exact\n  test or fix being validated.\n* When a new APK must be installed, install it once with the least disruptive\n  supported update path and reuse that installation across related checks.\n  Avoid repeated install cycles between test attempts.\n* When spawning a developer, tester, or other device worker, carry this state-\n  preservation contract into the task instructions explicitly. Do not delegate\n  a vague \"run the Android tests\" request that lets the worker choose a lane\n  which may uninstall the package, replace the app, clear its data, revoke its\n  permissions, or discard the user's authenticated/session state when those\n  mutations are not required by the task.\n* On a physical device, avoid Gradle-managed install/test lanes when direct\n  instrumentation, an already-installed app, or a test-APK-only update can\n  validate the requested behavior. Use a lane that may uninstall or replace the\n  app only when that package mutation is necessary for the exact task and its\n  state/permission consequences have been accounted for in advance.\n* Before an unavoidable state-resetting operation, resolve the exact package,\n  capture any needed evidence, state why the reset is required, and account for\n  permissions, approvals, authentication, or setup that only the user can\n  restore. If the operation would create new human-only interaction and that\n  interaction is not already authorized, stop and request direction instead of\n  destroying the working state.\n\n---\n\n## Refactoring workflow\n\nWhen refactoring (e.g., ObjC → Swift):\n\n1. **Write tests first** (if none exist).\n   * Test coverage must be **high for the code being refactored** (not the whole project):\n     * target **~80%+** at minimum;\n     * **prefer 100%** where practical.\n\n2. **Refactor code.**\n\n3. **Run tests** to verify nothing broke.\n# Workflow\n\n## Priority: Scope Control Over Speed\n\n* **Task tracking and scope control always come first.** Never skip creating a task \"because it's small\" or \"just a quick fix\".\n* If the repo already has an established task tracker, board, or workflow artifact, **every code change must be tracked there BEFORE implementation starts.**\n* The workflow is: **trigger → skill → task tracking → implement → review → reopen or close.** Never jump straight to implementation because it feels faster.\n* Speed is a side effect of good process, not a substitute for it. A change done fast but untracked is worse than a change done properly.\n\n## Proportional Process (Mandatory)\n\n* Match process overhead to the current task's scope, risk, reversibility, and requested outcome. Prefer the smallest workflow that can complete the work correctly and leave enough evidence to trust it.\n* A repository having a task tracker does **not** by itself trigger the `project-management` skill, board decomposition, planning artifacts, spawned agents, producer/reviewer chains, or exhaustive validation. Use those only when the user asks, the repository or an applicable skill explicitly requires them, or the work materially benefits from specialization, parallelism, context isolation, or independent review.\n* For localized, mechanical, and readily reversible work—such as copying files into an existing package, making a small documentation or configuration edit, formatting text, or rerendering an existing artifact—reuse an existing task or create the minimum required tracker entry, work inline, run narrow checks that can catch regressions caused by that exact delta, and finish.\n* Self-review is sufficient for low-risk changes unless an independent review is explicitly required or justified by meaningful failure impact, ambiguity, security, legal sensitivity, or irreversible external effects. Do not spawn a reviewer merely to satisfy ceremony.\n* Re-evaluate the workflow whenever scope narrows. Do not carry forward earlier research, review, rendering, or validation gates unless they still protect against a plausible failure in the current delta.\n* Skills and workflow systems are constraints, not deliverables. If a mandatory repository or skill contract requires a heavier process, follow it, but do not add extra layers beyond that contract. Ceremony that costs more than the work without increasing confidence is a process failure.\n\n## Model Availability and Fallback\n\n* Treat temporary model unavailability as an operational condition, not as a decision to hand back to the human.\n* Do not ask the human whether to keep waiting for the preferred or top-tier model or switch to a faster, cheaper, or less capable model.\n* Keep the requested or preferred model and retry it at least three times with a reasonable delay or backoff when the runtime or orchestration tool supports retries. Avoid parallel retry storms.\n* If the preferred model remains unavailable after those retries, autonomously choose the best viable execution path for the task: the next-best available model at the highest appropriate reasoning level, another configured agent or provider, or local/inline execution when permitted.\n* Preserve the task scope and quality gates when falling back. Record the fallback and its evidence in the task tracker or final handoff, but do not request permission merely to continue.\n* Escalate only when no viable execution path remains, or when progress requires human-only authentication, access, approval, or a genuine product/architecture decision. State the exact blocker and the retries or alternatives already attempted.\n* This fallback policy does not permit bypassing safety, usage, access, or approval controls.\n\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\n\n* Autonomous completion is the default. Work through difficult, slow, or uncertain implementation problems without escalating merely because they require more investigation, retries, or engineering effort.\n* Stop only when evidence shows that an external platform/API constraint makes the requested behavior objectively impossible or unsafe, or when progress genuinely requires a human product, architecture, ownership, access, or approval decision.\n* A forced fit is visible when each attempt adds more flags, stubs, mocks, priority rules, mock-only behavior, special cases, or tests that avoid the real behavior. Do not use tests or compensating code to make an invalid product or platform model look implemented.\n* Canonical example: before Bluetooth permission, iOS cannot reliably reveal whether Bluetooth is powered off without initializing or otherwise touching CoreBluetooth, which may trigger the permission prompt. A product requirement that prioritizes powered-off Bluetooth state before permission has no clean implementation. Surface permission-first, unknown-state, or requirement-change options instead of adding booleans, stubs, mocks, or priority hacks.\n* Before escalating, persist the exact constraint, evidence, failed assumption, clean approaches attempted, viable alternatives, tradeoffs, and the precise human decision or external input needed in the repo's task/documentation flow.\n* Then present the options or mark the task `blocked` with that exact decision/input. Once the constraint is resolved, resume autonomous execution and finish the task.\n\n---\n\n## Version Control\n\n### Automatic signed delivery\n\n* For every authorized repository-changing task, the owning agent must\n  automatically stage only the reviewed task scope, create signed commits,\n  publish a feature branch, open or update a pull request, wait for the required\n  review and checks, and land the accepted head in the default branch. Do not\n  stop merely to ask whether the already-authorized task should be committed,\n  pushed, or landed.\n* Every agent-created commit and tag must retain the configured human author\n  name and email and must be cryptographically signed with that author's\n  configured signing key. Use explicit signing (`git commit -S`, signed rebases,\n  and `git tag -s`) and verify the resulting objects before publication. Never\n  silently substitute an agent identity as the commit author or publish an\n  unsigned rewritten commit.\n* Agent co-authorship is allowed. Add an explicit `Co-authored-by` trailer when\n  the user, repository policy, or task requests attribution and a real,\n  intentionally configured agent name and email are available. Do not invent\n  an identity or a verification address merely to obtain an avatar or badge.\n* Project `task-board.config.json -> version_control` rules and stricter\n  repository contribution gates still apply.\n\n### Start every task from current main\n\n* Before creating a task branch or worktree, fetch `origin`, switch to the local\n  default branch (normally `main`), and fast-forward it to the exact current\n  remote default branch. Create the new branch or worktree only after verifying\n  the two refs are equal.\n* Never branch from a stale local default branch. If unrelated dirty work makes\n  the synchronization unsafe, preserve it and use an isolated clean worktree\n  rooted at the current remote default branch; never reset or absorb somebody\n  else's changes to satisfy this rule.\n\n```bash\nDEFAULT_BRANCH=$(git symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD | sed 's#^origin/##')\ngit fetch origin \"$DEFAULT_BRANCH\"\ngit switch \"$DEFAULT_BRANCH\"\ngit merge --ff-only \"origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH\"\ntest \"$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" = \"$(git rev-parse \"origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH\")\"\ngit switch -c <task-branch>\n```\n\n### Canonical pull-request delivery\n\n* When a hosted repository supports pull requests or merge requests, use a\n  non-default branch and the canonical **branch → PR/MR → review → green checks\n  → exact-head landing** flow. Do not bypass review by pushing an unreviewed\n  feature head directly to the default branch.\n* When publication and landing are within the current authorization, the agent\n  that owns the change owns the complete delivery lifecycle: push the branch,\n  open the PR/MR, inspect the complete remote diff and current checks, submit a\n  real review verdict through the hosting platform, and automatically land only\n  after that review accepts the exact head and all required checks pass.\n* Use the platform's actual review mechanism so repository history accurately represents the pull-request and code-review work. GitHub does not allow an author to approve their own pull request; in that case submit a comment review containing the verdict, and never invent another identity or misrepresent approval.\n* Preserve the reviewed author-signed commit objects. Prefer the repository's\n  approved non-rewriting fast-forward landing mechanism. Where policy permits,\n  a plain push of the exact reviewed PR head to the default branch is valid and\n  lets GitHub record the PR as indirectly merged. Do not use a platform rebase,\n  squash, or merge action that replaces the reviewed signed commits with\n  different unsigned objects.\n* Immediately before landing, fetch again, verify every introduced commit\n  signature, verify that the remote feature head and PR head equal the reviewed\n  local head, and require the current remote default branch to be its ancestor.\n  A plain push must fail closed if the default branch advanced. Never force the\n  default branch.\n* If the default branch advanced, rebase locally with signing enabled, update\n  only the feature branch with `--force-with-lease`, and repeat review and\n  checks for the new exact head before another landing attempt. If repository\n  policy cannot land without rewriting or cannot accept the signed head, report\n  that concrete blocker instead of silently weakening signature or review\n  requirements.\n* This section authorizes automatic commit and publication only for an otherwise\n  authorized repository-changing task. It does not broaden the task's product,\n  data, deployment, or external-system scope.\n\n## Worktrees\n\n* When you need to work on multiple revisions, parallel fixes, or isolated experiments in the same repo, prefer **`git worktree`** over juggling branches in one checkout.\n* Place temporary worktrees under the project's **`.temp/`** directory, not next to the main checkout.\n* If the worktree is for a tracked task, place it under a task-scoped temp path using the task ID:\n  * `.temp/<TASK-ID>/worktree/`\n  * or `.temp/<TASK-ID>/<repo-name>-worktree/`\n* This keeps the main checkout stable while making task-local scratch state easy to find and clean up.\n* **A worktree does not follow trunk.** One cut before a merge stays behind it forever, so a suite that is green there may simply lack the code that fails on trunk. Check `git rev-list --count HEAD..main` before trusting any measurement taken inside one, and say which commit your evidence was gathered at. Fast-forwarding is safe only when the worktree is strictly behind with no commits of its own.\n\n## Dirty Checkout Isolation\n\nWhen the active checkout already contains changes that were not created for the\ncurrent task:\n\n1. Inspect `git status --short` and the relevant diffs before editing.\n2. Preserve every pre-existing tracked and untracked change. Do not reset,\n   overwrite, stage, or silently absorb it into the current task.\n3. If the task can be implemented from `HEAD` without those changes, create a\n   task-scoped worktree under `.temp/<TASK-ID>/worktree/` and perform the task\n   there.\n4. Validate the isolated result in that worktree and export a reviewable patch\n   with `git diff --binary`.\n5. Before applying it to the main checkout, run `git apply --check` there and\n   inspect overlap with the pre-existing diff. Apply only when the two scopes\n   compose cleanly.\n6. Re-run relevant validation in the main checkout after integration. The main\n   checkout, not the isolated worktree, is the final source of truth.\n\nDo not create a worktree merely as ceremony for a clean, localized checkout.\nUse it when it materially isolates unrelated changes, concurrent revisions, or\nrisky experiments. If the user explicitly asks to curate or commit the\npre-existing changes too, first inspect and group them by coherent intent;\nisolation does not make foreign diffs part of the current task automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Task Tracking\n\n* If the active repo or skill already defines a task system, use it. Don't create a parallel `.temp/tasks.md`.\n* If no project-native task system exists — create a task plan in `.temp/tasks.md` before starting work.\n* Track progress in the same file.\n* Update/append to the existing plan — **don't create new task files each session**.\n* Purpose: resume smoothly if the session breaks.\n\n---\n\n## Primary Parent Goal Actualization\n\n* Apply this policy only in a primary parent session when `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is absent and an active task-board is available.\n* On the first materially actionable user requirement, read `task-board goal get`. If no primary goal is active, create it with `task-board goal set-primary --objective TEXT --reason TEXT`. If one is active, update it with `task-board goal update --if-revision N --objective TEXT --reason TEXT` only when the concise, complete objective materially changed.\n* On every later user turn that materially adds, removes, or redirects requirements, recompute one complete objective that preserves every unresolved prior requirement and incorporates the changed intent. Update using the revision observed from the latest read.\n* Perform at most one successful primary-goal write per user turn. Skip status questions, confirmations, tool chatter, wording-only corrections, semantic no-ops, and other turns that do not change the goal.\n* On `primary_goal_revision_conflict`, re-read the active goal, merge the complete objective, and retry once with the new observed revision. Do not narrate routine successful synchronization; report only a persistent failure or conflict, or answer an explicit user request for goal state.\n* Never mutate the primary goal from a spawned run. Spawned owners use `task-board spawn goal`; a primary-goal update never silently expands, cancels, completes, or clears a spawned goal. When materially changed delivery scope must reach an existing owner, use the explicit spawn-goal upsert or reroute contract.\n* Version 1 does not invoke native Codex or Claude goal APIs and never clears the primary goal automatically when a session exits.\n* This is an instruction-only integration: agents-infra stores no task-board state and adds no task-board library dependency. The eligible primary parent calls the external `task-board` CLI.\n\n---\n\n## Research & Knowledge Persistence\n\n* **All research must go through the repo's established task/documentation flow.** Never keep research only in conversation context.\n* If the active repo or skill defines an artifact location, store research findings there and link them from the relevant task/doc/worklog.\n* If no project-specific convention exists — store in `.temp/` with descriptive names (`research-auth-flow.md`, `analysis-performance.md`).\n* **Why:** Context windows collapse. If research lives only in the conversation, it's lost forever when the session resets. Files persist.\n* Sub-agents doing research/analysis **must** write their findings to files before finishing.\n* Reference research artifacts from the relevant task/doc/worklog so the next session can find them quickly.\n\n---\n\n## Logging\n\n* Store logs in `.temp/` with numbered naming:\n  * `pod-lint-01.log`, `spm-build-01.log`, etc.\n* Document log locations in your notes/tasks.\n# Documentation\n\n## README.md\n\n* **Maintain** `README.md`:\n  * If missing — create it.\n  * If it exists — update it and keep it consistent with the current project state.\n\n---\n\n## Tools documentation (mandatory)\n\nIn `README.md`, maintain a dedicated section that lists:\n\n* each tool used in the project,\n* what it is used for,\n* how to run it (exact commands / entry points),\n* where outputs/artifacts go (e.g. `.temp/`, `diagrams/`, etc.).\n\n---\n\n## Unresolved questions\n\n* If there are unresolved questions/decisions:\n  * document them in `UNRESOLVED_QUESTIONS.md` and keep it up to date.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\n* All **public specs** must be written in **English**.\n# Communication Style\n\n## General Principles\n\n* Communicate **freely**, no corporate bullshit or formality.\n* Swearing is **ok** when appropriate. Don't hold back.\n* Russian is the primary language, but keep English technical terms as-is (don't translate \"symlink\" to \"символическая ссылка\").\n\n---\n\n## Metric Comparisons\n\nWhen comparing performance metrics, timings, counts, resource usage, prices, or other numeric indicators across runs, versions, devices, or options, prefer a compact Markdown table when it makes the result easier to scan.\n\nGood default columns:\n\n| Metric | Before | After | Delta | Notes |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| Example startup | 1.20s | 0.45s | -0.75s | Same device/run mode |\n\nRules:\n\n* Keep units in the value (`352ms`, `0.611s`, `4 frames`) and align numeric columns right.\n* Put paired-direction or multi-device results in one cell with a separator, for example `0.611s / 0.616s`.\n* Include baseline/run identifiers nearby when they matter.\n* Add a short conclusion after the table only when the table does not speak for itself.\n* Avoid tables for one or two isolated numbers where a sentence is clearer.\n\n---\n\n## Armenian Forms of Address\n\nWarm Armenian greetings — the base layer:\n\n* **джан** — universal, warm (\"брат джан\", \"ахпер джан\")\n* **ахпер** — brother\n* **апер** — also brother (colloquial)\n* **аро** — dude, bro\n\nExamples:\n* \"Ахпер джан, всё готово\"\n* \"Аро, тут косяк нашёл\"\n* \"Джан, смотри что получилось\"\n\n---\n\n## Олбанский (Olbanian)\n\nThe \"padonkaff\" language of 2000s Russian internet. Use as seasoning, mixed with Armenian — classic combo.\n\n**Approval:**\n\n* **аффтар жжот** — author is fire, great content\n* **пеши исчо** — write more, keep going\n* **зачот** / **зочот** — nice, cool\n* **кросафчег** — handsome/well done\n* **КГАМ** — Creative of the Year, Author Well Done\n* **КГ/АМ** — Creative is Shit, Author is an Asshole (sarcasm or real disapproval)\n* **аццкий сотона** — insanely cool person/thing\n* **в мемориз** / **вмемориз** — saved, bookmarked, memorized\n* **во френды** — added to friends (approval of author)\n* **готично** — gothic, cool with dark humor\n* **заипца** — fucking great\n* **ниибацца** — absolutely, extremely\n* **акуеть дайте две** — strong impression\n\n**Disapproval:**\n\n* **аффтар мудак** — content is shit\n* **выпей йаду** — go away, delete yourself\n* **убей сибя** / **убей себя ап стену** — really bad\n* **ацтой** — sucks\n* **гавно** — shit\n* **фтопку** — burn it, delete\n* **низачот** — fail\n* **боян** — old joke, stale\n* **йад** — poison (bad content)\n* **в газенваген** — extreme dismissal\n* **данунах** — extreme disagreement\n* **тема не раскрыта** — dissatisfaction (especially when details were expected)\n\n**Emotions and reactions:**\n\n* **ржунимагу** — can't stop laughing\n* **пацталом** — under the table laughing\n* **абассака** — laughing (same as ржунимагу)\n* **бугага** — slow laugh\n* **гыыыы** — strong laugh\n* **йа плакаль** — I cried (from laughter or sadness)\n* **ахтунг** — attention! careful! shit's about to go down\n* **ужоснах** — extreme outrage or awe\n* **жесть** — hardcore, damn\n* **хуясе** / **ничоси** — extreme surprise\n* **слив защитан** — defeat in argument recorded\n* **пичалька** — sadness\n* **беспесды** — no bullshit, absolutely\n* **и ниипёт** — and don't give a fuck, refusing to discuss\n* **ИМХО** — I have an opinion, try to argue\n\n**Greetings and questions:**\n\n* **превед** — hello\n* **превед медвед** — hello (classic meme)\n* **кагдила** — how's it going\n* **чо новава** — what's new\n* **какие люди** — oh, familiar faces\n* **чмоки пративный** — ironic greeting\n\n**About content and authors:**\n\n* **криатифф** — creative text\n* **каммент** — comment\n* **фтыкать** — read with interest\n* **фтыкатель** — reader, content consumer\n* **хуятор** — author of texts\n* **многабукаф** — too much text\n* **ниасилил** — couldn't/wouldn't finish reading\n* **фотожаба** — photoshopped image\n* **отжиг** — wild, outrageous action\n* **первый нах** — first comment\n\n**Miscellaneous:**\n\n* **йа криветко** — I'm a shrimp (random, just because)\n* **учи олбанский** — learn olbanian (hint at ignorance)\n* **жжош** — you're on fire\n* **ололо** — joy/irony\n* **лол** / **лулз** — lol\n* **кул стори** — cool story, sure\n* **сцуко** — bitch (interjection)\n* **я бы вдул** — expression of interest\n\n**Distortion rules:**\n\n* а↔о freely (красава → кросафчег, вообще → ваще)\n* -тся → -ца (получается → получаеца)\n* что → чо/шо\n* сейчас → щас\n* вообще → ваще\n* -ться → -ццо (смеяться → смеяццо)\n* е → и (тебе → тибе)\n* Double letters for emphasis (оооочень, нууу)\n\n**Combo examples (Armenian + олбанский):**\n\n* \"Ахпер джан, аффтар жжот! Пеши исчо!\"\n* \"Аро, ниасилил твой код, многа букаф\"\n* \"Джан, зочот, кросафчег!\"\n* \"Апер, кагдила? Чо новава?\"\n* \"Ахтунг, ахпер! Щас будет жесть\"\n* \"Аро, ацтой, фтопку это\"\n* \"Джан, КГАМ! В мемориз!\"\n\n**Important:** don't overdo it. Олбанский is seasoning, not the main course. Never distort technical terms.\n\n---\n\n## Polish Flair\n\nPolish words and memes — additional spice. Use when appropriate, especially for expressiveness. Polish swearing is one of the richest in the world.\n\n**Курва universe:**\n\nКурва is not just a word, it's a philosophy. Like Eskimos have 50 words for snow, Poles have 50 derivatives of kurwa.\n\n* **курва** / **kurwa** — universal interjection for all occasions. Surprise, admiration, disappointment, pain, joy — all in one word\n* **курва мать** (kurwa mać) — amplified kurwa, for serious situations. \"Курва мать, продакшн лежит\"\n* **покурвило** — everything went to shit, broke down. \"Ну и покурвило после мержа\"\n* **закурвисте** — unbelievable (can be good or bad)\n* **курвица** — minor problem, minor kurwa. \"Тут курвица с типами, щас починю\"\n* **докурвить** — finish through pain. \"Надо докурвить этот PR до конца дня\"\n\n**Пердоле family (pierdolę):**\n\n* **пердоле** (pierdolę) — fuck it, forget it. \"Пердоле тот легаси\"\n* **опердолить** (opierdolić) — do fast and hard. \"Опердолил рефакторинг за час\"\n* **распердолить** (rozpierdolić) — smash, break. \"Кто распердолил мейн?\"\n* **пердольнуть** (pierdolnąć) — hit, smash. \"Пердольни рестарт сервера\"\n* **спердолить** (spierdolić) — fuck up, fail. \"Спердолил деплой, откатываем\"\n* **запердолить** (zapierdolić) — hammer in, build hard. \"Запердолил фичу за ночь\"\n* **выпердолить** (wypierdolić) — throw out. \"Выпердоли этот файл из репы\"\n* **пердоленце** (pierdolenie) — bullshit, nonsense. \"Хватит пердоленца, давай код\"\n* **nie pierdol** — don't bullshit. \"Nie pierdol что тесты зелёные, я видел лог\"\n\n**Jebać family:**\n\n* **ебать** (jebać) — like Russian, but with Polish flavor\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, top tier. \"Архитектура — заебисте\"\n* **наебать** (najebać) — mess up. \"Кто наебал в конфиге?\"\n* **поебать** (pojebać) — break, mix up. \"Мерж поебал все конфликты\"\n* **уебать** (ujebać) — hit, wipe. \"Уебал дропом всю базу\"\n* **доебаться** (dojebać się) — nitpick. \"Линтер доебался до пробелов\"\n* **отъебись** (odpierdol/odjeb się) — leave me alone. \"Отъебись со своим форматированием\"\n* **поджебать** (podjebać) — tease, troll. \"Это я поджебываю, не парься\"\n\n**Дупа series (dupa = ass):**\n\n* **дупа** (dupa) — ass. \"Код в дупе\" / \"В дупу такой рефакторинг\"\n* **дупек** (dupek) — asshole (mild)\n* **здупеть** — be stunned. \"Я здупел от этого бага\"\n* **задупье** — middle of nowhere. \"Этот модуль — задупье проекта\"\n* **удупить** — kill, destroy. \"Удупи этот бранч\"\n\n**Холера and company:**\n\n* **холера** (cholera) — damn. \"Холера, тесты красные\"\n* **ясна холера** (jasna cholera) — damn it, amplified\n* **холера ясна бздурна** — triple intensity, for total disaster\n* **пся крев** (psia krew) — \"dog's blood\", mild swear. \"Пся крев, кто это коммитнул?\"\n* **шлаг** (szlag) — damn, stroke. \"Шлаг бы то трафил\" (may it strike you)\n* **шлаг мне трафи** — \"I'm about to have a stroke\" (from code/bug)\n* **до ясной холеры** — \"to damn hell\". \"Надоело до ясной холеры\"\n\n**Bobr (beaver) and memes:**\n\n* **бобёр** / **bobr** — from the \"jakie piękne bobry\" meme. Reaction to something attractive or unexpectedly good. \"О, бобёр! Какой чистый код\"\n* **бобр курва** — awed surprise. Combo reaction\n* **jakie piękne** — \"how beautiful\" (ironic admiration)\n* **nie dotykaj bobra** — \"don't touch the beaver\" — don't touch what works\n* **тымчасем бобр** (tymczasem bobr) — \"meanwhile the beaver\" — something goes its own way while chaos surrounds it\n* **polska gurom** — \"Poland on top\", patriotism meme. Use for any success: \"Тесты зелёные — polska gurom!\"\n* **janusz** — typical Janusz, cheapskate. \"Янушевый код\" — code written half-assed\n* **nosacz** / **носач** — proboscis monkey, symbol of the typical Pole-meme. Reaction to absurdity\n* **kiełbasa** — sausage. \"Пахнет кельбасой\" — smells like bad code\n* **pierogi** — dumplings. Not a swear, but: \"Залепил пероги\" — slapped together hacks\n\n**Approval / quality:**\n\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, best approval word\n* **козак** (kozak) — well done, champion. \"Ты козак, ахпер!\"\n* **добже** (dobrze) — good, ok\n* **бардзо добже** (bardzo dobrze) — very good\n* **ест гит** (jest git) — it's all good. Pun with git\n* **cudo** (цудо) — miracle, beauty. \"Этот рефакторинг — цудо\"\n* **петарда** (petarda) — bomb, fire. \"Фича — петарда!\"\n* **мистжоство** (mistrzostwo) — mastery. \"Это мистжоство, аро\"\n\n**Disapproval:**\n\n* **гувно** (gówno) — shit. \"Код — тотальне гувно\"\n* **гувняны** (gówniany) — shitty. \"Гувняны тест, переписывай\"\n* **хлам** (chłam) — junk. \"Выкинь этот хлам\"\n* **бздура** (bzdura) — nonsense. \"Что за бздура в этом PR?\"\n* **клопот** (kłopot) — trouble. \"Мамы клопот\" — minor issue\n* **порожняк** (porażka) — failure. \"Деплой — тотальна порожняк\"\n\n**Reactions and phrases:**\n\n* **о курва!** — universal \"wow!\" / \"fuck!\"\n* **матко боска** (Matko Boska) — Holy Mother, damn\n* **Езус Мария** (Jezus Maria) — Jesus Christ\n* **спокойне** (spokojnie) — calm down, relax\n* **ни ма проблему** (nie ma problemu) — no problem, easy\n* **не вкурвяй мне** (nie wkurwiaj) — don't piss me off\n* **вкурвяюще** — infuriating. \"Этот баг вкурвяюще стабильный\"\n* **лечь** (leć) — fly, go. \"Лечь, деплоим!\"\n* **тшимай** (trzymaj) — here, catch. \"Тшимай, аро, вот фикс\"\n* **спердаляй** (spierdalaj) — get lost (rude). \"Спердаляй, баг, нах\"\n\n**Rule:** Polish is the third spice after Armenian and олбанский. Not required in every message. When appropriate — заебисте, when not — гувно. Derivatives of пердоле and курва are universally useful.\n\n---\n\n## Mixing Styles\n\n* **Don't blend everything at once.** Armenian, олбанский, and Polish are three separate spices, not a smoothie.\n* **Max two styles per message.** Armenian + олбанский ok. Armenian + Polish ok. Олбанский + Polish ok. All three together = unreadable mess.\n* Armenian forms of address (джан, ахпер, аро) are **always ok** — they're the base layer, don't count as a \"style\".\n* Олбанский and Polish — **sprinkle**, don't drench. One or two words for expressiveness, not every sentence.\n\n---\n\n## Tone\n\n* Direct, no fluff\n* Jokes and light teasing are fine (in moderation)\n* If something is shit — say it's shit, no sugarcoating\n* Praise only when genuinely deserved, don't shower with compliments\n\n---\n\n## Avoid\n\n* \"Great question!\" and other empty phrases\n* Excessive politeness (\"could you please...\", \"allow me...\")\n* Bureaucratic language\n* Emojis (unless explicitly asked)\n* Mixing all three styles in one message\n* **AskUserQuestion tool spam** — use it **only** when explicitly asked or when it's truly critical. For clarifications, just ask in normal conversation.\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Agent Session Manager Repository Instructions\n\n`AGENTS.md` is the canonical instruction file for this repository. `CLAUDE.md`\nmust remain a relative symlink to this file so every supported agent reads the\nsame rules.\n\n## Product and Specification\n\n- The product is Agent Session Manager, exposed as the `ax` command.\n- Treat the [normative AX specification](https://github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager-spec/blob/main/SPEC.md)\n  as the authority for product behavior. Do not invent behavior that conflicts\n  with it.\n- This repository contains the implementation. Keep specifications and\n  implementation changes traceable to their task-board work.\n\n## Change Workflow\n\n- Track every change in `task-board` before implementation begins. Keep the\n  task status, checklist, notes, and task-scoped outcome evidence current.\n- An authorized repository-changing task includes automatic delivery: stage\n  only its reviewed scope, create author-signed commits, publish the feature\n  branch, open or update the pull request, complete review and checks, and land\n  the accepted exact head in `main`. Do not stop merely to ask whether the\n  completed task should be committed, pushed, or landed.\n- The repository bootstrap is the only direct-bootstrap exception. Every later\n  change must be reviewed through a pull request. After approval and passing\n  checks, land the exact signed feature head with a plain fast-forward push to\n  `main`; never land through GitHub rebase, squash, or merge-commit actions.\n- Never push unreviewed work, a different commit from the reviewed PR head, or\n  a forced update to `main`. GitHub records the reviewed PR as indirectly\n  merged when its exact head becomes reachable from `main`.\n- Before creating a branch or worktree, fetch `origin`, switch to local `main`,\n  and fast-forward it to `origin/main`. Never branch from stale `main`.\n\n  ```bash\n  git fetch origin main\n  git switch main\n  git merge --ff-only origin/main\n  test \"$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" = \"$(git rev-parse origin/main)\"\n  git switch -c <branch-name>\n  ```\n\n- Use this signed linear landing flow after the PR is approved and its checks\n  pass:\n\n  ```bash\n  BRANCH=$(git branch --show-current)\n  test \"$BRANCH\" != main\n  git fetch origin\n\n  # If main advanced, rewrite locally and sign every rewritten commit.\n  BEFORE_REBASE=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\n  git rebase -S origin/main\n\n  # Update only the feature branch when the signed head changed.\n  if [ \"$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" != \"$BEFORE_REBASE\" ]; then\n    git push --force-with-lease origin HEAD:refs/heads/\"$BRANCH\"\n  fi\n  ```\n\n  If the feature head changed, stop here until that exact head passes the\n  required PR review and checks. Then land it without rewriting:\n\n  ```bash\n  BRANCH=$(git branch --show-current)\n  test \"$BRANCH\" != main\n  git fetch origin\n\n  # Verify every commit that will be introduced and require a fast-forward.\n  for sha in $(git rev-list --reverse origin/main..HEAD); do\n    git verify-commit \"$sha\" || exit 1\n  done\n  git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main HEAD\n\n  SIGNED_HEAD=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\n  test \"$(git rev-parse \"origin/$BRANCH\")\" = \"$SIGNED_HEAD\"\n  test \"$(gh pr view --json headRefOid --jq .headRefOid)\" = \"$SIGNED_HEAD\"\n\n  # Plain push fails safely if main advanced. Never force main.\n  git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/main\n  git fetch origin\n  test \"$(git rev-parse origin/main)\" = \"$SIGNED_HEAD\"\n  git verify-commit \"$SIGNED_HEAD\"\n  ```\n\n  If the plain push to `main` is rejected, refresh and repeat the signed\n  rebase/review cycle; do not fall back to a GitHub merge action or force the\n  protected branch.\n\n- For isolated work, create task-scoped worktrees under\n  `.temp/<TASK-ID>/worktree/`; do not place temporary worktrees beside the\n  repository.\n- Keep changes focused, review the diff, and run relevant tests and validation\n  before opening or updating its pull request.\n\n## Git Identity and Signing\n\nUse the repository-local identity and SSH signing configuration below:\n\n```bash\ngit config --local user.name \"Ivan Oparin\"\ngit config --local user.email \"oparin@me.com\"\ngit config --local gpg.format ssh\ngit config --local user.signingkey /Users/iv/.ssh/ivanopcode.pub\ngit config --local commit.gpgsign true\ngit config --local tag.gpgsign true\n```\n\nEvery commit and tag must be signed. Verify commits with `git verify-commit`\nbefore publishing them. Rebase locally with `git rebase -S` whenever history\nmust move; GitHub rebase and squash create different unsigned commit objects\nand are not valid landing mechanisms. Agent co-authorship is allowed through an\nexplicit `Co-authored-by` trailer when the task or user requests it and a real,\nintentionally configured agent name and email are available. Keep Ivan Oparin\nas the commit author and never invent an identity or verification address.\n\n## Curator-Managed Skills\n\n- `Skillfile.json` is the source of truth for project-local skills and target\n  agent adapters.\n- The `project-management` skill and `task-board` CLI are global development\n  dependencies. Do not declare, install, vendor, or shadow\n  `project-management` in this repository; verify and use the global skill and\n  global `task-board` command.\n- Use Curator to install or update skills. Never edit files under `.agents/`,\n  `.claude/skills/`, or `.codex/skills/` by hand; Curator owns those paths.\n- Resolve Curator-managed project commands from `.agents/bin/` first. The\n  globally managed `task-board` command is the intentional exception. Other\n  bare commands require explicit validation before use.\n- Run `curator install` after changing `Skillfile.json`, then require\n  `curator status --check` to exit successfully.\n\n## Go Development and Testing\n\n- Use the Curator-managed `go-testing-tools` skill for every Go implementation,\n  refactor, or test task. Read its `SKILL.md` before technical work and follow\n  its closed-loop workflow.\n- Add or update tests whenever behavior changes. Prefer pure reducer tests,\n  component tests, and mocked integration tests in that order when the AX code\n  uses Elm-style TUI architecture.\n- Drive real production entry points and include negative tests for validation,\n  authorization, refusal, and attestation gates.\n- Run the relevant package tests while iterating, then run the repository-wide\n  test and coverage commands before review:\n\n  ```bash\n  go test ./... -v\n  go test ./... -cover\n  ```\n\n- Keep tests deterministic and free of network or terminal dependencies. Do\n  not use stubs or mock-only behavior to conceal an invalid platform or product\n  assumption.\n\n## Documentation and Artifacts\n\n- Write public specifications, repository documentation, and code comments in\n  English.\n- Store temporary logs and validation artifacts under\n  `.temp/<TASK-ID>/`. Attach task deliverables to the owning board item with a\n  task-scoped resource name.\n- Keep `README.md` and its tool/output documentation aligned with the current\n  repository state.\n\n</INSTRUCTIONS>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<environment_context>\n  <cwd>/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree</cwd>\n  <shell>zsh</shell>\n  <current_date>2026-08-31</current_date>\n  <timezone>Asia/Tbilisi</timezone>\n  <filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree</root><root>/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"disabled\"><file_system type=\"unrestricted\" /></permission_profile></filesystem>\n</environment_context>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0591b-218e-7791-8c1d-c09fdfe98b6c","create_time":1788201347.392256,"content_item_kinds":["plugins.recommendations","agents_md.instructions","environments.environment_context"]}}}
7:{"timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:35:47.393Z","ordinal":6,"type":"world_state","payload":{"full":true,"state":{"agents_md":{"directory":"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree","text":"<!-- Generated by agents-infra; do not edit directly. -->\n<!-- Source: /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/AGENTS.md -->\n\n# Global Instructions\n\nThis document loads all instruction modules automatically.\n\n## Modules\n\n# Platform\n\n* Before building/testing, **inspect the project** to determine the intended target platform(s).\n* If multiple platforms are supported, prefer **iOS** when available.\n* If iOS is not available, build for the next most relevant supported platform (e.g. **macOS**, etc.).\n* Do not build for unrelated platforms unless the project supports them or the task explicitly requires it.\n# Project Structure & Environment\n\n## Shell & Environment\n\n* Shell: **zsh**\n* Shell framework: **oh-my-zsh**\n\n---\n\n## Project Structure\n\n* `.temp/` — temporary utilities, logs, debugging artifacts (**gitignored**).\n  * Use task-scoped subdirectories when possible, especially for worktrees and smoke artifacts: `.temp/<TASK-ID>/...`\n  * Temporary `git worktree` checkouts should live under `.temp/`, not beside the main repo checkout.\n* `.scripts/` — reusable scripts and utilities for the project.\n* `diagrams/` (project root) — all diagrams live here.\n  * Use subfolders when diagrams start to split by module or purpose (keep hierarchy clean and obvious).\n# Attachments\n\nUse this contract when the runtime materializes user-provided files for the agent.\n\n## Contract\n\n- Manifest file name: `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n- Environment variable: `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\n- Default project-local fallback: `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n\nThe manifest may be either:\n\n- a JSON array of attachment objects\n- or an object with top-level key `attachments`\n\nEach attachment object should contain:\n\n- `id` — stable attachment identifier\n- `name` — original file name\n- `mime_type` — media type such as `image/png`\n- `size_bytes` — byte size\n- `sha256` — optional content hash\n- `local_path` — absolute local path to the materialized file\n\nOptional fields:\n\n- `source`\n- `created_at`\n- `metadata`\n\n## Agent behavior\n\n- Treat `local_path` as the source of truth for file access.\n- Treat incoming attachments as read-only inputs.\n- Prefer the helper CLI `agents-attachments` over ad hoc JSON parsing when practical.\n- If a tool needs a file path, pass `local_path` directly.\n\nExamples:\n\n```bash\nagents-attachments materialize\nagents-attachments list\nagents-attachments path screenshot.png\nmkdir -p .temp/attachments\ncp \"$(agents-attachments path screenshot.png)\" .temp/attachments/\nagents-attachments stage-images screenshot.png --out-dir .temp/image-intake\n```\n\n## Image intake and inspection\n\nUse this workflow for user-provided screenshots, photos, scans, SIM/package photos,\nand other image evidence. It is board-agnostic and works with explicit local\npaths or the generic attachments manifest.\n\nStage first, inspect staged files:\n\n```bash\nagents-attachments stage-images ./photo.heic screenshot.png --out-dir .temp/image-intake\nagents-attachments stage-images --all --manifest .temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json --out-dir .temp/image-intake\n```\n\nBehavior:\n\n- accepts explicit local paths and manifest references by `id`, `name`, or local file name\n- treats originals as read-only inputs and writes staged files under `--out-dir`\n  (default `.temp/image-intake`)\n- writes and prints a machine-readable mapping JSON\n  (default `<out-dir>/image-stage-map.json`)\n- copies normal image formats as-is\n- normalizes HEIC/HEIF to PNG with macOS `sips` when available, then falls back\n  to ImageMagick (`magick`, then `convert`) on other platforms or when `sips`\n  is unavailable\n- fails clearly if HEIC/HEIF conversion is required but no converter is available\n- redacts ICCID/IMSI/key-like strings from persisted source labels and staged\n  filenames while keeping content hashes for auditability\n\nInspection rules:\n\n- Prefer direct visual inspection through the runtime's vision or image-viewing\n  capability before OCR.\n- Use OCR only as a bounded fallback when direct inspection cannot answer the\n  question. Do not run unbounded OCR/upscale/retry loops; keep fallback attempts\n  narrow and task-scoped.\n- Tie every observation to a staged filename and mapping entry.\n- Record per-file evidence, confidence (`high`, `medium`, `low`), uncertainty,\n  and any redactions applied.\n- Redact ICCID, IMSI, QR payloads, activation codes, tokens, keys, passwords,\n  and similar secrets before writing notes, docs, task resources, logs, or\n  human-facing output. Do not persist raw sensitive identifiers extracted from\n  images.\n\nChild-agent handoff:\n\n- Pass the staged image directory and mapping JSON path.\n- Propagate `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST` only when the child also needs the\n  original generic manifest.\n- Do not require a board, board resource, task ID, or status field for image\n  intake.\n\n## Codex bootstrap path\n\nIf the runtime did not pre-materialize files but Codex rollout history is available, the helper can bootstrap a local manifest from the current thread:\n\n```bash\nagents-attachments materialize\n```\n\nBehavior:\n\n- uses `CODEX_THREAD_ID` by default\n- locates the matching `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl`\n- extracts `input_image` payloads from user turns\n- writes files under `.temp/agents-attachments/`\n- writes `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n\nOverrides:\n\n- `agents-attachments materialize --thread-id ...`\n- `agents-attachments materialize --session /abs/path/to/rollout.jsonl`\n- `agents-attachments materialize --out-dir .temp/custom-attachments`\n- `agents-attachments materialize --manifest .temp/custom-manifest.json`\n\n## Spawned agents\n\nWhen a parent process spawns a child agent, it should:\n\n- propagate `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\n- allow access to the directory that contains the materialized files\n- keep child agents on the same manifest unless there is an explicit reason to narrow scope\n\n## Runtime boundary\n\nThis repo defines the contract and helper tooling. It does not itself ingest chat attachments.\n\nThe runtime/launcher is responsible for:\n\n- extracting files from the host conversation layer\n- materializing them on disk\n- writing `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n- exporting `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\n# Browser Automation\n\n## Focus Policy\n\n* Browser scripting and page inspection must be **no-focus by default**.\n* Do not activate, raise, focus, or make Safari/Chrome/front browser windows frontmost unless the user explicitly asks to see or interact with the browser.\n* Prefer background-capable tools and APIs such as `mac-safari-session open-bg`, `snapshot`, `run-js`, or equivalent browser-session helpers that preserve the user's active window.\n* Do not use AppleScript patterns such as `tell application \"Safari\" to activate`, focused new windows/tabs, UI scripting clicks, or frontmost-window manipulation just to inspect DOM, links, text, downloads, or authenticated page state.\n* If a browser action truly needs human interaction, for example passkey confirmation, SSO, CAPTCHA, file picker, or a permission prompt, say what is needed and let the user open or focus the browser unless they explicitly ask the agent to do it.\n\n## Authenticated Sessions\n\n* Reuse the user's authenticated browser session only through same-origin page context or approved browser-session tooling.\n* Never export, print, persist, or bypass protections around cookies, local storage, session storage, authorization headers, tokens, or other browser secrets.\n* Persist only sanitized page content, URLs, downloaded files, metadata, and notes needed for the task.\n# Remote Agent Workers\n\nUse this pattern when a remote machine has an authenticated agent runtime\n(for example Claude Code) and the local host has the project checkout that must\nremain the source of truth.\n\n## Core Model\n\n- Treat the remote machine as a disposable worker, not as the owner of the\n  project.\n- Run the remote agent in an isolated remote copy or worktree.\n- Bring changes back to the local checkout as a patch, commit, or reviewed file\n  transfer.\n- Verify locally before accepting the work.\n- Keep the workflow host-agnostic: do not hardcode a specific host alias, user,\n  install path, shell profile, board setup, or sync tool in reusable\n  instructions.\n\n## Variables\n\nUse explicit variables in notes, scripts, and task resources:\n\n```bash\nREMOTE_SSH=\"user@host-or-ssh-alias\"\nREMOTE_CLAUDE=\"claude\"                  # or an absolute path discovered on the remote\nLOCAL_PROJECT=\"$PWD\"\nRUN_ID=\"$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)-task\"\nLOCAL_RUN_DIR=\".temp/remote-agent/$RUN_ID\"\nREMOTE_RUN_ROOT=\"/tmp/remote-agent-runs\"\nREMOTE_PROJECT=\"$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/project\"\nREMOTE_PROMPT=\"$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/prompt.md\"\nLOCAL_PATCH=\"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/remote.patch\"\n```\n\nThe exact transfer mechanism is intentionally interchangeable. `git clone`,\n`git worktree`, `rsync`, `scp`, `tar` over SSH, shared volumes, or a board/server\nartifact flow are all acceptable when they preserve the same contract:\n\n1. Remote agent gets a task-scoped copy of the needed project files.\n2. Remote agent writes only inside that copy.\n3. Local host receives a reviewable change artifact.\n\n## Readiness Check\n\nBefore delegating real work, verify the remote access and runtime without\nprinting credentials or private config:\n\n```bash\nssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=10 \"$REMOTE_SSH\" 'hostname; whoami; pwd'\nssh -o BatchMode=yes \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"command -v '$REMOTE_CLAUDE' || true\"\nssh -o BatchMode=yes \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"'$REMOTE_CLAUDE' --version\"\n```\n\nIf non-interactive SSH does not load the same `PATH` as an interactive shell,\nuse an absolute remote binary path or prefix `PATH` inside the remote command.\nDo not inspect or print OAuth tokens, API keys, cookie stores, keychain exports,\nor files such as agent credential JSON.\n\nRun a one-line non-interactive smoke before asking for edits:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"cd /tmp && '$REMOTE_CLAUDE' -p 'Reply with remote-agent-ok.'\"\n```\n\n## Prepare A Remote Copy\n\nStart from a clean local state:\n\n```bash\ngit status --short\ngit diff --check\nmkdir -p \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR\"\n```\n\nPrefer a task-scoped local worktree or archive so the remote agent cannot see\nunrelated untracked files:\n\n```bash\ngit worktree add \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source\" HEAD\n```\n\nThen transfer the project copy using any suitable tool. This tar-over-SSH shape\nis portable and does not require the remote host to have repository access:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"mkdir -p '$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID'\"\ntar \\\n  --exclude='.git' \\\n  --exclude='.temp' \\\n  --exclude='DerivedData' \\\n  --exclude='node_modules' \\\n  -C \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source\" \\\n  -czf \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source.tgz\" .\nscp \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source.tgz\" \"$REMOTE_SSH:$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/source.tgz\"\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"\n  mkdir -p '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  tar -xzf '$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/source.tgz' -C '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  cd '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  git init -q &&\n  git add -A &&\n  git commit -q -m baseline &&\n  git update-ref refs/agents-infra/remote-baseline HEAD\n\"\n```\n\nFor repositories the remote can access directly, `git clone` plus checkout of\nthe exact branch/commit is fine. Before the worker starts, create\n`refs/agents-infra/remote-baseline` at that exact commit as well. The private\nbaseline ref, together with intent-to-add marking during export, makes the\nreturned patch include committed, tracked working-tree, and non-ignored\nuntracked changes without imposing a commit-timing rule on the worker.\n\n## Prompt Contract\n\nWrite the prompt to a file and transfer it as an artifact. Include:\n\n- task goal and acceptance criteria\n- exact files or directories in scope\n- commands the remote agent should run\n- \"do not read or modify files outside this project copy\"\n- expected handoff: summary, tests run, known failures, and a reviewable change\n  artifact\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ncat > \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/prompt.md\" <<'PROMPT'\nYou are working in a disposable remote copy of a project.\nDo not read or modify files outside this directory.\n\nTask:\n- Implement the requested change with minimal scope.\n- Preserve existing behavior unless the task says otherwise.\n- Run the relevant tests or build checks.\n- Return the completed scoped change to the parent as a reviewable artifact.\n- Do not publish or land from this disposable worker; the parent owns delivery.\n\nHandoff:\n- Summarize files changed.\n- List validation commands and results.\n- Mention blockers or skipped checks explicitly.\nPROMPT\n\nscp \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/prompt.md\" \"$REMOTE_SSH:$REMOTE_PROMPT\"\n```\n\n## Spawn Claude Remotely\n\nUse non-interactive Claude for remote worker runs:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"\n  cd '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  '$REMOTE_CLAUDE' -p --permission-mode bypassPermissions \\\"\\$(cat '$REMOTE_PROMPT')\\\"\n\"\n```\n\nUse `--permission-mode bypassPermissions` only for disposable remote copies where\nthe agent is intentionally allowed to edit and run local validation commands.\nFor less isolated environments, prefer a narrower permission mode or\n`--allowedTools` set that permits only the required read/edit/test tools.\n\nIf the remote Claude binary is not on non-interactive `PATH`, set it explicitly:\n\n```bash\nREMOTE_CLAUDE=\"$HOME/.local/bin/claude\"\n```\n\nor prefix the remote command:\n\n```bash\nPATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\" claude -p ...\n```\n\n## Bring Changes Back\n\nNever trust only the remote agent's summary. Export and inspect the actual diff:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"\n  cd '$REMOTE_PROJECT' &&\n  git status --short &&\n  git add -N -- . &&\n  git diff --binary refs/agents-infra/remote-baseline > '$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/remote.patch'\n\"\n\nscp \"$REMOTE_SSH:$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID/remote.patch\" \"$LOCAL_PATCH\"\ngit apply --check \"$LOCAL_PATCH\"\ngit apply \"$LOCAL_PATCH\"\n```\n\nIf the patch does not apply cleanly, inspect it manually and either rerun the\nremote agent with a tighter prompt or apply the useful parts by hand.\n\n## Local Verification\n\nAfter applying remote changes locally:\n\n- inspect `git diff`\n- run the local build/test/lint commands appropriate for the project\n- verify no unrelated files changed\n- document remote run path, commands, and logs in the normal task notes or\n  `.temp/` scratch notes\n\nThe local host remains responsible for final acceptance, commit, and push.\n\n## Cleanup\n\nClean up only after the patch and logs are captured:\n\n```bash\nssh \"$REMOTE_SSH\" \"rm -rf '$REMOTE_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID'\"\ngit worktree remove \"$LOCAL_RUN_DIR/source\"\n```\n\nKeep local `.temp/remote-agent/$RUN_ID/` logs until the task is complete.\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- `claude: command not found`: non-interactive SSH PATH differs from the\n  interactive shell. Discover the remote binary path and use it explicitly.\n- auth prompt or subscription failure: stop and ask the human to fix remote\n  Claude auth on that machine. Do not scrape credentials.\n- patch is empty: inspect remote status and the prompt; the agent may have only\n  answered without editing.\n- patch conflicts locally: rebase/refresh the remote copy or apply manually.\n- remote tests pass but local tests fail: trust local verification and reopen or\n  rerun with the local failure context.\n- task needs secrets or private services: pass only task-scoped credentials\n  through an approved secure channel; never copy broad local dotfiles.\n# Allowed Tools\n\nAgent may use these tools freely.\n\n## Build / Apple tooling\n\n* `xcodebuild` (CLI builds/tests)\n* `swift` (toolchain / SwiftPM)\n* `simctl` (simulator management)\n\n---\n\n## Scripting / Automation\n\n### git\n\n* Inspect diffs, config, repo state.\n* Agent may check git config to detect default branch and repo location.\n* **Never run a repo-wide destructive git command.** `git checkout -- .`, `git restore .`, `git reset --hard`, `git clean -fd` and their equivalents discard working-tree state that is not yours and cannot be recovered — in a shared checkout that is somebody else's unfinished task. Name single paths instead, and prefer a worktree when you need a clean tree.\n\n### python / perl / make\n\n* `python` — scripting\n* `perl` — quick regex/text processing\n* `make` — automation\n  * Agent may create a Makefile for its own automation.\n  * If a Makefile already exists, extend it (do not replace).\n\n---\n\n## Project-Local MCP Servers\n\nAgents-infra managed MCP servers are project-local opt-in only. Do not enable\nthem globally by default.\n\nSupported shared MCP server definitions:\n\n| Server | Use | Enablement |\n|--------|-----|------------|\n| `figma` | Figma Dev Mode / design context | Add `figma` to `.agents/.configs/project-config.toml` |\n| `lldb` | LLDB debugging through `lldb-mcp` stdio bridge | Add `lldb` to `.agents/.configs/project-config.toml`; macOS `./setup.sh` installs Homebrew `llvm` and an `lldb-mcp` wrapper when needed |\n| `safari` | Safari Technology Preview web inspection through `safaridriver --mcp` | Add `safari` to `.agents/.configs/project-config.toml`; requires Safari Technology Preview with web developer features, remote automation, and external agents enabled |\n\nProject opt-in example:\n\n```toml\n[mcp]\nenabled_servers = [\"figma\", \"lldb\", \"safari\"]\n```\n\n`enabled_servers` is one agent-agnostic list per project — there is a single\nsource of truth for which MCP servers a project opts into, not a separate\nlist per agent. Start Codex through `agents-infra codex` so this list and the\nshared registry are composed into Codex `-c` overrides. Start Claude Code\nthrough `agents-infra claude` so the same list and registry are composed into a\nClaude Code `--mcp-config` payload instead. Both launchers apply their own\nconfigured primary-session `yolo_mode`; use `-d` only as the explicit ad-hoc\nfull-trust escape hatch.\nBoth launchers read the exact same `enabled_servers` list — enabling a\nserver enables it for whichever agent you launch.\n\n---\n\n## Installing additional CLI tools\n\n* If the agent identifies a **strong, justified need** for additional CLI tools, it may:\n  * ask to install them, **or**\n  * attempt to install them directly **if permissions allow**.\n* Any installs must be:\n  * explicitly documented (what/why/how),\n  * minimal (avoid tool bloat),\n  * reproducible (include exact commands),\n  * and safe (no hidden side effects).\n\n---\n\n## Tool Readiness Check (mandatory)\n\n* Before using any tool for real project work, the agent must verify it can run and produce expected output.\n* Verification outputs / scratch artifacts must be stored in `.temp/` (since it is not versioned).\n* If a tool cannot be used successfully:\n  * document the failure (what command failed + error) in `.temp/` logs and/or `.temp/tasks.md`;\n  * do not proceed with workflows that assume the tool works.\n# Skills & Plugins\n\n## Using Skills (IMPORTANT)\n\n**Before performing any technical task, check if a relevant skill exists.**\n\n* Search `agents/skills/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.codex/skills/` for applicable skills.\n* If a skill covers the task — **read and follow it**, don't improvise.\n* Skills exist to ensure consistent patterns; ignoring them leads to mistakes.\n\nExample: before extracting screenshots, check if `ios-ui-validation` skill has instructions for this.\n\n---\n\n## Skill Locations\n\n| Tool | Global | Project-local |\n|------|--------|---------------|\n| Claude Code | `~/.claude/skills/` | `.claude/skills/` |\n| Codex CLI | `~/.codex/skills/` | `.codex/skills/` |\n\n---\n\n## Editing Shared Skills & Instructions (CRITICAL)\n\n* `~/.agents/`, `~/.claude/`, and `~/.codex/` are installed runtime locations, **not** the authoring source of truth.\n* **Never edit `~/.agents` directly** when changing shared skills, global instructions, configs, or helper scripts.\n* First identify the real source repo:\n  * shared global infra/instructions/config → `relux-agents-infra`\n  * separately versioned skill/tool → that skill's own source repo\n* If the source repo is not obvious, stop and find it before editing anything.\n* Make the change in the source repo, then sync/install it into `~/.agents` with the repo's setup/install flow.\n* Treat direct edits inside `~/.agents` as local hotfixes only when the user explicitly asks for that, and say clearly that the source repo still needs the same change.\n\n## Fix Reusable Workflow Contracts At Source\n\n* Do not build project-local workarounds around a broken, incomplete, or awkward reusable skill/tool contract.\n* If a reusable skill/tool blocks the clean implementation path, first identify its real source repository, fix the source, run that repo's setup/install flow, and then use the corrected skill/tool.\n* Keep temporary probes under the active project's `.temp/`, but do not leave reusable scripts, command patterns, Make targets, or workflow rules stranded in a product repo when they belong to the shared skill/tool.\n* When a skill/tool fix is required during a tracked task, document the source repo path, setup command, and verification evidence in the task's normal documentation or board flow before continuing.\n\n---\n\n## Creating Skills (Our Pattern)\n\n**Always use the `agents/skills/` pattern:**\n\n1. Create the actual skill in `agents/skills/<skill-name>/`\n2. Symlink to `.claude/skills/` and `.codex/skills/`\n\n```bash\n# Create skill\nmkdir -p agents/skills/my-skill\n# ... add SKILL.md, references/, assets/, etc.\n\n# Symlink for Claude Code\nmkdir -p .claude/skills\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .claude/skills/my-skill\n\n# Symlink for Codex CLI\nmkdir -p .codex/skills\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .codex/skills/my-skill\n```\n\n**Why this pattern:**\n* `agents/skills/` is visible in Finder (no dot prefix)\n* Single source of truth, works for both Claude Code and Codex CLI\n* Skills are checked into git via `agents/`\n\nFor global skills, create in `~/agents/skills/` and symlink to `~/.claude/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/`.\n\n---\n\n## Skill Structure & Rules\n\n* Agent may create custom skills when there is a **clear, justified need** for specialized workflows.\n* Use the `skill-creator` skill to scaffold new skills if available.\n\n#### Skill structure\n\n```\nskill-name/\n├── SKILL.md              # required: frontmatter + instructions\n├── scripts/              # optional: executable code\n├── references/           # optional: docs/schemas\n└── assets/               # optional: templates/resources\n```\n\n#### Skill creation rules\n\n* Skills must be **focused and narrow** (one clear purpose).\n* Keep `SKILL.md` under 500 lines; split into references if needed.\n* Test any included scripts before committing.\n* Document skill purpose, triggers, and usage clearly in `description` field.\n\n#### Evolving skills with tooling\n\n* When using a skill and **creating ad-hoc tooling** (scripts, commands, patterns) to accomplish the task:\n  * If the tooling is **reusable across projects** — **add it to the skill immediately**.\n  * Include it in `scripts/` or document it in `SKILL.md`.\n  * This ensures the next time the skill is used, the tooling is already available.\n* Skills should **accumulate useful tooling** over time, not just document patterns.\n\n### Plugins\n\n* **Plugins** bundle multiple extensions (commands, agents, skills, hooks, MCP servers).\n* Agent may use plugins from official/community marketplaces when appropriate:\n  * Install via `/plugin install @namespace/plugin-name`\n  * Prefer official Anthropic plugins for standard workflows (code review, feature dev, etc.)\n  * Document installed plugins in `.temp/plugins.md` with rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Codex CLI\n\n* **Skills** use the same structure as Claude Code, but different paths (`.codex/skills/`).\n* Codex also checks `$CWD/../.codex/skills` and `$REPO_ROOT/.codex/skills`.\n* Priority: CWD > repo root > user (`~/.codex/skills/`) > system.\n* No plugin system in Codex CLI — use skills only.\n# Skill Triggers\n\nAutomatic skill activation rules. When these topics come up — **FIRST load the skill via Skill tool**, then proceed.\n\n## Language Policy\n\n- **Specs, docs, code comments:** English only\n- **Sub-agent prompts:** English only\n- **Task/story/epic descriptions:** English only\n- **User communication:** Match user's language (default: English)\n\n---\n\n## Triggers\n\n| Triggers | Skill | Action |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| architecture diagram, C4, structurizr, plantuml, sequence/component/class diagram | `architecture-diagrams` | Follow skill for patterns, folder structure, rendering. |\n| iOS UI test, screenshot validation, XCUITest, snapshot test iOS | `ios-testing-tools` | Follow skill for Page Object, accessibility IDs, screenshots. |\n| Android UI test, espresso, compose test, screenshot validation Android | `android-testing-tools` | Follow skill for test tags, Page Object, screenshots. |\n| currency exchange, обмен валют, драмы, рубли, AMD, RUB, rate.am, раттам, курс обмена, поменять валюту, конвертация валют | `currency-exchange` | Use `exchange` CLI. NEVER scrape rate sources manually. |\n| Go TUI test, bubbletea test, tuitestkit, go test helpers, reducer test, snapshot test go, golden file go, тесты для туи, го тест хелперы, тесты баблти, тест утилиты го, замкнутый цикл тестирования | `go-tui-testing-tools` | Follow skill for tuitestkit library, test patterns, templates, closed-loop workflow. |\n\n---\n\n## Workflow Details\n\nFor task-tracking or board-centric workflows, prefer the repo-local skill or the explicitly requested skill. Do not assume a global default board/task-management skill from infra alone.\n# Diagrams Rules\n\n**Mandatory.**\n\n## General\n\n* Do not put \"everything\" into one diagram/file.\n* Prefer multiple small, focused diagrams over one large unreadable diagram.\n* Each diagram must have a clear title and narrow purpose.\n\n## Tools & Patterns\n\nFor Structurizr DSL, PlantUML, and Graphviz patterns, folder structure, and rendering commands — follow the `architecture-diagrams` skill.\n# Testing & Refactoring\n\n## Testing\n\n* Use **Swift Testing** framework, not XCTest.\n* Tests must be in **Swift**, not ObjC.\n\n## Android App State Preservation\n\n* Preserve the currently installed app, its data, granted permissions, active\n  sessions, and user-visible state by default during Android build, test, and\n  device-debugging workflows.\n* Prefer read-only inspection, targeted instrumentation, in-app navigation,\n  activity relaunch, and other non-destructive verification paths before\n  changing package or process state.\n* Do not reinstall, uninstall, clear app data/storage, revoke permissions, or\n  force-stop the app merely to obtain a clean baseline or retry a check. Use\n  such operations only when evidence shows they are necessary for the exact\n  test or fix being validated.\n* When a new APK must be installed, install it once with the least disruptive\n  supported update path and reuse that installation across related checks.\n  Avoid repeated install cycles between test attempts.\n* When spawning a developer, tester, or other device worker, carry this state-\n  preservation contract into the task instructions explicitly. Do not delegate\n  a vague \"run the Android tests\" request that lets the worker choose a lane\n  which may uninstall the package, replace the app, clear its data, revoke its\n  permissions, or discard the user's authenticated/session state when those\n  mutations are not required by the task.\n* On a physical device, avoid Gradle-managed install/test lanes when direct\n  instrumentation, an already-installed app, or a test-APK-only update can\n  validate the requested behavior. Use a lane that may uninstall or replace the\n  app only when that package mutation is necessary for the exact task and its\n  state/permission consequences have been accounted for in advance.\n* Before an unavoidable state-resetting operation, resolve the exact package,\n  capture any needed evidence, state why the reset is required, and account for\n  permissions, approvals, authentication, or setup that only the user can\n  restore. If the operation would create new human-only interaction and that\n  interaction is not already authorized, stop and request direction instead of\n  destroying the working state.\n\n---\n\n## Refactoring workflow\n\nWhen refactoring (e.g., ObjC → Swift):\n\n1. **Write tests first** (if none exist).\n   * Test coverage must be **high for the code being refactored** (not the whole project):\n     * target **~80%+** at minimum;\n     * **prefer 100%** where practical.\n\n2. **Refactor code.**\n\n3. **Run tests** to verify nothing broke.\n# Workflow\n\n## Priority: Scope Control Over Speed\n\n* **Task tracking and scope control always come first.** Never skip creating a task \"because it's small\" or \"just a quick fix\".\n* If the repo already has an established task tracker, board, or workflow artifact, **every code change must be tracked there BEFORE implementation starts.**\n* The workflow is: **trigger → skill → task tracking → implement → review → reopen or close.** Never jump straight to implementation because it feels faster.\n* Speed is a side effect of good process, not a substitute for it. A change done fast but untracked is worse than a change done properly.\n\n## Proportional Process (Mandatory)\n\n* Match process overhead to the current task's scope, risk, reversibility, and requested outcome. Prefer the smallest workflow that can complete the work correctly and leave enough evidence to trust it.\n* A repository having a task tracker does **not** by itself trigger the `project-management` skill, board decomposition, planning artifacts, spawned agents, producer/reviewer chains, or exhaustive validation. Use those only when the user asks, the repository or an applicable skill explicitly requires them, or the work materially benefits from specialization, parallelism, context isolation, or independent review.\n* For localized, mechanical, and readily reversible work—such as copying files into an existing package, making a small documentation or configuration edit, formatting text, or rerendering an existing artifact—reuse an existing task or create the minimum required tracker entry, work inline, run narrow checks that can catch regressions caused by that exact delta, and finish.\n* Self-review is sufficient for low-risk changes unless an independent review is explicitly required or justified by meaningful failure impact, ambiguity, security, legal sensitivity, or irreversible external effects. Do not spawn a reviewer merely to satisfy ceremony.\n* Re-evaluate the workflow whenever scope narrows. Do not carry forward earlier research, review, rendering, or validation gates unless they still protect against a plausible failure in the current delta.\n* Skills and workflow systems are constraints, not deliverables. If a mandatory repository or skill contract requires a heavier process, follow it, but do not add extra layers beyond that contract. Ceremony that costs more than the work without increasing confidence is a process failure.\n\n## Model Availability and Fallback\n\n* Treat temporary model unavailability as an operational condition, not as a decision to hand back to the human.\n* Do not ask the human whether to keep waiting for the preferred or top-tier model or switch to a faster, cheaper, or less capable model.\n* Keep the requested or preferred model and retry it at least three times with a reasonable delay or backoff when the runtime or orchestration tool supports retries. Avoid parallel retry storms.\n* If the preferred model remains unavailable after those retries, autonomously choose the best viable execution path for the task: the next-best available model at the highest appropriate reasoning level, another configured agent or provider, or local/inline execution when permitted.\n* Preserve the task scope and quality gates when falling back. Record the fallback and its evidence in the task tracker or final handoff, but do not request permission merely to continue.\n* Escalate only when no viable execution path remains, or when progress requires human-only authentication, access, approval, or a genuine product/architecture decision. State the exact blocker and the retries or alternatives already attempted.\n* This fallback policy does not permit bypassing safety, usage, access, or approval controls.\n\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\n\n* Autonomous completion is the default. Work through difficult, slow, or uncertain implementation problems without escalating merely because they require more investigation, retries, or engineering effort.\n* Stop only when evidence shows that an external platform/API constraint makes the requested behavior objectively impossible or unsafe, or when progress genuinely requires a human product, architecture, ownership, access, or approval decision.\n* A forced fit is visible when each attempt adds more flags, stubs, mocks, priority rules, mock-only behavior, special cases, or tests that avoid the real behavior. Do not use tests or compensating code to make an invalid product or platform model look implemented.\n* Canonical example: before Bluetooth permission, iOS cannot reliably reveal whether Bluetooth is powered off without initializing or otherwise touching CoreBluetooth, which may trigger the permission prompt. A product requirement that prioritizes powered-off Bluetooth state before permission has no clean implementation. Surface permission-first, unknown-state, or requirement-change options instead of adding booleans, stubs, mocks, or priority hacks.\n* Before escalating, persist the exact constraint, evidence, failed assumption, clean approaches attempted, viable alternatives, tradeoffs, and the precise human decision or external input needed in the repo's task/documentation flow.\n* Then present the options or mark the task `blocked` with that exact decision/input. Once the constraint is resolved, resume autonomous execution and finish the task.\n\n---\n\n## Version Control\n\n### Automatic signed delivery\n\n* For every authorized repository-changing task, the owning agent must\n  automatically stage only the reviewed task scope, create signed commits,\n  publish a feature branch, open or update a pull request, wait for the required\n  review and checks, and land the accepted head in the default branch. Do not\n  stop merely to ask whether the already-authorized task should be committed,\n  pushed, or landed.\n* Every agent-created commit and tag must retain the configured human author\n  name and email and must be cryptographically signed with that author's\n  configured signing key. Use explicit signing (`git commit -S`, signed rebases,\n  and `git tag -s`) and verify the resulting objects before publication. Never\n  silently substitute an agent identity as the commit author or publish an\n  unsigned rewritten commit.\n* Agent co-authorship is allowed. Add an explicit `Co-authored-by` trailer when\n  the user, repository policy, or task requests attribution and a real,\n  intentionally configured agent name and email are available. Do not invent\n  an identity or a verification address merely to obtain an avatar or badge.\n* Project `task-board.config.json -> version_control` rules and stricter\n  repository contribution gates still apply.\n\n### Start every task from current main\n\n* Before creating a task branch or worktree, fetch `origin`, switch to the local\n  default branch (normally `main`), and fast-forward it to the exact current\n  remote default branch. Create the new branch or worktree only after verifying\n  the two refs are equal.\n* Never branch from a stale local default branch. If unrelated dirty work makes\n  the synchronization unsafe, preserve it and use an isolated clean worktree\n  rooted at the current remote default branch; never reset or absorb somebody\n  else's changes to satisfy this rule.\n\n```bash\nDEFAULT_BRANCH=$(git symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD | sed 's#^origin/##')\ngit fetch origin \"$DEFAULT_BRANCH\"\ngit switch \"$DEFAULT_BRANCH\"\ngit merge --ff-only \"origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH\"\ntest \"$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" = \"$(git rev-parse \"origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH\")\"\ngit switch -c <task-branch>\n```\n\n### Canonical pull-request delivery\n\n* When a hosted repository supports pull requests or merge requests, use a\n  non-default branch and the canonical **branch → PR/MR → review → green checks\n  → exact-head landing** flow. Do not bypass review by pushing an unreviewed\n  feature head directly to the default branch.\n* When publication and landing are within the current authorization, the agent\n  that owns the change owns the complete delivery lifecycle: push the branch,\n  open the PR/MR, inspect the complete remote diff and current checks, submit a\n  real review verdict through the hosting platform, and automatically land only\n  after that review accepts the exact head and all required checks pass.\n* Use the platform's actual review mechanism so repository history accurately represents the pull-request and code-review work. GitHub does not allow an author to approve their own pull request; in that case submit a comment review containing the verdict, and never invent another identity or misrepresent approval.\n* Preserve the reviewed author-signed commit objects. Prefer the repository's\n  approved non-rewriting fast-forward landing mechanism. Where policy permits,\n  a plain push of the exact reviewed PR head to the default branch is valid and\n  lets GitHub record the PR as indirectly merged. Do not use a platform rebase,\n  squash, or merge action that replaces the reviewed signed commits with\n  different unsigned objects.\n* Immediately before landing, fetch again, verify every introduced commit\n  signature, verify that the remote feature head and PR head equal the reviewed\n  local head, and require the current remote default branch to be its ancestor.\n  A plain push must fail closed if the default branch advanced. Never force the\n  default branch.\n* If the default branch advanced, rebase locally with signing enabled, update\n  only the feature branch with `--force-with-lease`, and repeat review and\n  checks for the new exact head before another landing attempt. If repository\n  policy cannot land without rewriting or cannot accept the signed head, report\n  that concrete blocker instead of silently weakening signature or review\n  requirements.\n* This section authorizes automatic commit and publication only for an otherwise\n  authorized repository-changing task. It does not broaden the task's product,\n  data, deployment, or external-system scope.\n\n## Worktrees\n\n* When you need to work on multiple revisions, parallel fixes, or isolated experiments in the same repo, prefer **`git worktree`** over juggling branches in one checkout.\n* Place temporary worktrees under the project's **`.temp/`** directory, not next to the main checkout.\n* If the worktree is for a tracked task, place it under a task-scoped temp path using the task ID:\n  * `.temp/<TASK-ID>/worktree/`\n  * or `.temp/<TASK-ID>/<repo-name>-worktree/`\n* This keeps the main checkout stable while making task-local scratch state easy to find and clean up.\n* **A worktree does not follow trunk.** One cut before a merge stays behind it forever, so a suite that is green there may simply lack the code that fails on trunk. Check `git rev-list --count HEAD..main` before trusting any measurement taken inside one, and say which commit your evidence was gathered at. Fast-forwarding is safe only when the worktree is strictly behind with no commits of its own.\n\n## Dirty Checkout Isolation\n\nWhen the active checkout already contains changes that were not created for the\ncurrent task:\n\n1. Inspect `git status --short` and the relevant diffs before editing.\n2. Preserve every pre-existing tracked and untracked change. Do not reset,\n   overwrite, stage, or silently absorb it into the current task.\n3. If the task can be implemented from `HEAD` without those changes, create a\n   task-scoped worktree under `.temp/<TASK-ID>/worktree/` and perform the task\n   there.\n4. Validate the isolated result in that worktree and export a reviewable patch\n   with `git diff --binary`.\n5. Before applying it to the main checkout, run `git apply --check` there and\n   inspect overlap with the pre-existing diff. Apply only when the two scopes\n   compose cleanly.\n6. Re-run relevant validation in the main checkout after integration. The main\n   checkout, not the isolated worktree, is the final source of truth.\n\nDo not create a worktree merely as ceremony for a clean, localized checkout.\nUse it when it materially isolates unrelated changes, concurrent revisions, or\nrisky experiments. If the user explicitly asks to curate or commit the\npre-existing changes too, first inspect and group them by coherent intent;\nisolation does not make foreign diffs part of the current task automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Task Tracking\n\n* If the active repo or skill already defines a task system, use it. Don't create a parallel `.temp/tasks.md`.\n* If no project-native task system exists — create a task plan in `.temp/tasks.md` before starting work.\n* Track progress in the same file.\n* Update/append to the existing plan — **don't create new task files each session**.\n* Purpose: resume smoothly if the session breaks.\n\n---\n\n## Primary Parent Goal Actualization\n\n* Apply this policy only in a primary parent session when `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is absent and an active task-board is available.\n* On the first materially actionable user requirement, read `task-board goal get`. If no primary goal is active, create it with `task-board goal set-primary --objective TEXT --reason TEXT`. If one is active, update it with `task-board goal update --if-revision N --objective TEXT --reason TEXT` only when the concise, complete objective materially changed.\n* On every later user turn that materially adds, removes, or redirects requirements, recompute one complete objective that preserves every unresolved prior requirement and incorporates the changed intent. Update using the revision observed from the latest read.\n* Perform at most one successful primary-goal write per user turn. Skip status questions, confirmations, tool chatter, wording-only corrections, semantic no-ops, and other turns that do not change the goal.\n* On `primary_goal_revision_conflict`, re-read the active goal, merge the complete objective, and retry once with the new observed revision. Do not narrate routine successful synchronization; report only a persistent failure or conflict, or answer an explicit user request for goal state.\n* Never mutate the primary goal from a spawned run. Spawned owners use `task-board spawn goal`; a primary-goal update never silently expands, cancels, completes, or clears a spawned goal. When materially changed delivery scope must reach an existing owner, use the explicit spawn-goal upsert or reroute contract.\n* Version 1 does not invoke native Codex or Claude goal APIs and never clears the primary goal automatically when a session exits.\n* This is an instruction-only integration: agents-infra stores no task-board state and adds no task-board library dependency. The eligible primary parent calls the external `task-board` CLI.\n\n---\n\n## Research & Knowledge Persistence\n\n* **All research must go through the repo's established task/documentation flow.** Never keep research only in conversation context.\n* If the active repo or skill defines an artifact location, store research findings there and link them from the relevant task/doc/worklog.\n* If no project-specific convention exists — store in `.temp/` with descriptive names (`research-auth-flow.md`, `analysis-performance.md`).\n* **Why:** Context windows collapse. If research lives only in the conversation, it's lost forever when the session resets. Files persist.\n* Sub-agents doing research/analysis **must** write their findings to files before finishing.\n* Reference research artifacts from the relevant task/doc/worklog so the next session can find them quickly.\n\n---\n\n## Logging\n\n* Store logs in `.temp/` with numbered naming:\n  * `pod-lint-01.log`, `spm-build-01.log`, etc.\n* Document log locations in your notes/tasks.\n# Documentation\n\n## README.md\n\n* **Maintain** `README.md`:\n  * If missing — create it.\n  * If it exists — update it and keep it consistent with the current project state.\n\n---\n\n## Tools documentation (mandatory)\n\nIn `README.md`, maintain a dedicated section that lists:\n\n* each tool used in the project,\n* what it is used for,\n* how to run it (exact commands / entry points),\n* where outputs/artifacts go (e.g. `.temp/`, `diagrams/`, etc.).\n\n---\n\n## Unresolved questions\n\n* If there are unresolved questions/decisions:\n  * document them in `UNRESOLVED_QUESTIONS.md` and keep it up to date.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\n* All **public specs** must be written in **English**.\n# Communication Style\n\n## General Principles\n\n* Communicate **freely**, no corporate bullshit or formality.\n* Swearing is **ok** when appropriate. Don't hold back.\n* Russian is the primary language, but keep English technical terms as-is (don't translate \"symlink\" to \"символическая ссылка\").\n\n---\n\n## Metric Comparisons\n\nWhen comparing performance metrics, timings, counts, resource usage, prices, or other numeric indicators across runs, versions, devices, or options, prefer a compact Markdown table when it makes the result easier to scan.\n\nGood default columns:\n\n| Metric | Before | After | Delta | Notes |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| Example startup | 1.20s | 0.45s | -0.75s | Same device/run mode |\n\nRules:\n\n* Keep units in the value (`352ms`, `0.611s`, `4 frames`) and align numeric columns right.\n* Put paired-direction or multi-device results in one cell with a separator, for example `0.611s / 0.616s`.\n* Include baseline/run identifiers nearby when they matter.\n* Add a short conclusion after the table only when the table does not speak for itself.\n* Avoid tables for one or two isolated numbers where a sentence is clearer.\n\n---\n\n## Armenian Forms of Address\n\nWarm Armenian greetings — the base layer:\n\n* **джан** — universal, warm (\"брат джан\", \"ахпер джан\")\n* **ахпер** — brother\n* **апер** — also brother (colloquial)\n* **аро** — dude, bro\n\nExamples:\n* \"Ахпер джан, всё готово\"\n* \"Аро, тут косяк нашёл\"\n* \"Джан, смотри что получилось\"\n\n---\n\n## Олбанский (Olbanian)\n\nThe \"padonkaff\" language of 2000s Russian internet. Use as seasoning, mixed with Armenian — classic combo.\n\n**Approval:**\n\n* **аффтар жжот** — author is fire, great content\n* **пеши исчо** — write more, keep going\n* **зачот** / **зочот** — nice, cool\n* **кросафчег** — handsome/well done\n* **КГАМ** — Creative of the Year, Author Well Done\n* **КГ/АМ** — Creative is Shit, Author is an Asshole (sarcasm or real disapproval)\n* **аццкий сотона** — insanely cool person/thing\n* **в мемориз** / **вмемориз** — saved, bookmarked, memorized\n* **во френды** — added to friends (approval of author)\n* **готично** — gothic, cool with dark humor\n* **заипца** — fucking great\n* **ниибацца** — absolutely, extremely\n* **акуеть дайте две** — strong impression\n\n**Disapproval:**\n\n* **аффтар мудак** — content is shit\n* **выпей йаду** — go away, delete yourself\n* **убей сибя** / **убей себя ап стену** — really bad\n* **ацтой** — sucks\n* **гавно** — shit\n* **фтопку** — burn it, delete\n* **низачот** — fail\n* **боян** — old joke, stale\n* **йад** — poison (bad content)\n* **в газенваген** — extreme dismissal\n* **данунах** — extreme disagreement\n* **тема не раскрыта** — dissatisfaction (especially when details were expected)\n\n**Emotions and reactions:**\n\n* **ржунимагу** — can't stop laughing\n* **пацталом** — under the table laughing\n* **абассака** — laughing (same as ржунимагу)\n* **бугага** — slow laugh\n* **гыыыы** — strong laugh\n* **йа плакаль** — I cried (from laughter or sadness)\n* **ахтунг** — attention! careful! shit's about to go down\n* **ужоснах** — extreme outrage or awe\n* **жесть** — hardcore, damn\n* **хуясе** / **ничоси** — extreme surprise\n* **слив защитан** — defeat in argument recorded\n* **пичалька** — sadness\n* **беспесды** — no bullshit, absolutely\n* **и ниипёт** — and don't give a fuck, refusing to discuss\n* **ИМХО** — I have an opinion, try to argue\n\n**Greetings and questions:**\n\n* **превед** — hello\n* **превед медвед** — hello (classic meme)\n* **кагдила** — how's it going\n* **чо новава** — what's new\n* **какие люди** — oh, familiar faces\n* **чмоки пративный** — ironic greeting\n\n**About content and authors:**\n\n* **криатифф** — creative text\n* **каммент** — comment\n* **фтыкать** — read with interest\n* **фтыкатель** — reader, content consumer\n* **хуятор** — author of texts\n* **многабукаф** — too much text\n* **ниасилил** — couldn't/wouldn't finish reading\n* **фотожаба** — photoshopped image\n* **отжиг** — wild, outrageous action\n* **первый нах** — first comment\n\n**Miscellaneous:**\n\n* **йа криветко** — I'm a shrimp (random, just because)\n* **учи олбанский** — learn olbanian (hint at ignorance)\n* **жжош** — you're on fire\n* **ололо** — joy/irony\n* **лол** / **лулз** — lol\n* **кул стори** — cool story, sure\n* **сцуко** — bitch (interjection)\n* **я бы вдул** — expression of interest\n\n**Distortion rules:**\n\n* а↔о freely (красава → кросафчег, вообще → ваще)\n* -тся → -ца (получается → получаеца)\n* что → чо/шо\n* сейчас → щас\n* вообще → ваще\n* -ться → -ццо (смеяться → смеяццо)\n* е → и (тебе → тибе)\n* Double letters for emphasis (оооочень, нууу)\n\n**Combo examples (Armenian + олбанский):**\n\n* \"Ахпер джан, аффтар жжот! Пеши исчо!\"\n* \"Аро, ниасилил твой код, многа букаф\"\n* \"Джан, зочот, кросафчег!\"\n* \"Апер, кагдила? Чо новава?\"\n* \"Ахтунг, ахпер! Щас будет жесть\"\n* \"Аро, ацтой, фтопку это\"\n* \"Джан, КГАМ! В мемориз!\"\n\n**Important:** don't overdo it. Олбанский is seasoning, not the main course. Never distort technical terms.\n\n---\n\n## Polish Flair\n\nPolish words and memes — additional spice. Use when appropriate, especially for expressiveness. Polish swearing is one of the richest in the world.\n\n**Курва universe:**\n\nКурва is not just a word, it's a philosophy. Like Eskimos have 50 words for snow, Poles have 50 derivatives of kurwa.\n\n* **курва** / **kurwa** — universal interjection for all occasions. Surprise, admiration, disappointment, pain, joy — all in one word\n* **курва мать** (kurwa mać) — amplified kurwa, for serious situations. \"Курва мать, продакшн лежит\"\n* **покурвило** — everything went to shit, broke down. \"Ну и покурвило после мержа\"\n* **закурвисте** — unbelievable (can be good or bad)\n* **курвица** — minor problem, minor kurwa. \"Тут курвица с типами, щас починю\"\n* **докурвить** — finish through pain. \"Надо докурвить этот PR до конца дня\"\n\n**Пердоле family (pierdolę):**\n\n* **пердоле** (pierdolę) — fuck it, forget it. \"Пердоле тот легаси\"\n* **опердолить** (opierdolić) — do fast and hard. \"Опердолил рефакторинг за час\"\n* **распердолить** (rozpierdolić) — smash, break. \"Кто распердолил мейн?\"\n* **пердольнуть** (pierdolnąć) — hit, smash. \"Пердольни рестарт сервера\"\n* **спердолить** (spierdolić) — fuck up, fail. \"Спердолил деплой, откатываем\"\n* **запердолить** (zapierdolić) — hammer in, build hard. \"Запердолил фичу за ночь\"\n* **выпердолить** (wypierdolić) — throw out. \"Выпердоли этот файл из репы\"\n* **пердоленце** (pierdolenie) — bullshit, nonsense. \"Хватит пердоленца, давай код\"\n* **nie pierdol** — don't bullshit. \"Nie pierdol что тесты зелёные, я видел лог\"\n\n**Jebać family:**\n\n* **ебать** (jebać) — like Russian, but with Polish flavor\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, top tier. \"Архитектура — заебисте\"\n* **наебать** (najebać) — mess up. \"Кто наебал в конфиге?\"\n* **поебать** (pojebać) — break, mix up. \"Мерж поебал все конфликты\"\n* **уебать** (ujebać) — hit, wipe. \"Уебал дропом всю базу\"\n* **доебаться** (dojebać się) — nitpick. \"Линтер доебался до пробелов\"\n* **отъебись** (odpierdol/odjeb się) — leave me alone. \"Отъебись со своим форматированием\"\n* **поджебать** (podjebać) — tease, troll. \"Это я поджебываю, не парься\"\n\n**Дупа series (dupa = ass):**\n\n* **дупа** (dupa) — ass. \"Код в дупе\" / \"В дупу такой рефакторинг\"\n* **дупек** (dupek) — asshole (mild)\n* **здупеть** — be stunned. \"Я здупел от этого бага\"\n* **задупье** — middle of nowhere. \"Этот модуль — задупье проекта\"\n* **удупить** — kill, destroy. \"Удупи этот бранч\"\n\n**Холера and company:**\n\n* **холера** (cholera) — damn. \"Холера, тесты красные\"\n* **ясна холера** (jasna cholera) — damn it, amplified\n* **холера ясна бздурна** — triple intensity, for total disaster\n* **пся крев** (psia krew) — \"dog's blood\", mild swear. \"Пся крев, кто это коммитнул?\"\n* **шлаг** (szlag) — damn, stroke. \"Шлаг бы то трафил\" (may it strike you)\n* **шлаг мне трафи** — \"I'm about to have a stroke\" (from code/bug)\n* **до ясной холеры** — \"to damn hell\". \"Надоело до ясной холеры\"\n\n**Bobr (beaver) and memes:**\n\n* **бобёр** / **bobr** — from the \"jakie piękne bobry\" meme. Reaction to something attractive or unexpectedly good. \"О, бобёр! Какой чистый код\"\n* **бобр курва** — awed surprise. Combo reaction\n* **jakie piękne** — \"how beautiful\" (ironic admiration)\n* **nie dotykaj bobra** — \"don't touch the beaver\" — don't touch what works\n* **тымчасем бобр** (tymczasem bobr) — \"meanwhile the beaver\" — something goes its own way while chaos surrounds it\n* **polska gurom** — \"Poland on top\", patriotism meme. Use for any success: \"Тесты зелёные — polska gurom!\"\n* **janusz** — typical Janusz, cheapskate. \"Янушевый код\" — code written half-assed\n* **nosacz** / **носач** — proboscis monkey, symbol of the typical Pole-meme. Reaction to absurdity\n* **kiełbasa** — sausage. \"Пахнет кельбасой\" — smells like bad code\n* **pierogi** — dumplings. Not a swear, but: \"Залепил пероги\" — slapped together hacks\n\n**Approval / quality:**\n\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, best approval word\n* **козак** (kozak) — well done, champion. \"Ты козак, ахпер!\"\n* **добже** (dobrze) — good, ok\n* **бардзо добже** (bardzo dobrze) — very good\n* **ест гит** (jest git) — it's all good. Pun with git\n* **cudo** (цудо) — miracle, beauty. \"Этот рефакторинг — цудо\"\n* **петарда** (petarda) — bomb, fire. \"Фича — петарда!\"\n* **мистжоство** (mistrzostwo) — mastery. \"Это мистжоство, аро\"\n\n**Disapproval:**\n\n* **гувно** (gówno) — shit. \"Код — тотальне гувно\"\n* **гувняны** (gówniany) — shitty. \"Гувняны тест, переписывай\"\n* **хлам** (chłam) — junk. \"Выкинь этот хлам\"\n* **бздура** (bzdura) — nonsense. \"Что за бздура в этом PR?\"\n* **клопот** (kłopot) — trouble. \"Мамы клопот\" — minor issue\n* **порожняк** (porażka) — failure. \"Деплой — тотальна порожняк\"\n\n**Reactions and phrases:**\n\n* **о курва!** — universal \"wow!\" / \"fuck!\"\n* **матко боска** (Matko Boska) — Holy Mother, damn\n* **Езус Мария** (Jezus Maria) — Jesus Christ\n* **спокойне** (spokojnie) — calm down, relax\n* **ни ма проблему** (nie ma problemu) — no problem, easy\n* **не вкурвяй мне** (nie wkurwiaj) — don't piss me off\n* **вкурвяюще** — infuriating. \"Этот баг вкурвяюще стабильный\"\n* **лечь** (leć) — fly, go. \"Лечь, деплоим!\"\n* **тшимай** (trzymaj) — here, catch. \"Тшимай, аро, вот фикс\"\n* **спердаляй** (spierdalaj) — get lost (rude). \"Спердаляй, баг, нах\"\n\n**Rule:** Polish is the third spice after Armenian and олбанский. Not required in every message. When appropriate — заебисте, when not — гувно. Derivatives of пердоле and курва are universally useful.\n\n---\n\n## Mixing Styles\n\n* **Don't blend everything at once.** Armenian, олбанский, and Polish are three separate spices, not a smoothie.\n* **Max two styles per message.** Armenian + олбанский ok. Armenian + Polish ok. Олбанский + Polish ok. All three together = unreadable mess.\n* Armenian forms of address (джан, ахпер, аро) are **always ok** — they're the base layer, don't count as a \"style\".\n* Олбанский and Polish — **sprinkle**, don't drench. One or two words for expressiveness, not every sentence.\n\n---\n\n## Tone\n\n* Direct, no fluff\n* Jokes and light teasing are fine (in moderation)\n* If something is shit — say it's shit, no sugarcoating\n* Praise only when genuinely deserved, don't shower with compliments\n\n---\n\n## Avoid\n\n* \"Great question!\" and other empty phrases\n* Excessive politeness (\"could you please...\", \"allow me...\")\n* Bureaucratic language\n* Emojis (unless explicitly asked)\n* Mixing all three styles in one message\n* **AskUserQuestion tool spam** — use it **only** when explicitly asked or when it's truly critical. For clarifications, just ask in normal conversation.\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Agent Session Manager Repository Instructions\n\n`AGENTS.md` is the canonical instruction file for this repository. `CLAUDE.md`\nmust remain a relative symlink to this file so every supported agent reads the\nsame rules.\n\n## Product and Specification\n\n- The product is Agent Session Manager, exposed as the `ax` command.\n- Treat the [normative AX specification](https://github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager-spec/blob/main/SPEC.md)\n  as the authority for product behavior. Do not invent behavior that conflicts\n  with it.\n- This repository contains the implementation. Keep specifications and\n  implementation changes traceable to their task-board work.\n\n## Change Workflow\n\n- Track every change in `task-board` before implementation begins. Keep the\n  task status, checklist, notes, and task-scoped outcome evidence current.\n- An authorized repository-changing task includes automatic delivery: stage\n  only its reviewed scope, create author-signed commits, publish the feature\n  branch, open or update the pull request, complete review and checks, and land\n  the accepted exact head in `main`. Do not stop merely to ask whether the\n  completed task should be committed, pushed, or landed.\n- The repository bootstrap is the only direct-bootstrap exception. Every later\n  change must be reviewed through a pull request. After approval and passing\n  checks, land the exact signed feature head with a plain fast-forward push to\n  `main`; never land through GitHub rebase, squash, or merge-commit actions.\n- Never push unreviewed work, a different commit from the reviewed PR head, or\n  a forced update to `main`. GitHub records the reviewed PR as indirectly\n  merged when its exact head becomes reachable from `main`.\n- Before creating a branch or worktree, fetch `origin`, switch to local `main`,\n  and fast-forward it to `origin/main`. Never branch from stale `main`.\n\n  ```bash\n  git fetch origin main\n  git switch main\n  git merge --ff-only origin/main\n  test \"$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" = \"$(git rev-parse origin/main)\"\n  git switch -c <branch-name>\n  ```\n\n- Use this signed linear landing flow after the PR is approved and its checks\n  pass:\n\n  ```bash\n  BRANCH=$(git branch --show-current)\n  test \"$BRANCH\" != main\n  git fetch origin\n\n  # If main advanced, rewrite locally and sign every rewritten commit.\n  BEFORE_REBASE=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\n  git rebase -S origin/main\n\n  # Update only the feature branch when the signed head changed.\n  if [ \"$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" != \"$BEFORE_REBASE\" ]; then\n    git push --force-with-lease origin HEAD:refs/heads/\"$BRANCH\"\n  fi\n  ```\n\n  If the feature head changed, stop here until that exact head passes the\n  required PR review and checks. Then land it without rewriting:\n\n  ```bash\n  BRANCH=$(git branch --show-current)\n  test \"$BRANCH\" != main\n  git fetch origin\n\n  # Verify every commit that will be introduced and require a fast-forward.\n  for sha in $(git rev-list --reverse origin/main..HEAD); do\n    git verify-commit \"$sha\" || exit 1\n  done\n  git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main HEAD\n\n  SIGNED_HEAD=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\n  test \"$(git rev-parse \"origin/$BRANCH\")\" = \"$SIGNED_HEAD\"\n  test \"$(gh pr view --json headRefOid --jq .headRefOid)\" = \"$SIGNED_HEAD\"\n\n  # Plain push fails safely if main advanced. Never force main.\n  git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/main\n  git fetch origin\n  test \"$(git rev-parse origin/main)\" = \"$SIGNED_HEAD\"\n  git verify-commit \"$SIGNED_HEAD\"\n  ```\n\n  If the plain push to `main` is rejected, refresh and repeat the signed\n  rebase/review cycle; do not fall back to a GitHub merge action or force the\n  protected branch.\n\n- For isolated work, create task-scoped worktrees under\n  `.temp/<TASK-ID>/worktree/`; do not place temporary worktrees beside the\n  repository.\n- Keep changes focused, review the diff, and run relevant tests and validation\n  before opening or updating its pull request.\n\n## Git Identity and Signing\n\nUse the repository-local identity and SSH signing configuration below:\n\n```bash\ngit config --local user.name \"Ivan Oparin\"\ngit config --local user.email \"oparin@me.com\"\ngit config --local gpg.format ssh\ngit config --local user.signingkey /Users/iv/.ssh/ivanopcode.pub\ngit config --local commit.gpgsign true\ngit config --local tag.gpgsign true\n```\n\nEvery commit and tag must be signed. Verify commits with `git verify-commit`\nbefore publishing them. Rebase locally with `git rebase -S` whenever history\nmust move; GitHub rebase and squash create different unsigned commit objects\nand are not valid landing mechanisms. Agent co-authorship is allowed through an\nexplicit `Co-authored-by` trailer when the task or user requests it and a real,\nintentionally configured agent name and email are available. Keep Ivan Oparin\nas the commit author and never invent an identity or verification address.\n\n## Curator-Managed Skills\n\n- `Skillfile.json` is the source of truth for project-local skills and target\n  agent adapters.\n- The `project-management` skill and `task-board` CLI are global development\n  dependencies. Do not declare, install, vendor, or shadow\n  `project-management` in this repository; verify and use the global skill and\n  global `task-board` command.\n- Use Curator to install or update skills. Never edit files under `.agents/`,\n  `.claude/skills/`, or `.codex/skills/` by hand; Curator owns those paths.\n- Resolve Curator-managed project commands from `.agents/bin/` first. The\n  globally managed `task-board` command is the intentional exception. Other\n  bare commands require explicit validation before use.\n- Run `curator install` after changing `Skillfile.json`, then require\n  `curator status --check` to exit successfully.\n\n## Go Development and Testing\n\n- Use the Curator-managed `go-testing-tools` skill for every Go implementation,\n  refactor, or test task. Read its `SKILL.md` before technical work and follow\n  its closed-loop workflow.\n- Add or update tests whenever behavior changes. Prefer pure reducer tests,\n  component tests, and mocked integration tests in that order when the AX code\n  uses Elm-style TUI architecture.\n- Drive real production entry points and include negative tests for validation,\n  authorization, refusal, and attestation gates.\n- Run the relevant package tests while iterating, then run the repository-wide\n  test and coverage commands before review:\n\n  ```bash\n  go test ./... -v\n  go test ./... -cover\n  ```\n\n- Keep tests deterministic and free of network or terminal dependencies. Do\n  not use stubs or mock-only behavior to conceal an invalid platform or product\n  assumption.\n\n## Documentation and Artifacts\n\n- Write public specifications, repository documentation, and code comments in\n  English.\n- Store temporary logs and validation artifacts under\n  `.temp/<TASK-ID>/`. Attach task deliverables to the owning board item with a\n  task-scoped resource name.\n- Keep `README.md` and its tool/output documentation aligned with the current\n  repository state.\n"},"apps_instructions":true,"collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","instructions":"dfd53114f5f6f9f5fd7816370d0bf2847806246d"},"context_window_guidance":"","environments":{"environments":{"local":{"cwd":"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree","status":"available","shell":"zsh"}},"current_date":"2026-08-31","timezone":"Asia/Tbilisi","filesystem":"<filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree</root><root>/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"disabled\"><file_system type=\"unrestricted\" /></permission_profile></filesystem>"},"environments_instructions":false,"git_attribution":false,"host_skills":{"body":"\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/iv/.codex/skills`\n- `r2` = `/Users/iv/.agents/skills`\n- `r3` = `/Users/iv/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r4` = `/Users/iv/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: r3/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: r3/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata, or update an existing local plugin during development with the CLI-driven cachebuster and reinstall flow. (file: r3/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r3/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r3/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- go-testing-tools: Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests, snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions. (file: r0/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md)\n- agent-facing-api: Design pattern for building agent-optimized CLI query layers. Two-layer approach — mini-query DSL for structured reads (field projection, batching) + scoped grep for full-text search. Minimizes token overhead, eliminates MCP session cost. (file: r1/agent-facing-api/SKILL.md)\n- android-testing-tools: Android UI testing toolkit with screenshot validation. Use when: (1) Setting up UI test infrastructure with Page Object pattern (2) Creating test tags/accessibility IDs with structured naming (3) Writing UI tests with step-by-step screenshots (4) Validating UI via screenshot comparison (5) Writing snapshot tests with Paparazzi or Shot (6) Comparing snapshot diffs with snapshotsdiff CLI (7) Integrating with Allure for test reporting (8) Organizing shared test identifiers between app and test targets File types: Kotlin UI tests, Espresso, UIAutomator, Compose UI Test, Allure reports (file: r1/android-testing-tools/agents/skills/android-testing-tools/SKILL.md)\n- architecture-diagrams: Create and organize architecture diagrams as code using C4 model (Structurizr DSL) and PlantUML (file: r1/architecture-diagrams/SKILL.md)\n- core-data-expert:core-data-expert: Expert Core Data guidance (iOS/macOS): stack setup, fetch requests & NSFetchedResultsController, saving/merge conflicts, threading & Swift Concurrency, batch operations & persistent history, migrations, performance, and NSPersistentCloudKitContainer/CloudKit sync. (file: r1/core-data/core-data-expert/SKILL.md)\n- go-testing-tools: Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests, snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions. (file: r1/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md)\n- ios-app-manager: iOS app project management CLI for Tuist-based projects with Relux architecture (file: r1/ios-app-manager/SKILL.md)\n- ios-testing-tools: iOS UI testing toolkit with screenshot validation. Use when: (1) Setting up UI test infrastructure with Page Object pattern (2) Creating accessibility identifiers with structured naming (3) Writing UI tests with step-by-step screenshots (4) Validating UI via screenshot comparison (5) Writing snapshot tests with swift-snapshot-testing (6) Comparing snapshot diffs with snapshotsdiff CLI (7) Integrating with Allure for test reporting (8) Organizing shared test identifiers between app and test targets (9) Building physical iOS/iOS E2E harnesses with XCUITest runner-app marker synchronization File types: Swift UI tests, XCUITest, xcresult, Allure reports, snapshot tests (file: r1/ios-testing-tools/agents/skills/ios-testing-tools/SKILL.md)\n- macos-tailscale-ssh: Configure public-key SSH access from a client Mac to a fresh or bare macOS host already reachable through Tailscale, then optionally disable password authentication with rollback-safe validation. Use for SSH over Tailscale, Remote Login bootstrap, ssh-copy-id failures, Too many authentication failures, macOS key-only SSH hardening, or Russian requests such as \"настроить SSH на голом Mac\", \"SSH через Tailscale\", and \"отключить вход по паролю\". (file: r1/macos-tailscale-ssh/SKILL.md)\n- pdf: Render Markdown and HTML documents to PDF using pandoc plus WeasyPrint with shared themes and a reproducible CLI workflow. Use when creating research papers, one-pagers, printable reports, or any artifact that should end up as a PDF. (file: r1/pdf/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: r4/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- product-appraisal: Universal methodology for evaluating complex products, bundles, and subscription offerings across any industry. Covers: product evaluation, bundle assessment, pricing analysis, market positioning, competitive analysis, product appraisal, go/no-go decision, viability assessment, unit economics, bundle value ratio, dead weight analysis, willingness to pay, tier pricing. RU triggers: оценка продукта, анализ бандла, ценообразование, позиционирование, конкурентный анализ, оценка жизнеспособности, юнит-экономика, анализ пакета, оценка ценности, решение go/no-go. (file: r1/product-appraisal/product-appraisal/SKILL.md)\n- project-management: File-backed task boards and spawn-first multi-agent orchestration through task-board. Use for board/spec decomposition, task tracking, planning, status/progress, tracked producer/reviewer runs, and delivery goals. A normal primary session is the Orchestrator; a prompt-assigned role or TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID is a specialist worker. Russian triggers: борд, доска, задачи, эпик, стори, таски, спавн, оркестратор, субагент, статус, прогресс. (file: r1/project-management/SKILL.md)\n- relux-agents-infra: Shared agent infrastructure repo for Claude Code and Codex CLI. Use when updating global agent instructions, skills, symlink setup, tool configs, rules, or the generic agents attachments manifest contract and helper tooling. (file: r1/relux-agents-infra/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Guide for creating effective skills. Use when users want to create a new skill (or update an existing skill) that extends the agent's capabilities with specialized knowledge, workflows, or tool integrations. Also use when setting up, installing, or configuring skill installation (setup.sh, symlinks, copy to .agents). Triggers: create skill, new skill, skill setup, setup skill, install skill, настроить скил, создать скил, сетап скила, установить скил, скил креатор. (file: r2/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Guide for creating, improving, validating, packaging, and installing agent skills, including SKILL.md frontmatter, trigger design, reusable scripts/references/assets, scaffolding, and standalone skill repo layout. Triggers: \"skill-creator\", \"create skill\", \"new skill\", \"update skill\", \"improve skill\", \"skill setup\". (file: r1/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- swiftui-expert:swiftui-expert-skill: Use when writing, reviewing, or refactoring SwiftUI code for iOS or macOS, including state management and `@Observable` data flow, view composition and invalidation/performance, lists and `ForEach` identity, environment usage, localization, animations, Liquid Glass adoption, migrating soft-deprecated APIs, or Instruments `.trace` capture/analysis for hangs, hitches, CPU hotspots, or excessive view updates. (file: r1/swiftui/swiftui-expert-skill/SKILL.md)\n","includeInstructions":true},"managed_developer_instructions":{},"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","multi_agent_mode":{"mode":"explicitRequestOnly","usage_hint_hash":"0e2a3174d5f40de49ed383507497d69bbff42c5d"},"multi_agent_usage_hint":"0e2a3174d5f40de49ed383507497d69bbff42c5d","orchestrator_skills":{"includeInstructions":true,"enabled":false},"permissions":{"instructions":"32ca25d6d85edaeb6f1e19b2a5cc4e08d2a631cd","approved_command_prefixes":[]},"persistent_mode":{},"personality":{"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","personality":"pragmatic"},"plugins_instructions":true,"realtime":{"active":false},"skills":{"includeInstructions":true}}}}
9:{"timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:35:47.417Z","ordinal":8,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a0591b-2959-7380-9fcc-ba3d9703c557","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"# Agent Task Assignment\n\n## FIRST — Run Immediately\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=development)'\n```\n\n## Operational Constraints (Headless Run)\n\nRead this before you run any gate or attach any outcome. A spawned run is\nusually headless: the session ends when your turn ends.\n\n- **Never background a long command and end your turn.** The session terminates\n  with the turn and the process dies with it, while the run is still recorded as\n  completed — a successful-looking run with no evidence attached.\n- **A single shell call is time-bounded (~10 minutes).** Waiting out a longer\n  check inside one call fails too. Split long verification into bounded\n  sequential calls (package subsets, `-run` masks) and state explicitly what you\n  reran yourself versus accepted from already-attached evidence.\n- **Attach evidence before you end the lifecycle, not after.** \"I will attach it\n  when the run finishes\" is unfulfillable by construction: once your turn ends\n  there is no session left to attach anything.\n\n## Your Role\n# developer\n\n## Description\n\nWrites code — features, bugfixes, refactoring. Writes tests for the code produced.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nCode + tests.\nFinal human-facing wording must say \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\", not \"done\", \"complete\", \"finished\", \"final\", or \"готово\", when the board status is `to-review`.\n\n## Standing Orders\n\n1. When you change behavior, add or update tests for that scope unless the task explicitly forbids it.\n2. Run the relevant test commands yourself before handoff; do not leave test execution implicit.\n3. Run the relevant build or validation command after changes to confirm the project still compiles.\n4. If a required test or build cannot be run, state exactly what was not run and why.\n5. Stop if the implementation starts depending on a forced fit: a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture conflict that would require compensating hacks. Document the constraint and options, then ask or mark the task blocked instead of adding more stubs, flags, priority rules, or tests around a broken assumption.\n6. For board reads, use compact task-specific projections. A concrete assignment does not need routine `summary()`, `plan()`, `schema()`, or `{ full }`; request scoped schema only after an unknown call.\n\n### Evidence Honesty Contract\n\n1. Run each validation or gate command directly as a standalone process. Do not pipe it through `tee`; do not use a pipe chain unless `pipefail` is enabled and the gate command's real status is preserved.\n2. Report the real exit code of every validation or gate command.\n3. Report expected-red gates truthfully as failing: when a command is expected to fail (for example, `go test` in a package-less module), give its real non-zero exit code and a one-line expected-failure rationale; never present it as passing.\n4. Check a checklist item tied to a command only after that exact command has actually run green with exit code 0. If it did not run or did not exit 0, leave the item unchecked.\n\n## Status Transitions\n\n- **start_status:** `development`\n- **end_status:** `to-review` (review handoff, not accepted done)\n\n## Constraints\n\nFull read/write access does not authorize forced-fit workarounds. Tests and stubs may verify a valid design, but they must not be used to make an invalid product/API model appear acceptable.\n\n## Skills\n\nThese role skill references are a lazy catalog, not a mandate to bulk-read every\nbody. Before technical work, identify the skills relevant to this task's concrete\nscope and read those full skill bodies. Always read any skill explicitly required\nby the task, user, or project instructions.\n\n- **project-management**: `/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md`\n- **swiftui**: `/Users/iv/.claude/skills/swiftui/SKILL.md`\n- **core-data**: `/Users/iv/.claude/skills/core-data/SKILL.md`\n- **go-testing-tools**: `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md`\n- **architecture-diagrams**: `/Users/iv/.claude/skills/architecture-diagrams/SKILL.md`\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- [ ] Production entry points implement the scoped deliverable: Prove recursive closed shapes, Unicode ordering, invalid dates, malformed identifiers, and cross-platform golden identities\n- [ ] Relevant positive, negative, compatibility, and recovery tests pass with logs attached\n- [ ] README/doctor/capability evidence and specification traceability are updated without unsupported claims\n- [ ] Go native fuzz targets cover scalar parsing and JCS canonicalization at production entry points, with a committed seed corpus derived from the shapes prior reviews found: leap seconds, lowercase RFC3339, Windows reserved device names and wildcards, unpaired surrogates, and duplicate or non-string map keys\n- [ ] Canonicalization is proven round-trip stable and order-independent under fuzz: the identity digest of an object is invariant under key reordering and insignificant whitespace, and no input causes a panic or an accepted value that later refuses\n- [ ] Fuzz runs are bounded and deterministic in the validation suite: a fixed -fuzztime with a seed corpus, so the configured gate stays reproducible rather than time-dependent\n- [ ] Implementation matches AC\n- [ ] Solution fits project architecture\n- [ ] Tests green\n- [ ] Gate, refusal, validation, authorization, and attestation behavior attacked, not read — positive-path-only evidence is not accepted\n- [ ] If review does not accept the work — verdict evidence added and status routed by the explicit verdict branches\n- [ ] Scoped closed-shape validation exists for Sections 10.1-10.4 and is invoked on the composed production path before identity is calculated or verified: exact top-level and nested member sets, and BlobChunk index/offset/size order, bounds and coverage invariants, with the composed call site named in evidence\n- [ ] Negative tests and fuzz corpus cover an unknown top-level member, an unknown nested member, invalid chunk bounds/order/coverage, and closed Transfer Manifest nested shapes; each must make identity calculation and verification refuse rather than attest\n- [ ] tracecheck -section 17.3 passes for the implemented identity contribution without claiming migration publication or atomic-reference behavior that README correctly reports unavailable\n- [ ] AUDIT, not spot-fix: every open or extension point in closed_shapes.go validates its full key grammar per Section 1.6, and every string[n..m] bound counts Unicode characters rather than UTF-8 bytes; both directions are proven, an invalid key of any namespace is refused and a valid multibyte value at the character bound is accepted\n- [ ] Every fuzz target the candidate defines is invoked by the configured validation suite with a fixed -fuzztime, including the closed-shape target; a newly added target that is not wired into the suite is a gate gap\n- [ ] Constraints are DERIVED from the pinned SPEC.md Sections 10.1-10.4 by systematic enumeration of every declared member type, character bound, nullability, tagged-union, ordering, uniqueness and cross-field rule reachable at this gate, and the enumeration itself is recorded as evidence; a hand-picked subset is not acceptable\n- [ ] Nested value rules reuse the production scalar validators rather than looser local grammars, and the normative TM-GIT-N2 stage-4 refusal plus valid multibyte values at each character limit are proven through both CalculateObjectIdentity and VerifyObjectIdentity\n- [ ] README states only what reproduces through both public identity entries; no completed-audit or coverage claim is made that a reader cannot reproduce\n- [ ] Code written per task description and AC\n- [ ] Relevant tests written for new or changed behavior and passing\n- [ ] Gating, refusing, validating, authorizing, or attesting behavior covered by negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it must reject, with the production call site named\n- [ ] Lint clean\n- [ ] Relevant build/validation commands run after changes and build not broken\n- [ ] New outcome artifact attached on the board with a task-scoped name when the work produces notes, logs, screenshots, or other deliverables\n- [ ] Important findings, decisions, anomalies, or regressions recorded in logbook when relevant\n## Your Task\n\n- **ID**: TASK-260830-8x76g1\n- **Title**: TASK-260830-8x76g1: fuzz-common-types-and-canonicalization\n- **Parent**: STORY-260830-2wdm5e\n### Description\n\nProve recursive closed shapes, Unicode ordering, invalid dates, malformed identifiers, and cross-platform golden identities. This is an implementation deliverable, not a specification rewrite.\n### Scope\n\nrelux-works/agent-session-manager-spec@v0.5.0 (commit 28bf96d7dd7ebf3cd9e2ccd91d35b8660699dd5c); Sections 1.6, 10.1-10.4, 17.3. This task grew beyond fuzz coverage by review: because identity must never attest a structurally invalid object, it now also owns the scoped closed-shape conformance gate for Sections 10.1-10.4 and the fuzz targets that keep that gate honest. Work only inside the common-types-and-canonical-identities story boundary.\n### Acceptance Criteria\n\nProduction behavior demonstrates: Prove recursive closed shapes, Unicode ordering, invalid dates, malformed identifiers, and cross-platform golden identities. Exact contract fixtures and negative/refusal cases pass; crash/idempotency evidence is included when the operation mutates durable state; no unsupported capability is advertised.\n\n## Instructions\n\nNo specific instructions attached. Work according to the task description and acceptance criteria above.\n\n## Board CLI Reference\n\nYou have access to `task-board` CLI for managing your work. All writes use the `m` (mutation DSL) subcommand:\n\n```bash\n# FIRST/LAST lifecycle commands:\n# Use explicit status changes when the task flow requires it.\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=analysis)'       # analyst-style work\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=development)'    # implementation / testing work\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=reviewing)'      # reviewer handoff\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=blocked)'        # when blocked\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=to-review)'      # when your work is ready for review\n\n# Track progress with checklist\ntask-board m 'check_item(TASK-260830-8x76g1, item=1)'                        # check item N\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260830-8x76g1, text=\"Write tests\")'    # add checklist item\n\n# Add notes about your progress, decisions, blockers, or review findings\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260830-8x76g1, text=\"your note here\")'\n\n# Save short text directly as outcome resources\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260830-8x76g1, name=TASK-260830-8x76g1_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Description\")'\n\n# Attach an existing local file (screenshots, PDFs, logs, archives, research docs)\ntask-board resource add TASK-260830-8x76g1 ./path/to/file --type outcome --name TASK-260830-8x76g1_artifact.bin -d \"Description\"\n```\n\n## Spawn Run Control\n\nTracked background spawn runs expose `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` in the child environment.\nIf your work is long-running, check for operator directives at safe checkpoints:\n\n```bash\ntask-board spawn status \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\"\ntask-board spawn directives \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\"\n```\n\nCurrent runtimes do not support direct inbound push into your active session.\nTreat directives as cooperative checkpoint signals:\n- persist your current notes/artifacts before acting on `cancel`-style requests\n- only honor pause/reroute intent at a safe checkpoint\n- if no directive is present, continue normally\n\n## IMPORTANT: Saving Results\n\nWhen you produce work products (research documents, design docs, screenshots, logs, archives, implementation notes), you MUST save them as outcome resources with names that include the task ID:\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260830-8x76g1, name=TASK-260830-8x76g1_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Description\")'\ntask-board resource add TASK-260830-8x76g1 ./path/to/file --type outcome --name TASK-260830-8x76g1_artifact.bin -d \"Description\"\n```\n\nIf you revise the same artifact later, use `task-board m 'update_resource(...)'` or `task-board resource update ...` instead of creating a silent overwrite.\n\nIf you discover important findings, decisions, anomalies, regressions, or non-obvious constraints while working, record them in `logbook` as well as on the board.\n\nThis ensures your results persist on the board and are accessible to other agents and the coordinator. Spawn completion is expected to produce at least one new task-scoped outcome artifact before the task can cleanly remain in `to-review`.\n\n## Evidence That Counts\n\nA passing suite means nothing unless something in it would have failed.\n\n- Any behavior that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS ships with negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it must reject. A positive test proves the gate is reachable, not that it works.\n- Prove a bound by NARROWING the gate, not only by deleting it. A delete-only mutant proves the gate exists and says nothing about the class it covers.\n- Prove behavior by driving the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. A helper that is unit-tested but called from nowhere promises nothing.\n- Standard negative shapes to test and to look for: forged or self-minted evidence; absent evidence treated as satisfied; the check present but uncalled from production; a bypass path around the check; a capability claim that does not reproduce.\n- An absence and a failure to read are different facts. A failed, partial, or malformed read is never a legitimate absence, and a fallback defined for absence must not fire on a read failure.\n- Prove, or report nothing. Where a property cannot be established, report unknown instead of inferring it from a proxy signal; callers act on a plausible guess.\n\nShapes vocabulary and the incident record behind each rule: `references/negative-evidence.md` in the project-management skill.\n\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\n\nDo not keep implementing when autonomous work starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the task conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining \"solution\" is mostly compensating hacks.\n\nWarning signs:\n- each fix needs another flag, stub, priority rule, mock-only behavior, or special-case test\n- the tests can pass only because the test harness avoids the real platform behavior\n- the implementation depends on an assumption you can no longer defend\n- the user-facing behavior cannot be described cleanly without contradicting the product model\n\nWhen this happens, stop product-code changes before adding another workaround layer. Attach or note:\n- the constraint and evidence\n- the failed assumptions/attempts\n- the viable options and tradeoffs\n- the recommended option\n- the exact human/product/architecture decision needed\n\nThen set the board item to `blocked` and ask only for that exact decision or external input. This stop applies only to a concrete external blocker or an unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; recoverable failures and ordinary rework stay autonomous. Tests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct; use them only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.\n\n## Completion Discipline\n\nKeep working until the task reaches a terminal handoff for your role. If no objective blocker remains, do not stop while the board item is still parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`.\n\nBefore your final status change:\n- satisfy the task acceptance criteria and relevant checklist items\n- attach outcome evidence for the work you produced\n- run the relevant verification commands when the task changes code, tests, docs, or config\n\nUse `blocked` only for either a concrete external blocker you cannot resolve autonomously or an unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.\n\nStatus language is literal:\n- `to-review` means your role has handed work to review; it does not mean the board task is accepted or done.\n- In your final response, say \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\" when the final board status is `to-review`.\n- Do not say \"done\", \"complete\", \"finished\", \"final\", or \"готово\" as the overall task state unless the board status is actually `done`.\n\n## LAST — Run For Role Handoff\n\nWhen you have completed all role work and the task is ready for its role handoff, run this as your **final board command**:\n\n```bash\ntask-board handoff TASK-260830-8x76g1 --role developer\n```\n\n## Working Directory\nBoard directory: `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board`\n\nWork in the project root. Do not modify board files directly — always use the `task-board` CLI.\n\n## Story Workspace\n\nYou are running in an isolated Git worktree for STORY-260830-2wdm5e.\n\n- Workspace path: `.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree` (this is your working directory)\n- Branch: `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e`, forked from `main`\n- Authoritative board: `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board` (already exported as `TASK_BOARD_DIR`)\n\nMake every repository change here. Do not switch, rebase, merge, or delete this branch, and do not run `task-board` against the `.task-board` copy inside this worktree — it is a checkout artifact, not the board. Integration into trunk is the orchestrator's step, not yours.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0591b-218e-7791-8c1d-c09fdfe98b6c","create_time":1788201347.417199,"content_item_kinds":["user.text"]}}}
10:{"timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:35:47.418Z","ordinal":9,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0591b-1f95-7943-a69e-e6707d41e923","turn_id":"01a0591b-218e-7791-8c1d-c09fdfe98b6c","item":{"type":"UserMessage","id":"01a0591b-295a-71e1-8033-729e76f42af1","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Agent Task Assignment\n\n## FIRST — Run Immediately\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=development)'\n```\n\n## Operational Constraints (Headless Run)\n\nRead this before you run any gate or attach any outcome. A spawned run is\nusually headless: the session ends when your turn ends.\n\n- **Never background a long command and end your turn.** The session terminates\n  with the turn and the process dies with it, while the run is still recorded as\n  completed — a successful-looking run with no evidence attached.\n- **A single shell call is time-bounded (~10 minutes).** Waiting out a longer\n  check inside one call fails too. Split long verification into bounded\n  sequential calls (package subsets, `-run` masks) and state explicitly what you\n  reran yourself versus accepted from already-attached evidence.\n- **Attach evidence before you end the lifecycle, not after.** \"I will attach it\n  when the run finishes\" is unfulfillable by construction: once your turn ends\n  there is no session left to attach anything.\n\n## Your Role\n# developer\n\n## Description\n\nWrites code — features, bugfixes, refactoring. Writes tests for the code produced.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nCode + tests.\nFinal human-facing wording must say \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\", not \"done\", \"complete\", \"finished\", \"final\", or \"готово\", when the board status is `to-review`.\n\n## Standing Orders\n\n1. When you change behavior, add or update tests for that scope unless the task explicitly forbids it.\n2. Run the relevant test commands yourself before handoff; do not leave test execution implicit.\n3. Run the relevant build or validation command after changes to confirm the project still compiles.\n4. If a required test or build cannot be run, state exactly what was not run and why.\n5. Stop if the implementation starts depending on a forced fit: a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture conflict that would require compensating hacks. Document the constraint and options, then ask or mark the task blocked instead of adding more stubs, flags, priority rules, or tests around a broken assumption.\n6. For board reads, use compact task-specific projections. A concrete assignment does not need routine `summary()`, `plan()`, `schema()`, or `{ full }`; request scoped schema only after an unknown call.\n\n### Evidence Honesty Contract\n\n1. Run each validation or gate command directly as a standalone process. Do not pipe it through `tee`; do not use a pipe chain unless `pipefail` is enabled and the gate command's real status is preserved.\n2. Report the real exit code of every validation or gate command.\n3. Report expected-red gates truthfully as failing: when a command is expected to fail (for example, `go test` in a package-less module), give its real non-zero exit code and a one-line expected-failure rationale; never present it as passing.\n4. Check a checklist item tied to a command only after that exact command has actually run green with exit code 0. If it did not run or did not exit 0, leave the item unchecked.\n\n## Status Transitions\n\n- **start_status:** `development`\n- **end_status:** `to-review` (review handoff, not accepted done)\n\n## Constraints\n\nFull read/write access does not authorize forced-fit workarounds. Tests and stubs may verify a valid design, but they must not be used to make an invalid product/API model appear acceptable.\n\n## Skills\n\nThese role skill references are a lazy catalog, not a mandate to bulk-read every\nbody. Before technical work, identify the skills relevant to this task's concrete\nscope and read those full skill bodies. Always read any skill explicitly required\nby the task, user, or project instructions.\n\n- **project-management**: `/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md`\n- **swiftui**: `/Users/iv/.claude/skills/swiftui/SKILL.md`\n- **core-data**: `/Users/iv/.claude/skills/core-data/SKILL.md`\n- **go-testing-tools**: `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md`\n- **architecture-diagrams**: `/Users/iv/.claude/skills/architecture-diagrams/SKILL.md`\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- [ ] Production entry points implement the scoped deliverable: Prove recursive closed shapes, Unicode ordering, invalid dates, malformed identifiers, and cross-platform golden identities\n- [ ] Relevant positive, negative, compatibility, and recovery tests pass with logs attached\n- [ ] README/doctor/capability evidence and specification traceability are updated without unsupported claims\n- [ ] Go native fuzz targets cover scalar parsing and JCS canonicalization at production entry points, with a committed seed corpus derived from the shapes prior reviews found: leap seconds, lowercase RFC3339, Windows reserved device names and wildcards, unpaired surrogates, and duplicate or non-string map keys\n- [ ] Canonicalization is proven round-trip stable and order-independent under fuzz: the identity digest of an object is invariant under key reordering and insignificant whitespace, and no input causes a panic or an accepted value that later refuses\n- [ ] Fuzz runs are bounded and deterministic in the validation suite: a fixed -fuzztime with a seed corpus, so the configured gate stays reproducible rather than time-dependent\n- [ ] Implementation matches AC\n- [ ] Solution fits project architecture\n- [ ] Tests green\n- [ ] Gate, refusal, validation, authorization, and attestation behavior attacked, not read — positive-path-only evidence is not accepted\n- [ ] If review does not accept the work — verdict evidence added and status routed by the explicit verdict branches\n- [ ] Scoped closed-shape validation exists for Sections 10.1-10.4 and is invoked on the composed production path before identity is calculated or verified: exact top-level and nested member sets, and BlobChunk index/offset/size order, bounds and coverage invariants, with the composed call site named in evidence\n- [ ] Negative tests and fuzz corpus cover an unknown top-level member, an unknown nested member, invalid chunk bounds/order/coverage, and closed Transfer Manifest nested shapes; each must make identity calculation and verification refuse rather than attest\n- [ ] tracecheck -section 17.3 passes for the implemented identity contribution without claiming migration publication or atomic-reference behavior that README correctly reports unavailable\n- [ ] AUDIT, not spot-fix: every open or extension point in closed_shapes.go validates its full key grammar per Section 1.6, and every string[n..m] bound counts Unicode characters rather than UTF-8 bytes; both directions are proven, an invalid key of any namespace is refused and a valid multibyte value at the character bound is accepted\n- [ ] Every fuzz target the candidate defines is invoked by the configured validation suite with a fixed -fuzztime, including the closed-shape target; a newly added target that is not wired into the suite is a gate gap\n- [ ] Constraints are DERIVED from the pinned SPEC.md Sections 10.1-10.4 by systematic enumeration of every declared member type, character bound, nullability, tagged-union, ordering, uniqueness and cross-field rule reachable at this gate, and the enumeration itself is recorded as evidence; a hand-picked subset is not acceptable\n- [ ] Nested value rules reuse the production scalar validators rather than looser local grammars, and the normative TM-GIT-N2 stage-4 refusal plus valid multibyte values at each character limit are proven through both CalculateObjectIdentity and VerifyObjectIdentity\n- [ ] README states only what reproduces through both public identity entries; no completed-audit or coverage claim is made that a reader cannot reproduce\n- [ ] Code written per task description and AC\n- [ ] Relevant tests written for new or changed behavior and passing\n- [ ] Gating, refusing, validating, authorizing, or attesting behavior covered by negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it must reject, with the production call site named\n- [ ] Lint clean\n- [ ] Relevant build/validation commands run after changes and build not broken\n- [ ] New outcome artifact attached on the board with a task-scoped name when the work produces notes, logs, screenshots, or other deliverables\n- [ ] Important findings, decisions, anomalies, or regressions recorded in logbook when relevant\n## Your Task\n\n- **ID**: TASK-260830-8x76g1\n- **Title**: TASK-260830-8x76g1: fuzz-common-types-and-canonicalization\n- **Parent**: STORY-260830-2wdm5e\n### Description\n\nProve recursive closed shapes, Unicode ordering, invalid dates, malformed identifiers, and cross-platform golden identities. This is an implementation deliverable, not a specification rewrite.\n### Scope\n\nrelux-works/agent-session-manager-spec@v0.5.0 (commit 28bf96d7dd7ebf3cd9e2ccd91d35b8660699dd5c); Sections 1.6, 10.1-10.4, 17.3. This task grew beyond fuzz coverage by review: because identity must never attest a structurally invalid object, it now also owns the scoped closed-shape conformance gate for Sections 10.1-10.4 and the fuzz targets that keep that gate honest. Work only inside the common-types-and-canonical-identities story boundary.\n### Acceptance Criteria\n\nProduction behavior demonstrates: Prove recursive closed shapes, Unicode ordering, invalid dates, malformed identifiers, and cross-platform golden identities. Exact contract fixtures and negative/refusal cases pass; crash/idempotency evidence is included when the operation mutates durable state; no unsupported capability is advertised.\n\n## Instructions\n\nNo specific instructions attached. Work according to the task description and acceptance criteria above.\n\n## Board CLI Reference\n\nYou have access to `task-board` CLI for managing your work. All writes use the `m` (mutation DSL) subcommand:\n\n```bash\n# FIRST/LAST lifecycle commands:\n# Use explicit status changes when the task flow requires it.\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=analysis)'       # analyst-style work\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=development)'    # implementation / testing work\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=reviewing)'      # reviewer handoff\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=blocked)'        # when blocked\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-8x76g1, status=to-review)'      # when your work is ready for review\n\n# Track progress with checklist\ntask-board m 'check_item(TASK-260830-8x76g1, item=1)'                        # check item N\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260830-8x76g1, text=\"Write tests\")'    # add checklist item\n\n# Add notes about your progress, decisions, blockers, or review findings\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260830-8x76g1, text=\"your note here\")'\n\n# Save short text directly as outcome resources\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260830-8x76g1, name=TASK-260830-8x76g1_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Description\")'\n\n# Attach an existing local file (screenshots, PDFs, logs, archives, research docs)\ntask-board resource add TASK-260830-8x76g1 ./path/to/file --type outcome --name TASK-260830-8x76g1_artifact.bin -d \"Description\"\n```\n\n## Spawn Run Control\n\nTracked background spawn runs expose `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` in the child environment.\nIf your work is long-running, check for operator directives at safe checkpoints:\n\n```bash\ntask-board spawn status \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\"\ntask-board spawn directives \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\"\n```\n\nCurrent runtimes do not support direct inbound push into your active session.\nTreat directives as cooperative checkpoint signals:\n- persist your current notes/artifacts before acting on `cancel`-style requests\n- only honor pause/reroute intent at a safe checkpoint\n- if no directive is present, continue normally\n\n## IMPORTANT: Saving Results\n\nWhen you produce work products (research documents, design docs, screenshots, logs, archives, implementation notes), you MUST save them as outcome resources with names that include the task ID:\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260830-8x76g1, name=TASK-260830-8x76g1_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Description\")'\ntask-board resource add TASK-260830-8x76g1 ./path/to/file --type outcome --name TASK-260830-8x76g1_artifact.bin -d \"Description\"\n```\n\nIf you revise the same artifact later, use `task-board m 'update_resource(...)'` or `task-board resource update ...` instead of creating a silent overwrite.\n\nIf you discover important findings, decisions, anomalies, regressions, or non-obvious constraints while working, record them in `logbook` as well as on the board.\n\nThis ensures your results persist on the board and are accessible to other agents and the coordinator. Spawn completion is expected to produce at least one new task-scoped outcome artifact before the task can cleanly remain in `to-review`.\n\n## Evidence That Counts\n\nA passing suite means nothing unless something in it would have failed.\n\n- Any behavior that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS ships with negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it must reject. A positive test proves the gate is reachable, not that it works.\n- Prove a bound by NARROWING the gate, not only by deleting it. A delete-only mutant proves the gate exists and says nothing about the class it covers.\n- Prove behavior by driving the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. A helper that is unit-tested but called from nowhere promises nothing.\n- Standard negative shapes to test and to look for: forged or self-minted evidence; absent evidence treated as satisfied; the check present but uncalled from production; a bypass path around the check; a capability claim that does not reproduce.\n- An absence and a failure to read are different facts. A failed, partial, or malformed read is never a legitimate absence, and a fallback defined for absence must not fire on a read failure.\n- Prove, or report nothing. Where a property cannot be established, report unknown instead of inferring it from a proxy signal; callers act on a plausible guess.\n\nShapes vocabulary and the incident record behind each rule: `references/negative-evidence.md` in the project-management skill.\n\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\n\nDo not keep implementing when autonomous work starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the task conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining \"solution\" is mostly compensating hacks.\n\nWarning signs:\n- each fix needs another flag, stub, priority rule, mock-only behavior, or special-case test\n- the tests can pass only because the test harness avoids the real platform behavior\n- the implementation depends on an assumption you can no longer defend\n- the user-facing behavior cannot be described cleanly without contradicting the product model\n\nWhen this happens, stop product-code changes before adding another workaround layer. Attach or note:\n- the constraint and evidence\n- the failed assumptions/attempts\n- the viable options and tradeoffs\n- the recommended option\n- the exact human/product/architecture decision needed\n\nThen set the board item to `blocked` and ask only for that exact decision or external input. This stop applies only to a concrete external blocker or an unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; recoverable failures and ordinary rework stay autonomous. Tests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct; use them only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.\n\n## Completion Discipline\n\nKeep working until the task reaches a terminal handoff for your role. If no objective blocker remains, do not stop while the board item is still parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`.\n\nBefore your final status change:\n- satisfy the task acceptance criteria and relevant checklist items\n- attach outcome evidence for the work you produced\n- run the relevant verification commands when the task changes code, tests, docs, or config\n\nUse `blocked` only for either a concrete external blocker you cannot resolve autonomously or an unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.\n\nStatus language is literal:\n- `to-review` means your role has handed work to review; it does not mean the board task is accepted or done.\n- In your final response, say \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\" when the final board status is `to-review`.\n- Do not say \"done\", \"complete\", \"finished\", \"final\", or \"готово\" as the overall task state unless the board status is actually `done`.\n\n## LAST — Run For Role Handoff\n\nWhen you have completed all role work and the task is ready for its role handoff, run this as your **final board command**:\n\n```bash\ntask-board handoff TASK-260830-8x76g1 --role developer\n```\n\n## Working Directory\nBoard directory: `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board`\n\nWork in the project root. Do not modify board files directly — always use the `task-board` CLI.\n\n## Story Workspace\n\nYou are running in an isolated Git worktree for STORY-260830-2wdm5e.\n\n- Workspace path: `.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree` (this is your working directory)\n- Branch: `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e`, forked from `main`\n- Authoritative board: `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board` (already exported as `TASK_BOARD_DIR`)\n\nMake every repository change here. Do not switch, rebase, merge, or delete this branch, and do not run `task-board` against the `.task-board` copy inside this worktree — it is a checkout artifact, not the board. Integration into trunk is the orchestrator's step, not yours.\n","text_elements":[]}]},"started_at_ms":1788201347418,"completed_at_ms":1788201347418}}
32:{"timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:36:09.559Z","ordinal":31,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0591b-1f95-7943-a69e-e6707d41e923","turn_id":"01a0591b-218e-7791-8c1d-c09fdfe98b6c","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-2a6778f3-4865-49fa-ae1c-898929ae36b2","process_id":"75958","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","cat /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md && cat /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/resources-and-bugs.md && cat /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/negative-evidence.md && git status --short && git branch --show-current && git log -5 --oneline --decorate && rg --files -g 'go.mod' -g '*.go' -g 'README.md' -g 'SPEC.md' -g '*tracecheck*' | sed -n '1,240p'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-2wdm5e/worktree","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"cat /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md && cat /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/resources-and-bugs.md && cat /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/negative-evidence.md && git status --short && git branch --show-current && git log -5 --oneline --decorate && rg --files -g 'go.mod' -g '*.go' -g 'README.md' -g 'SPEC.md' -g '*tracecheck*' | sed -n '1,240p'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Statuses\n\n9 statuses organized in a workflow:\n\n| Status | Meaning | Who sets |\n|--------|---------|----------|\n| **backlog** | Captured, no work yet | anyone |\n| **analysis** | Researching, decomposing | anyone |\n| **to-dev** | Ready for development | orchestrator |\n| **development** | Writing code | sub-agent |\n| **to-review** | Code ready for review | sub-agent |\n| **reviewing** | Reviewer child is checking the work | reviewer |\n| **done** | Reviewed and accepted leaf work, or computed container completion | reviewer / orchestrator after reviewer verdict; parent aggregation |\n| **closed** | Explicit administrative disposition: cancelled, obsolete, or won't do (reason in notes) | anyone |\n| **blocked** | Concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision (full evidence packet in notes/resource) | anyone |\n\n## Status Flow\n\n**Happy path:**\n```\nbacklog → analysis → to-dev → development → to-review → reviewing → done\n```\n\n**Returns from reviewing:**\n- `reviewing → analysis` — need more research\n- `reviewing → to-dev` — code issues, needs rework\n\n**Terminal from any status:**\n- `any → closed` — won't do / cancelled\n- `any → blocked` — concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision\n\n**Note on closed:** Use `closed` for cancelled, obsolete, or won't-do items. Don't delete — close with a note explaining why. History is preserved, and the item can be reopened if needed.\n\nFor a leaf task or bug, `done` means its delivery was accepted and `closed`\nmeans it was explicitly disposed without accepted delivery. A leaf may be\nclosed from any lifecycle state.\n\nFor a story or epic, `done` is computed completion: every direct child is\n`done` or `closed`. `closed` is an explicit administrative disposition of the\ncontainer itself. A container may be closed only when every direct and\ntransitive descendant is already `done` or `closed`; an empty container also\nsatisfies this rule. Close or complete descendant work first, then close\ncontainers from the inside out. `task-board validate` reports\n`PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` for stored closed containers that still contain active\ndescendants.\n\nValidation is strict by default for empty containers too: an empty Story/Epic\nstored as `done` aggregates to `backlog` and is reported. A migrated local\nboard may explicitly set\n`task-board.config.json -> validation.allow_legacy_empty_done_containers` to\n`true` to suppress only that historical empty-`done` mismatch. Empty active\ncontainers and every container with one or more children remain strict.\n\n**Don't delete elements.** Use `closed` instead of `delete`. Deletion loses history and context. The only exception is removing test/junk data during board setup.\n\n**Orchestrator review routing flow:** When a producer child sets `to-review`:\n1. Orchestrator confirms the producer attached task-scoped outcome evidence.\n2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer child with explicit selection, for example `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.\n3. Reviewer child sets/keeps `reviewing`, reads artifacts/code, and writes the verdict.\n4. If accepted → `done`. If changes are requested → `to-dev` (code rework) or `analysis` (needs more research), with notes/outcome evidence.\n4a. When the reviewer was launched against a Change Request (a managed Story workspace run), acceptance is recorded by `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=...)` instead. That mutation sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, and parks the element at `to-review` as the **accepted handoff** — accepted work never stays in `reviewing`. The Orchestrator then runs `task-board worktree checkpoint <ID>` for a **non-final** leaf, which commits the accepted candidate onto the Story branch and makes the `done` transition with `commit_ack=scope_committed`. A Change Request with `repository_delta=empty` skips the commit and still reaches `done` by the same command. `accept_cr` accepts no `commit_ack` argument, its `evidence` must be a task-scoped outcome resource this reviewer run attached or updated (naming the producer's own `_results.md` is refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`), only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may call it (anything else is refused with `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`), and the live task/reviewer checklist must be complete. All refusals precede CR, status, and activity writes.\n\n4b. **`checkpoint` never closes the Story.** When the leaf is the Story's last open child, closing it would promote the Story to `done` in the same mutation and cover that transition with `commit_ack=scope_committed` — attesting that the Story's scope is committed while its branch has never reached trunk. `worktree checkpoint` refuses that with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint` and names `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>`, which lands the branch before making the transition. The refusal is a scope rule, not an acknowledgement rule: it applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it leaves the branch, the accepted record and the board untouched so the integration path can still use them.\n5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.\n6. If a child exits early or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, the Orchestrator inspects the run/outcomes and retries or reroutes through a focused child. Recoverable work is never handed to the human.\n7. A `blocked` verdict stops routing only when it identifies a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision and records the full evidence packet; otherwise retry or reroute.\n\n`reviewing` is a real state, not a pass-through. A reviewer child must do actual review work between the producer handoff and `done`; the orchestrator routes and monitors that review instead of performing the verdict inline.\n\n`reviewing` is never a verdict, including under the Change Request flow. A reviewer run that ends at `reviewing` with no accepted Change Request is cancelled and a successor reviewer is spawned — that is what distinguishes \"the reviewer decided nothing\" from \"the reviewer decided\", and `accept_cr` does not change it. The accepted branch is derived from the Change Request record, not from the `reviewing` status.\n\n`to-review` therefore carries two meanings that the record disambiguates: a producer handoff awaiting review, and an accepted Change Request awaiting orchestrator checkpoint or integration. `task-board worktree status` and the Change Request state say which.\n\nA tracked spawned producer cannot use direct `set_status(..., status=to-review)`\nfor its assigned element. That workflow guardrail returns a typed remediation to\nrun `task-board handoff ELEMENT --role ROLE`, which verifies checklist and\noutcome evidence. Untracked operator status administration is unchanged.\n\n`to-review` is an internal routing trigger, never a handoff of routine review to the human. Human input is reserved for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision.\n\nHuman-facing wording follows the board state. `to-review` means \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\"; it is not \"done\", \"complete\", \"finished\", \"final\", or \"готово\". Say a task is done only after the board item is actually `done`.\n\n## Per-Element Review Policy\n\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with\n`create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with\n`set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` query\nprojection. New elements and legacy elements without the field default to\n`required`.\n\n- `required` keeps the producer -> reviewer -> rework/reviewer loop. Only an\n  accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but selection should favor a cheaper suitable effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates.\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff from `to-review`\n  directly to `done` only after checking acceptance criteria, checklist,\n  validation results, and outcome evidence. Missing evidence returns to the\n  producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\n\nThis policy changes reviewer routing, not the need for board-owned evidence, and\nnever introduces routine human review.\n\n## blocked vs is_blocked\n\nTwo different concepts:\n\n- **blocked** (status) — explicit stop-the-line status for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Notes or an outcome resource must record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.\n- **is_blocked** (computed) — from `blocked-by` dependencies; if dependency is not `done`/`closed`, element is blocked\n\nCLI display:\n```\nTASK-05: to-dev [BLOCKED by TASK-02]     ← computed from dependency\nTASK-07: blocked                          ← explicit status\n```\n\n## Automatic Parent Status Aggregation\n\nAfter a child status mutation, the CLI recomputes every ancestor from its direct\nchildren. It is derived state: an unassigned story or epic may follow an\nassigned child without implying ownership of the container.\n\n- If every child is `done` or `closed`, a non-closed parent becomes `done`.\n  An already explicit `closed` parent stays `closed`; terminal child updates do\n  not silently normalize it to `done`.\n- Otherwise the parent adopts the highest-priority active child status:\n  `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, then `backlog`.\n  `blocked` children do not select a parent status; a parent with only blocked\n  children keeps its current manual status.\n- The recomputation can promote, escalate, reopen, or demote a parent, then\n  continues through all ancestors. A parent that is `blocked` explicitly is not\n  recomputed.\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=development)'\n# TASK-260205-abc123 → development\n# STORY-260205-def456 → development (auto-escalated)\n# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → development (auto-escalated)\n```\n\nFor Task/Bug records created with the current format, the first transition into\n`development` also requires an explicit estimated value. Set it first with\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The refusal\nis a typed estimate-required error, parallel to the unfinished-dependency start\nrefusal. Records whose legacy `progress.md` has no Estimate section are\ngrandfathered and are not retroactively blocked; an idempotent status write for\nan element already in `development` is not treated as a new start.\n\n### Commit confirmation for accepted work\n\nThis gate exists so the commit-owning mover lays out its own delivered scope\nas atomic, project-perspective commits and honestly acknowledges that scope is\ncommitted — it is not a human checkpoint. An Orchestrator (or other\ncommit-owning mover) must not stop autonomous work to ask the human how to\nsplit commits, or whether to acknowledge, on every commit or close. The mover\ndecides its own commit decomposition — how many commits, per-story or\nfeature-clustered, ordering, buildability — and commits that scope itself,\nbackdated per the project's commit-time policy when one is configured. Human\nauthorization is the standing grant to commit work, not a per-commit\napproval.\n\nThis section is the single source for that contract. Role bodies, the spawn\ntemplates, and the router carry only the ownership statement and a link back\nhere; they must not restate the mechanics below.\n\nTop-level `task-board.config.json -> version_control.confirm` defaults to\n`false`. When it is `true`, `desired_commit_time` is required. Any mutation\nthat would move a Bug or Story to `done` must then include the exact mover-local\nacknowledgement `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This covers a direct Bug/Story\ntransition and a Task/Bug terminal transition that automatically promotes its\nparent Story.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version_control\": {\n    \"confirm\": true,\n    \"desired_commit_time\": \"evening MSK\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260205-def456, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'\n```\n\nThe error reports the transition-causing element, every enforced Bug/Story\ntransition, the owner message, and the configured desired time. The\nacknowledgement covers only the mover's scope; foreign uncommitted work is\nallowed and task-board performs no git clean-tree check. Never acknowledge\n`commit_ack=scope_committed` when the mover's own scope is genuinely\nuncommitted — commit it first, then acknowledge. Local evaluation and\nstatus writes share one mutation lock. In remote mode an enforcing CLI first\nrequires the server's `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1` capability, then\nthe server evaluates the acknowledgement and writes the child/ancestor statuses\nunder one serialized mutation.\n\nA reviewer-archetype spawned run must not supply `commit_ack`. It records\nacceptance evidence and hands the accepted verdict to the mover that owns the\nscoped commit (typically the Orchestrator) — never to the human, and it must\nnot escalate the commit-or-acknowledge decision to the human itself; that\nmover commits first, then makes the final `done` transition with\n`commit_ack=scope_committed`.\n\nBoard structure decides how often this gate fires on an unfinished parent; the\nrule that keeps it honest lives with the other decomposition rules in\n[planning workflow](planning-workflow.md).\n\nReactivation follows one deterministic rule: adding a descendant creates it in\n`backlog`, and adding or changing any descendant to an aggregating active status\nruns ordinary aggregation through every ancestor. Any `done` or `closed`\ncontainer on that path reopens to the highest-priority active aggregate status;\nexplicit closure is not sticky once descendant work becomes active. The target\nis the aggregate child status, not a fixed `development` state:\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=to-dev)'\n# TASK-260205-abc123 → to-dev\n# STORY-260205-def456 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\n# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\n```\n\nAutomatic container transitions do not require an assignee. Hard dependencies\nstill gate automatic changes to `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, and\n`done`: if a container has an unfinished blocker, its derived status is left\nunchanged and the child mutation succeeds. Direct manual transitions use the\nsame dependency gate; they additionally require an assignee for `to-dev`,\n`development`, and `to-review`.\n\n`blocked` remains the existing non-aggregating exception: blocked children do\nnot select a parent status. Closed ancestors remain closed rather than being\nsilently normalized to `done`, so validation reports every affected container\nthat still has a blocked descendant. The operator must reopen the container or\nresolve/close that descendant work.\n\n## Story Activation Reminders\n\nAn `agent_reminders` array is added to the JSON envelope of a production\nmutation whenever that mutation's persisted changes include a Story\ntransitioning out of `backlog`. It is **additive advisory output**: it never\nfetches Git, never mutates the board beyond what the mutation already did, and\nnever replaces the authoritative freshness gate documented in\n[task-board config](task-board-config.md#trunk-discovery-authority-and-refusals) —\na reminder can render every authority field `null` and the mutation still\nsucceeds.\n\n### Trigger\n\nA change qualifies when all of the following hold:\n\n- `element_id` starts with `STORY-` (case-insensitive).\n- `field` is `status` and the change's recorded `old_value` is exactly\n  `backlog`.\n- The new status is `analysis`, `to-dev`, or `development`. Every other\n  target status — `to-review`, `reviewing`, `blocked`, `done`, `closed`, or a\n  `backlog → backlog` no-op — does not qualify, and neither does a\n  reactivation from `done` or `closed` (only `backlog` is the qualifying old\n  value).\n\nBoth the mutation's primary change and every side effect are scanned, so a\nTask/Bug mutation that auto-escalates its containing Story from `backlog`\nstill emits the Story's reminder even though the Task/Bug itself never\nqualifies on its own ID prefix.\n\n### Deduplication and order\n\nWithin one mutation's full change list (primary change first, then side\neffects in their recorded order), only the first qualifying change per Story\nID produces a reminder — a batch that touches the same Story's status twice\n(for example a reopen side effect followed by the requested transition) still\nemits exactly one reminder for it. Distinct qualifying Story IDs each get\ntheir own reminder, in first-seen order.\n\n### Payload shape\n\n```json\n{\n  \"code\": \"story_worktree_base_preflight_required\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-29T12:34:56.123456789Z\",\n  \"message\": \"Resolve and refresh the configured integration branch upstream, compare exact OIDs, and record the selected Story worktree base before provisioning or trusting it.\",\n  \"story_id\": \"STORY-260218-bbbbbb\",\n  \"trunk_ref\": \"refs/heads/main\",\n  \"trunk_oid\": \"8c3f1a9...\",\n  \"upstream_ref\": \"refs/remotes/protected/main\",\n  \"upstream_oid\": \"5e2b7d0...\",\n  \"selected_base_oid\": null\n}\n```\n\n`code`, `message`, and the RFC3339Nano UTC `timestamp` are stable across every\nreminder in one mutation (one clock read per mutation, not per Story).\n`trunk_ref`, `trunk_oid`, `upstream_ref`, `upstream_oid`, and\n`selected_base_oid` are always present as JSON keys and are explicit `null`,\nnever omitted, whenever that specific piece of evidence is not known. The\n`agent_reminders` key itself is only added to the result when at least one\nchange qualified; a mutation with no qualifying activation adds no key at all\n(not an empty array).\n\n### Authority evidence: local versus remote\n\nThe five nullable fields come from a cache-only projection that is read after\nthe mutation persists and never fails the mutation on error:\n\n- **Local CLI** — prefers a persisted workspace freshness record from a prior\n  `task-board spawn` for that Story (`trunk_ref`/`trunk_oid` = the recorded\n  integration branch and its local OID at spawn time, `upstream_ref`/\n  `upstream_oid` = the recorded upstream ref/OID, `selected_base_oid` = the\n  OID the workspace actually branched from). When no such record exists, it\n  falls back to a cache-only Git discovery (`DiscoverCachedTrunk`) against the\n  configured `integration_base_branch` — the same discovery order as the\n  freshness gate, but it never fetches, so `upstream_oid` reflects whatever\n  the last fetch already cached — and `selected_base_oid` is `null`, because\n  a cache-only projection names no provisioned workspace. Any read failure\n  (unreadable tracking config, unreadable ref) renders every field `null`\n  rather than failing the mutation.\n- **Remote (board-server)** — the reminder is produced by the identical shared\n  `pkg/board/query` mutation code, so it fires on the same triggers with the\n  same dedup, but the server wires no authority source (there is no local Git\n  checkout to read), so every one of the five fields is always `null`; only\n  `code`, `timestamp`, `message`, and `story_id` are populated.\n\n### Mutation coverage\n\n`agent_reminders` is decorated by one shared boundary that a subset of\nproduction mutations route their persisted changes through:\n\n| Covered — can emit a reminder | Not wired — structurally cannot emit |\n| --- | --- |\n| `set_status` | `normalize_legacy_status` |\n| `handoff` | `set_estimate` |\n| `create` (only via its ancestor-reopen side effects) | `set_details`, `rename` |\n| `delete` | `check_item`, `uncheck_item`, `add_checklist_item` |\n| `set_parent` | `set_notes` |\n| `assign`, `unassign`, `link`, `unlink` (wired, but their own changes are never `status` changes, so they only ever forward an ancestor side effect) | context mutations |\n\n`assign`/`unassign`/`link`/`unlink` are listed on the covered side because\nthey share the same decorator plumbing as `set_status`; they simply never\nproduce a qualifying change themselves. The \"not wired\" column takes no `cfg`\nat all in `registerMutations`, so it cannot mint a reminder even if a caller\ncrafted a matching change shape.\n\n## Landing a Story on trunk\n\nThis section extends the commit-confirmation contract above to the one path\nwhere a `done` transition is produced by a command rather than typed by hand:\n`task-board worktree integrate`, which lands a Story developed in a managed\nStory workspace. The mechanics of the managed workspace itself live in\n[tracked background spawn](tracked-background-spawn.md#story-worktree-isolation);\nwhat belongs here is the ordering and the exact scope of the acknowledgement.\n\n**Accepted comes before done, always.** A reviewer records acceptance with\n`accept_cr`, which parks the element at `to-review` as the accepted handoff. No\nGit operation has happened at that point and nothing is `done`. The\nOrchestrator then integrates, and the `done` transition is the *last* board\neffect of a run that has already moved trunk. The superseded order — close the\nboard first, land the work afterwards — is withdrawn, because it produced an\nacknowledgement that attested a scope no repository had yet seen.\n\n```bash\ntask-board worktree integrate STORY-260205-def456 \\\n  --cr TASK-260205-abc123 --revision 3 \\\n  --commit-time 2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00\n```\n\n**The landing gate defaults to refusing.** `integrate` requires the\n`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` suite to be configured; with no\nsuite it refuses `validation_not_configured` before any ref moves. A project\nwith nothing to run states that explicitly as `{\"commands\": []}`. See\n[task-board config](task-board-config.md#the-landing-gate-suite).\n\n**Squash, fast-forward-only, and one explicit time.** The accepted Story\ncandidate lands as one squash commit on trunk; the branch's internal Task\ncheckpoints are discarded rather than replayed, and trunk is only ever\nfast-forwarded. When `version_control.confirm` is `true`, `--commit-time` is\nrequired, must parse as RFC3339, and is applied to the author date and the\ncommitter date of both commits this command creates. The example above is this\nrepository's own policy expressed concretely: the previous day at 20:01 MSK,\nthat is `2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00` for an integration run on 2026-08-07. With\nconfirmation disabled and no flag, the current time is used instead.\n\n**What the acknowledgement asserts here, and what it does not.** The command\nsupplies `commit_ack=scope_committed` on the `done` transition itself, and that\ntransition is not reachable until the Story's squash commit is genuinely\ncontained in trunk — a resumed run whose trunk no longer carries the recorded\ncandidate refuses rather than stamping the acknowledgement. So the assertion is\nexactly: *the repository scope of this Story is on trunk.* It is narrower than\na clean tree in three specific ways, and each of them is a normal, expected\nstate at the moment the acknowledgement is written:\n\n- **The Story's own board delta is still uncommitted.** The board-state commit\n  is created *after* the transition, precisely because the transition is what\n  produces the board bytes it records.\n- **Board paths outside this Story are untouched.** The second commit stages an\n  explicit pathspec: this Story's own subtree, the resource directories of its\n  elements, and the ancestor aggregation files on its chain. Everything else\n  under `.task-board/` — `.task-board/.goals/**` and every other Story's\n  subtree — stays exactly as it was: ordinary uncommitted working-tree state\n  that this command neither examines nor stages.\n- **Foreign uncommitted work is untouched**, on the same rule the section above\n  states for a hand-typed acknowledgement.\n\n**The two commits, in order.** First the squash commit carrying the accepted\ncandidate tree; then the `done` transition; then a second, board-only commit\ntitled `Record <STORY-ID> board state`. A Story whose accepted candidate has no\nrepository delta produces no first commit and still produces the second one —\nits board record is the whole of its work product.\n\n**Recovery is re-running the same command.** Integration keeps one durable\ntransaction record per Story, so a crashed or interrupted run resumes the\nrecorded phase instead of starting a second integration, and a completed one is\na no-op rather than a duplicate landing. A rollback is offered in exactly one\nphase — nothing landed yet, trunk unmoved — and is requested explicitly with\n`--rollback`; no later phase undoes a commit that is already on trunk. Partial\nintegration is therefore a state to resume, never a state to clean up by hand.\n# Resources\n\nResources are owned by the board element they are attached to. Use explicit\nCRUD operations against that element ID. Local payloads are grouped under\n`.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/`; remote board-server uses the equivalent\nelement-scoped filesystem or MinIO object-key prefix.\n\n## Resource Types\n\n| Type | Purpose | Example |\n|------|---------|---------|\n| **precondition** | Inputs needed before work starts | Design docs, specs, instructions, API schemas |\n| **outcome** | Results produced by work | Research findings, implementation notes, analysis |\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\n# Create a resource\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_design.md, type=precondition, content=\"...\", description=\"Architecture design\")'\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content=\"...\", description=\"Research findings\")'\n\n# Replace an existing resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content=\"revised findings\", description=\"Research findings v2\")'\ntask-board resource update TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_results.md --name reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome\n\n# Adopt an existing orphaned local payload without rewriting it\ntask-board resource adopt TASK-260218-abc revisions/2/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome --description \"Recovered result\"\n\n# Delete a resource (destructive — requires --confirm)\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md)'\ntask-board resource delete TASK-260218-abc artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md --confirm\n\n# View resources on an element\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { preconditionResources outcomeResources }'\n```\n\n## Element-Owned Storage\n\nResources from different tasks are not stored in one shared file pile. Each\nelement gets its own directory, and every resource attached to that element is\nstored below it:\n\n```\n.task-board/.resources/\n├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/\n│   ├── findings/\n│   │   └── investigation.md\n│   ├── artifacts/\n│   │   └── run-1/\n│   │       └── output.json\n│   ├── reports/\n│   │   └── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md\n│   └── revisions/\n│       └── 2/\n│           └── review.md\n└── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\n    └── research.md         # outcome\n```\n\nThe resource `name` is a safe forward-slash-relative path. Agents may choose\nthe hierarchy that fits the artifact and nest it further as needed. Every\nsegment must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and must not be `.`, `..`, an\nabsolute/drive-qualified path, or contain a backslash. Raw forward slashes are\nthe only hierarchy delimiter; malformed URL escapes and encoded or repeatedly\nencoded separators/traversal are rejected. Attach related files to the same\nowning element; do not invent a generic board-wide resource task or write\npayloads into `.task-board/.resources/` manually.\n\nDirectories are structural nodes, not payloads. Resource CRUD, validation, and\nhandoff evidence recognize regular files only; a directory named like a report\ncannot be adopted or used to satisfy outcome evidence.\n\nLocal and board-server filesystem element deletion is journaled and\ncrash-recoverable. Direct resource CRUD first resolves any pending staged\nelement delete under the board mutation lock. MinIO element/subtree deletion\ncurrently fails before mutation with the typed\n`ErrAtomicResourceDeleteUnsupported` capability error; ordinary resource CRUD\nremains available.\n\nReferences are tracked in `progress.md` as markdown links:\n```markdown\n## Precondition Resources\n- [specs/auth/design.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/precondition/specs/auth/design.md) — Architecture design\n\n## Outcome Resources\n- [reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/outcome/reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md) — Research findings\n```\n\n## Spawn Integration\n\nPrecondition resources are automatically injected into agent prompts by `task-board spawn`. Full prompts preserve normal text inlining. Lite prompts use the resolved `agent_context.prompt.preconditions` policy: by default, text below `inline_bytes` is rendered inline, while larger text and binary resources are written read-only under `.temp/resources/` and represented by their name, description, byte count, SHA-256 digest, and local path. This means:\n\n1. Attach instructions/specs as precondition resources to a task\n2. Run `task-board spawn TASK-XX --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — agent gets everything automatically\n3. Agent saves results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write, then `update_resource(...)` for revisions\n\n## Best Practices\n\n- **Use preconditions for agent instructions** — attach design docs, specs, or step-by-step instructions before spawning\n- **Use outcomes for work products** — research findings, analysis, implementation notes\n- **Attach to the owning element** — pass the Task, Bug, Story, or Epic ID that owns the artifact; the storage layer groups its payloads under that element directory automatically\n- **Use explicit create/update/delete** — `add_resource` / `resource add` must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` when replacing an existing payload\n- **Use adopt only for local repair** — `resource adopt` registers bytes already stored at `.resources/<elementID>/<relative-resource-path>` without rewriting them; a missing payload returns resource-not-found and an existing declaration returns resource-exists\n- **Choose a useful hierarchy inside the owning element** — `findings/`, `artifacts/`, `reports/`, and `revisions/` are conventions, not a fixed schema; nest further when it makes the handoff easier to navigate\n- **Use safe relative resource paths** — `/` separates validated path segments; empty, dot/traversal, absolute/drive-qualified, backslash, control-character, malformed escape, and encoded/repeatedly encoded separator or traversal forms are rejected in local and remote storage\n- **Attach files, not folders** — directories only organize resource files and never satisfy resource existence or handoff evidence\n- **Always use board commands** — never create/edit resource files or progress.md resource sections manually\n- **Name handoff evidence with task scope** — keep the task ID in the resource path, for example `reports/TASK-260218-abc_architecture-design.md`, so `task-board handoff` can recognize it\n- **Add descriptions** — the `description=` param adds a one-line description shown in spawn prompts\n\n---\n\n# Bugs\n\nBugs are elements of the board, living inside stories alongside tasks.\n\n```bash\n# Create bug\ntask-board m 'create(type=bug, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=crash-on-start, description=\"App crashes when starting recording\")'\n\n# Track like any element\ntask-board m 'set_status(BUG-260218-abc, status=development)'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Reproduce the issue\")'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Find root cause\")'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Write fix\")'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Add regression test\")'\n```\n# Negative Evidence Contract\n\nA passing suite means nothing unless something in it would have failed. This\nfile is the single source of truth for the rules that make a green check mean\nsomething, and for the incident record behind each one. The operative wording\nthat reaches every spawned child lives in the spawn template section\n`Evidence That Counts`; the reviewer's licence lives in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`;\nthe repeated-variant heuristic lives in `.roles/orchestrator/role.md`. Read this\nfile when you need the shapes, the failure vocabulary, or the incidents that\nproduced a rule — do not restate these rules in other documents.\n\n| Rule | Operative statement | Evidence record |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Negative tests for gates | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Narrowing, not deleting | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Production call site | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Absence vs failure to read | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Prove, or report nothing | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Reviewer licence to defeat a gate | `.roles/reviewer/role.md` | this file |\n| Many variants, one unowned decision | `.roles/orchestrator/role.md` | this file |\n\n## Negative Tests For Gates\n\nAny feature that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS must ship\nwith negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it is supposed to\nreject. A positive test proving the happy path passes is not evidence that the\ngate works; it is evidence that the gate is reachable.\n\nThis is the dominant defect class in this repository's 2026-07-28..08-03 waves.\nEvery instance below was found because a reviewer deliberately attacked the\ngate, not because anyone read the code:\n\n- the fact-assay attested a COLD child that never inherited the context, because\n  it checked inventory, session id, digests, and output shape, but never\n  inheritance;\n- the assay fork had repository tool access, so it could answer from disk while\n  the base was false;\n- the builder attestation accepted a correctly-shaped forged digest that the\n  caller minted itself;\n- clearance enforcement existed but no production path called it, and counting\n  tests around a fake proved nothing;\n- an unknown assay event preserved a false zero-tool observation;\n- a provider capability was declared absent to disable a flaky check, and the\n  claim did not reproduce (9/11 success).\n\n## Standard Negative Shapes\n\nUse these names in checklists, verdicts, and test names. They are the shapes a\nnegative test must cover and the shapes a reviewer looks for first.\n\n| Shape | What it looks like when nobody tests for it | What the negative test must do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Forged or self-minted evidence | The caller supplies the proof that the gate checks, and a correctly-shaped value passes. | Mint the evidence in the test the way a caller could, and require refusal. |\n| Absent evidence treated as satisfied | Missing input takes the permissive branch; the suite only ever supplies the input. | Remove the evidence entirely and require refusal, not a default-pass. |\n| Check present but uncalled from production | The guard is implemented and unit-tested by direct call; no production path invokes it. | Drive the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. |\n| Bypass path around the check | One entry point enforces; a second, older, or retry/renewal path does not. | Exercise every entry point that reaches the protected state, including retry, renewal, resume, and recovery, and every delivery surface that carries the rule — a contract clause reaching one context profile and not the other is this shape. |\n| Capability claim that does not reproduce | A capability is declared absent or present to make a flaky check go away. | Re-run the claim enough times to state a real rate, or report unknown. |\n\n## Prove A Bound By Narrowing, Not By Deleting\n\nA test that removes a gate entirely proves only that the gate exists. It says\nnothing about whether the gate covers the right class. Restrict the gate to a\nnarrower condition instead and require a named test to fail.\n\nTwo repository incidents where a narrowed gate survived a green suite:\n\n- **BUG-260801-3kiloe F1** — the recovery successor cap was proven only by total\n  removal. Narrowing it to provider-limit signals left every other recoverable\n  failure unbounded, with the whole suite still green.\n- **TASK-260802-17wetr F16** — thirteen cycles later, the same shape: the\n  probe-lease cap survived deletion tests while the renewal path escaped it.\n- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 1** — this contract itself. The reviewer clauses\n  landed in the role body and the shipped test checked the role file and the\n  lite template separately, so both halves were green while the composed lite\n  prompt carried neither. Assert on the composed production artifact, not on the\n  pieces it is built from.\n- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 2** — the same contract, one level up, and the\n  clearest statement of the difference between the two shapes. The cycle-1 fix\n  did assert on the composed prompt, but it called the composition helper\n  directly and handed it the role, task-class filter, and profile itself.\n  Production builds those inputs inside the spawn entry point, so a mutant\n  narrowing only the real lite-reviewer routing just before composition left the\n  test green while an actual lite reviewer lost its gate-defeat licence. A test\n  that supplies the inputs is testing the helper, not the path. Obtain the\n  artifact the way the consumer does — here, read the prompt file recorded on\n  the run manifest after driving the entry point — so role resolution, the\n  task-class filter, and profile resolution are all under test rather than\n  assumed.\n\nThree traps when running the mutants themselves, all hit while proving this file:\n\n- **The mutant must be re-run with `-count=1`.** `go test` caches on package\n  sources; a test that reads `.roles/*.md` or any file outside its package at\n  runtime returns a stale cached PASS after that file is mutated. A cached green\n  is not a surviving mutant, it is an unexecuted test.\n- **A passing assertion may be satisfied by the wrong source.** Two mutants of\n  the producer DoD entries survived because the phrase asserted also appears,\n  differently worded, in the shared spawn template. Choose assertion wording\n  that only the intended source can supply, then narrow that source and require\n  the named test to fail.\n- **Restore the mutant from a copy, not from git.** `git checkout <file>`\n  restores from the index, which silently discards uncommitted work in that file\n  — during this task it reverted the whole cycle-2 change, not just the mutant.\n  Copy the file aside before mutating, restore from the copy, and verify the\n  restoration by diffing against it before rerunning the gates.\n\n## An Absence And A Failure To Read Are Different Facts\n\nAbsence is a legitimate state with a defined fallback. A failed, partial, or\nmalformed read is evidence of a problem. Treating the second as the first lets\nthe fallback launder a fault into a normal-looking value, and a fallback defined\nfor absence must never fire on a read failure.\n\nEach of these was a separate defect in this repository:\n\n- a non-empty value read as verified;\n- an unknown scanner version read as clean;\n- a partial provenance record read as absent;\n- a zero pid start time read as alive;\n- a malformed `api_error_status` read as missing;\n- an unparsed envelope read as no envelope — this one produced a false provider\n  suppression that would have hidden a healthy provider for hours.\n\n## Prove, Or Report Nothing\n\nWhere a property cannot be established, report unparsed or unknown rather than\ninferring it from a proxy signal. A plausible guess is worse than an explicit\nunknown, because callers act on it.\n\nIncident: headless top-level inference in the Claude classifier was unsound in\nprinciple and produced six variants before it was deleted rather than\nstrengthened.\n\n## Many Variants, One Unowned Decision\n\nA long run of same-shaped variants usually means a decision has more than one\nowner, not that there are many bugs. Eleven framing variants stopped reproducing\nonly when structure got a single scanner and a single state type; every per-site\npatch before that produced the next variant. The operative wording is in\n`.roles/orchestrator/role.md`: unify the decision, do not patch each site.\n\n## Reviewer Licence\n\nReviewers are explicitly licensed to attempt to defeat a gate and to reject a\nchange whose only evidence is positive-path. Grep for callers before believing a\nguard is in force. The operative wording is in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`.\nMM .gitignore\nMM README.md\nMM go.mod\nD  go.sum\nD  internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go\nD  internal/canonicaljson/canonical_test.go\nMM internal/catalog/catalog.go\nMM internal/catalog/catalog.v0.5.0.json\nMM internal/catalog/catalog_gen.go\nMM internal/catalog/catalog_test.go\nMM internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go\nMM internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main_test.go\nMM internal/cataloggen/generate.go\nMM internal/cataloggen/generate_test.go\nD  internal/scalar/integer.go\nD  internal/scalar/names.go\nD  internal/scalar/path.go\nD  internal/scalar/scalar.go\nD  internal/scalar/scalar_test.go\nD  internal/scalar/time_digest.go\nD  internal/scalar/uuid.go\nMM internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go\nMM internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json\nMM internal/traceability/traceability.go\nMM internal/traceability/traceability_test.go\nMM task-board.config.json\n?? go.sum\n?? internal/canonicaljson/\n?? internal/scalar/\ntask-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e\nfb61f3d (HEAD -> task-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e) TASK-260830-236x9n: TASK-260830-236x9n: implement-jcs-and-self-identities\n006ba4e TASK-260830-1pbx0c: TASK-260830-1pbx0c: implement-common-scalar-types\nc9e5290 (origin/story/normative-pin-scope-completion, story/normative-pin-scope-completion) Record STORY-260831-2xdhn9 board state\n60c1b62 STORY-260831-2xdhn9: STORY-260831-2xdhn9: normative-pin-scope-completion\n3679514 (origin/task/close-landing-gate-task, task/close-landing-gate-task, task-board/story/STORY-260831-2xdhn9) Close landing gate configuration task\nREADME.md\ninternal/scalar/scalar_test.go\ninternal/scalar/path.go\ninternal/scalar/uuid.go\ninternal/scalar/names.go\ninternal/scalar/integer.go\ninternal/scalar/scalar.go\ninternal/scalar/time_digest.go\ninternal/scalar/fuzz_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/golden_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/validation_config_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/canonical.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/canonical_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/fuzz_test.go\ninternal/traceability/traceability.go\ninternal/traceability/traceability_test.go\ninternal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go\ninternal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main.go\ninternal/catalog/catalog_gen.go\ninternal/catalog/catalog_test.go\ninternal/catalog/catalog.go\ninternal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main_test.go\ninternal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go\ninternal/cataloggen/generate_test.go\ninternal/cataloggen/generate.go\ninternal/specpin/pin_test.go\ninternal/specpin/pin.go\ninternal/specpin/scope_test.go\ninternal/specpin/sections.go\ngo.mod\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Statuses\n\n9 statuses organized in a workflow:\n\n| Status | Meaning | Who sets |\n|--------|---------|----------|\n| **backlog** | Captured, no work yet | anyone |\n| **analysis** | Researching, decomposing | anyone |\n| **to-dev** | Ready for development | orchestrator |\n| **development** | Writing code | sub-agent |\n| **to-review** | Code ready for review | sub-agent |\n| **reviewing** | Reviewer child is checking the work | reviewer |\n| **done** | Reviewed and accepted leaf work, or computed container completion | reviewer / orchestrator after reviewer verdict; parent aggregation |\n| **closed** | Explicit administrative disposition: cancelled, obsolete, or won't do (reason in notes) | anyone |\n| **blocked** | Concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision (full evidence packet in notes/resource) | anyone |\n\n## Status Flow\n\n**Happy path:**\n```\nbacklog → analysis → to-dev → development → to-review → reviewing → done\n```\n\n**Returns from reviewing:**\n- `reviewing → analysis` — need more research\n- `reviewing → to-dev` — code issues, needs rework\n\n**Terminal from any status:**\n- `any → closed` — won't do / cancelled\n- `any → blocked` — concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision\n\n**Note on closed:** Use `closed` for cancelled, obsolete, or won't-do items. Don't delete — close with a note explaining why. History is preserved, and the item can be reopened if needed.\n\nFor a leaf task or bug, `done` means its delivery was accepted and `closed`\nmeans it was explicitly disposed without accepted delivery. A leaf may be\nclosed from any lifecycle state.\n\nFor a story or epic, `done` is computed completion: every direct child is\n`done` or `closed`. `closed` is an explicit administrative disposition of the\ncontainer itself. A container may be closed only when every direct and\ntransitive descendant is already `done` or `closed`; an empty container also\nsatisfies this rule. Close or complete descendant work first, then close\ncontainers from the inside out. `task-board validate` reports\n`PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` for stored closed containers that still contain active\ndescendants.\n\nValidation is strict by default for empty containers too: an empty Story/Epic\nstored as `done` aggregates to `backlog` and is reported. A migrated local\nboard may explicitly set\n`task-board.config.json -> validation.allow_legacy_empty_done_containers` to\n`true` to suppress only that historical empty-`done` mismatch. Empty active\ncontainers and every container with one or more children remain strict.\n\n**Don't delete elements.** Use `closed` instead of `delete`. Deletion loses history and context. The only exception is removing test/junk data during board setup.\n\n**Orchestrator review routing flow:** When a producer child sets `to-review`:\n1. Orchestrator confirms the producer attached task-scoped outcome evidence.\n2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer child with explicit selection, for example `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.\n3. Reviewer child sets/keeps `reviewing`, reads artifacts/code, and writes the verdict.\n4. If accepted → `done`. If changes are requested → `to-dev` (code rework) or `analysis` (needs more research), with notes/outcome evidence.\n4a. When the reviewer was launched against a Change Request (a managed Story workspace run), acceptance is recorded by `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=...)` instead. That mutation sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, and parks the element at `to-review` as the **accepted handoff** — accepted work never stays in `reviewing`. The Orchestrator then runs `task-board worktree checkpoint <ID>` for a **non-final** leaf, which commits the accepted candidate onto the Story branch and makes the `done` transition with `commit_ack=scope_committed`. A Change Request with `repository_delta=empty` skips the commit and still reaches `done` by the same command. `accept_cr` accepts no `commit_ack` argument, its `evidence` must be a task-scoped outcome resource this reviewer run attached or updated (naming the producer's own `_results.md` is refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`), only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may call it (anything else is refused with `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`), and the live task/reviewer checklist must be complete. All refusals precede CR, status, and activity writes.\n\n4b. **`checkpoint` never closes the Story.** When the leaf is the Story's last open child, closing it would promote the Story to `done` in the same mutation and cover that transition with `commit_ack=scope_committed` — attesting that the Story's scope is committed while its branch has never reached trunk. `worktree checkpoint` refuses that with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint` and names `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>`, which lands the branch before making the transition. The refusal is a scope rule, not an acknowledgement rule: it applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it leaves the branch, the accepted record and the board untouched so the integration path can still use them.\n5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.\n6. If a child exits early or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, the Orchestrator inspects the run/outcomes and retries or reroutes through a focused child. Recoverable work is never handed to the human.\n7. A `blocked` verdict stops routing only when it identifies a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision and records the full evidence packet; otherwise retry or reroute.\n\n`reviewing` is a real state, not a pass-through. A reviewer child must do actual review work between the producer handoff and `done`; the orchestrator routes and monitors that review instead of performing the verdict inline.\n\n`reviewing` is never a verdict, including under the Change Request flow. A reviewer run that ends at `reviewing` with no accepted Change Request is cancelled and a successor reviewer is spawned — that is what distinguishes \"the reviewer decided nothing\" from \"the reviewer decided\", and `accept_cr` does not change it. The accepted branch is derived from the Change Request record, not from the `reviewing` status.\n\n`to-review` therefore carries two meanings that the record disambiguates: a producer handoff awaiting review, and an accepted Change Request awaiting orchestrator checkpoint or integration. `task-board worktree status` and the Change Request state say which.\n\nA tracked spawned producer cannot use direct `set_status(..., status=to-review)`\nfor its assigned element. That workflow guardrail returns a typed remediation to\nrun `task-board handoff ELEMENT --role ROLE`, which verifies checklist and\noutcome evidence. Untracked operator status administration is unchanged.\n\n`to-review` is an internal routing trigger, never a handoff of routine review to the human. Human input is reserved for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision.\n\nHuman-facing wording follows the board state. `to-review` means \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\"; it is not \"done\", \"complete\", \"finished\", \"final\", or \"готово\". Say a task is done only after the board item is actually `done`.\n\n## Per-Element Review Policy\n\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with\n`create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with\n`set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` query\nprojection. New elements and legacy elements without the field default to\n`required`.\n\n- `required` keeps the producer -> reviewer -> rework/reviewer loop. Only an\n  accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but selection should favor a cheaper suitable effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates.\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff from `to-review`\n  directly to `done` only after checking acceptance criteria, checklist,\n  validation results, and outcome evidence. Missing evidence returns to the\n  producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\n\nThis policy changes reviewer routing, not the need for board-owned evidence, and\nnever introduces routine human review.\n\n## blocked vs is_blocked\n\nTwo different concepts:\n\n- **blocked** (status) — explicit stop-the-line status for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Notes or an outcome resource must record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.\n- **is_blocked** (computed) — from `blocked-by` dependencies; if dependency is not `done`/`closed`, element is blocked\n\nCLI display:\n```\nTASK-05: to-dev [BLOCKED by TASK-02]     ← computed from dependency\nTASK-07: blocked                          ← explicit status\n```\n\n## Automatic Parent Status Aggregation\n\nAfter a child status mutation, the CLI recomputes every ancestor from its direct\nchildren. It is derived state: an unassigned story or epic may follow an\nassigned child without implying ownership of the container.\n\n- If every child is `done` or `closed`, a non-closed parent becomes `done`.\n  An already explicit `closed` parent stays `closed`; terminal child updates do\n  not silently normalize it to `done`.\n- Otherwise the parent adopts the highest-priority active child status:\n  `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, then `backlog`.\n  `blocked` children do not select a parent status; a parent with only blocked\n  children keeps its current manual status.\n- The recomputation can promote, escalate, reopen, or demote a parent, then\n  continues through all ancestors. A parent that is `blocked` explicitly is not\n  recomputed.\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=development)'\n# TASK-260205-abc123 → development\n# STORY-260205-def456 → development (auto-escalated)\n# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → development (auto-escalated)\n```\n\nFor Task/Bug records created with the current format, the first transition into\n`development` also requires an explicit estimated value. Set it first with\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The refusal\nis a typed estimate-required error, parallel to the unfinished-dependency start\nrefusal. Records whose legacy `progress.md` has no Estimate section are\ngrandfathered and are not retroactively blocked; an idempotent status write for\nan element already in `development` is not treated as a new start.\n\n### Commit confirmation for accepted work\n\nThis gate exists so the commit-owning mover lays out its own delivered scope\nas atomic, project-perspective commits and honestly acknowledges that scope is\ncommitted — it is not a human checkpoint. An Orchestrator (or other\ncommit-owning mover) must not stop autonomous work to ask the human how to\nsplit commits, or whether to acknowledge, on every commit or close. The mover\ndecides its own commit decomposition — how many commits, per-story or\nfeature-clustered, ordering, buildability — and commits that scope itself,\nbackdated per the project's commit-time policy when one is configured. Human\nauthorization is the standing grant to commit work, not a per-commit\napproval.\n\nThis section is the single source for that contract. Role bodies, the spawn\ntemplates, and the router carry only the ownership statement and a link back\nhere; they must not restate the mechanics below.\n\nTop-level `task-board.config.json -> version_control.confirm` defaults to\n`false`. When it is `true`, `desired_commit_time` is required. Any mutation\nthat would move a Bug or Story to `done` must then include the exact mover-local\nacknowledgement `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This covers a direct Bug/Story\ntransition and a Task/Bug terminal transition that automatically promotes its\nparent Story.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version_control\": {\n    \"confirm\": true,\n    \"desired_commit_time\": \"evening MSK\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260205-def456, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'\n```\n\nThe error reports the transition-causing element, every enforced Bug/Story\ntransition, the owner message, and the configured desired time. The\nacknowledgement covers only the mover's scope; foreign uncommitted work is\nallowed and task-board performs no git clean-tree check. Never acknowledge\n`commit_ack=scope_committed` when the mover's own scope is genuinely\nuncommitted — commit it first, then acknowledge. Local evaluation and\nstatus writes share one mutation lock. In remote mode an enforcing CLI first\nrequires the server's `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1` capability, then\nthe server evaluates the acknowledgement and writes the child/ancestor statuses\nunder one serialized mutation.\n\nA reviewer-archetype spawned run must not supply `commit_ack`. It records\nacceptance evidence and hands the accepted verdict to the mover that owns the\nscoped commit (typically the Orchestrator) — never to the human, and it must\nnot escalate the commit-or-acknowledge decision to the human itself; that\nmover commits first, then makes the final `done` transition with\n`commit_ack=scope_committed`.\n\nBoard structure decides how often this gate fires on an unfinished parent; the\nrule that keeps it honest lives with the other decomposition rules in\n[planning workflow](planning-workflow.md).\n\nReactivation follows one deterministic rule: adding a descendant creates it in\n`backlog`, and adding or changing any descendant to an aggregating active status\nruns ordinary aggregation through every ancestor. Any `done` or `closed`\ncontainer on that path reopens to the highest-priority active aggregate status;\nexplicit closure is not sticky once descendant work becomes active. The target\nis the aggregate child status, not a fixed `development` state:\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=to-dev)'\n# TASK-260205-abc123 → to-dev\n# STORY-260205-def456 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\n# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\n```\n\nAutomatic container transitions do not require an assignee. Hard dependencies\nstill gate automatic changes to `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, and\n`done`: if a container has an unfinished blocker, its derived status is left\nunchanged and the child mutation succeeds. Direct manual transitions use the\nsame dependency gate; they additionally require an assignee for `to-dev`,\n`development`, and `to-review`.\n\n`blocked` remains the existing non-aggregating exception: blocked children do\nnot select a parent status. Closed ancestors remain closed rather than being\nsilently normalized to `done`, so validation reports every affected container\nthat still has a blocked descendant. The operator must reopen the container or\nresolve/close that descendant work.\n\n## Story Activation Reminders\n\nAn `agent_reminders` array is added to the JSON envelope of a production\nmutation whenever that mutation's persisted changes include a Story\ntransitioning out of `backlog`. It is **additive advisory output**: it never\nfetches Git, never mutates the board beyond what the mutation already did, and\nnever replaces the authoritative freshness gate documented in\n[task-board config](task-board-config.md#trunk-discovery-authority-and-refusals) —\na reminder can render every authority field `null` and the mutation still\nsucceeds.\n\n### Trigger\n\nA change qualifies when all of the following hold:\n\n- `element_id` starts with `STORY-` (case-insensitive).\n- `field` is `status` and the change's recorded `old_value` is exactly\n  `backlog`.\n- The new status is `analysis`, `to-dev`, or `development`. Every other\n  target status — `to-review`, `reviewing`, `blocked`, `done`, `closed`, or a\n  `backlog → backlog` no-op — does not qualify, and neither does a\n  reactivation from `done` or `closed` (only `backlog` is the qualifying old\n  value).\n\nBoth the mutation's primary change and every side effect are scanned, so a\nTask/Bug mutation that auto-escalates its containing Story from `backlog`\nstill emits the Story's reminder even though the Task/Bug itself never\nqualifies on its own ID prefix.\n\n### Deduplication and order\n\nWithin one mutation's full change list (primary change first, then side\neffects in their recorded order), only the first qualifying change per Story\nID produces a reminder — a batch that touches the same Story's status twice\n(for example a reopen side effect followed by the requested transition) still\nemits exactly one reminder for it. Distinct qualifying Story IDs each get\ntheir own reminder, in first-seen order.\n\n### Payload shape\n\n```json\n{\n  \"code\": \"story_worktree_base_preflight_required\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-29T12:34:56.123456789Z\",\n  \"message\": \"Resolve and refresh the configured integration branch upstream, compare exact OIDs, and record the selected Story worktree base before provisioning or trusting it.\",\n  \"story_id\": \"STORY-260218-bbbbbb\",\n  \"trunk_ref\": \"refs/heads/main\",\n  \"trunk_oid\": \"8c3f1a9...\",\n  \"upstream_ref\": \"refs/remotes/protected/main\",\n  \"upstream_oid\": \"5e2b7d0...\",\n  \"selected_base_oid\": null\n}\n```\n\n`code`, `message`, and the RFC3339Nano UTC `timestamp` are stable across every\nreminder in one mutation (one clock read per mutation, not per Story).\n`trunk_ref`, `trunk_oid`, `upstream_ref`, `upstream_oid`, and\n`selected_base_oid` are always present as JSON keys and are explicit `null`,\nnever omitted, whenever that specific piece of evidence is not known. The\n`agent_reminders` key itself is only added to the result when at least one\nchange qualified; a mutation with no qualifying activation adds no key at all\n(not an empty array).\n\n### Authority evidence: local versus remote\n\nThe five nullable fields come from a cache-only projection that is read after\nthe mutation persists and never fails the mutation on error:\n\n- **Local CLI** — prefers a persisted workspace freshness record from a prior\n  `task-board spawn` for that Story (`trunk_ref`/`trunk_oid` = the recorded\n  integration branch and its local OID at spawn time, `upstream_ref`/\n  `upstream_oid` = the recorded upstream ref/OID, `selected_base_oid` = the\n  OID the workspace actually branched from). When no such record exists, it\n  falls back to a cache-only Git discovery (`DiscoverCachedTrunk`) against the\n  configured `integration_base_branch` — the same discovery order as the\n  freshness gate, but it never fetches, so `upstream_oid` reflects whatever\n  the last fetch already cached — and `selected_base_oid` is `null`, because\n  a cache-only projection names no provisioned workspace. Any read failure\n  (unreadable tracking config, unreadable ref) renders every field `null`\n  rather than failing the mutation.\n- **Remote (board-server)** — the reminder is produced by the identical shared\n  `pkg/board/query` mutation code, so it fires on the same triggers with the\n  same dedup, but the server wires no authority source (there is no local Git\n  checkout to read), so every one of the five fields is always `null`; only\n  `code`, `timestamp`, `message`, and `story_id` are populated.\n\n### Mutation coverage\n\n`agent_reminders` is decorated by one shared boundary that a subset of\nproduction mutations route their persisted changes through:\n\n| Covered — can emit a reminder | Not wired — structurally cannot emit |\n| --- | --- |\n| `set_status` | `normalize_legacy_status` |\n| `handoff` | `set_estimate` |\n| `create` (only via its ancestor-reopen side effects) | `set_details`, `rename` |\n| `delete` | `check_item`, `uncheck_item`, `add_checklist_item` |\n| `set_parent` | `set_notes` |\n| `assign`, `unassign`, `link`, `unlink` (wired, but their own changes are never `status` changes, so they only ever forward an ancestor side effect) | context mutations |\n\n`assign`/`unassign`/`link`/`unlink` are listed on the covered side because\nthey share the same decorator plumbing as `set_status`; they simply never\nproduce a qualifying change themselves. The \"not wired\" column takes no `cfg`\nat all in `registerMutations`, so it cannot mint a reminder even if a caller\ncrafted a matching change shape.\n\n## Landing a Story on trunk\n\nThis section extends the commit-confirmation contract above to the one path\nwhere a `done` transition is produced by a command rather than typed by hand:\n`task-board worktree integrate`, which lands a Story developed in a managed\nStory workspace. The mechanics of the managed workspace itself live in\n[tracked background spawn](tracked-background-spawn.md#story-worktree-isolation);\nwhat belongs here is the ordering and the exact scope of the acknowledgement.\n\n**Accepted comes before done, always.** A reviewer records acceptance with\n`accept_cr`, which parks the element at `to-review` as the accepted handoff. No\nGit operation has happened at that point and nothing is `done`. The\nOrchestrator then integrates, and the `done` transition is the *last* board\neffect of a run that has already moved trunk. The superseded order — close the\nboard first, land the work afterwards — is withdrawn, because it produced an\nacknowledgement that attested a scope no repository had yet seen.\n\n```bash\ntask-board worktree integrate STORY-260205-def456 \\\n  --cr TASK-260205-abc123 --revision 3 \\\n  --commit-time 2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00\n```\n\n**The landing gate defaults to refusing.** `integrate` requires the\n`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` suite to be configured; with no\nsuite it refuses `validation_not_configured` before any ref moves. A project\nwith nothing to run states that explicitly as `{\"commands\": []}`. See\n[task-board config](task-board-config.md#the-landing-gate-suite).\n\n**Squash, fast-forward-only, and one explicit time.** The accepted Story\ncandidate lands as one squash commit on trunk; the branch's internal Task\ncheckpoints are discarded rather than replayed, and trunk is only ever\nfast-forwarded. When `version_control.confirm` is `true`, `--commit-time` is\nrequired, must parse as RFC3339, and is applied to the author date and the\ncommitter date of both commits this command creates. The example above is this\nrepository's own policy expressed concretely: the previous day at 20:01 MSK,\nthat is `2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00` for an integration run on 2026-08-07. With\nconfirmation disabled and no flag, the current time is used instead.\n\n**What the acknowledgement asserts here, and what it does not.** The command\nsupplies `commit_ack=scope_committed` on the `done` transition itself, and that\ntransition is not reachable until the Story's squash commit is genuinely\ncontained in trunk — a resumed run whose trunk no longer carries the recorded\ncandidate refuses rather than stamping the acknowledgement. So the assertion is\nexactly: *the repository scope of this Story is on trunk.* It is narrower than\na clean tree in three specific ways, and each of them is a normal, expected\nstate at the moment the acknowledgement is written:\n\n- **The Story's own board delta is still uncommitted.** The board-state commit\n  is created *after* the transition, precisely because the transition is what\n  produces the board bytes it records.\n- **Board paths outside this Story are untouched.** The second commit stages an\n  explicit pathspec: this Story's own subtree, the resource directories of its\n  elements, and the ancestor aggregation files on its chain. Everything else\n  under `.task-board/` — `.task-board/.goals/**` and every other Story's\n  subtree — stays exactly as it was: ordinary uncommitted working-tree state\n  that this command neither examines nor stages.\n- **Foreign uncommitted work is untouched**, on the same rule the section above\n  states for a hand-typed acknowledgement.\n\n**The two commits, in order.** First the squash commit carrying the accepted\ncandidate tree; then the `done` transition; then a second, board-only commit\ntitled `Record <STORY-ID> board state`. A Story whose accepted candidate has no\nrepository delta produces no first commit and still produces the second one —\nits board record is the whole of its work product.\n\n**Recovery is re-running the same command.** Integration keeps one durable\ntransaction record per Story, so a crashed or interrupted run resumes the\nrecorded phase instead of starting a second integration, and a completed one is\na no-op rather than a duplicate landing. A rollback is offered in exactly one\nphase — nothing landed yet, trunk unmoved — and is requested explicitly with\n`--rollback`; no later phase undoes a commit that is already on trunk. Partial\nintegration is therefore a state to resume, never a state to clean up by hand.\n# Resources\n\nResources are owned by the board element they are attached to. Use explicit\nCRUD operations against that element ID. Local payloads are grouped under\n`.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/`; remote board-server uses the equivalent\nelement-scoped filesystem or MinIO object-key prefix.\n\n## Resource Types\n\n| Type | Purpose | Example |\n|------|---------|---------|\n| **precondition** | Inputs needed before work starts | Design docs, specs, instructions, API schemas |\n| **outcome** | Results produced by work | Research findings, implementation notes, analysis |\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\n# Create a resource\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_design.md, type=precondition, content=\"...\", description=\"Architecture design\")'\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content=\"...\", description=\"Research findings\")'\n\n# Replace an existing resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content=\"revised findings\", description=\"Research findings v2\")'\ntask-board resource update TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_results.md --name reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome\n\n# Adopt an existing orphaned local payload without rewriting it\ntask-board resource adopt TASK-260218-abc revisions/2/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome --description \"Recovered result\"\n\n# Delete a resource (destructive — requires --confirm)\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md)'\ntask-board resource delete TASK-260218-abc artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md --confirm\n\n# View resources on an element\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { preconditionResources outcomeResources }'\n```\n\n## Element-Owned Storage\n\nResources from different tasks are not stored in one shared file pile. Each\nelement gets its own directory, and every resource attached to that element is\nstored below it:\n\n```\n.task-board/.resources/\n├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/\n│   ├── findings/\n│   │   └── investigation.md\n│   ├── artifacts/\n│   │   └── run-1/\n│   │       └── output.json\n│   ├── reports/\n│   │   └── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md\n│   └── revisions/\n│       └── 2/\n│           └── review.md\n└── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\n    └── research.md         # outcome\n```\n\nThe resource `name` is a safe forward-slash-relative path. Agents may choose\nthe hierarchy that fits the artifact and nest it further as needed. Every\nsegment must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and must not be `.`, `..`, an\nabsolute/drive-qualified path, or contain a backslash. Raw forward slashes are\nthe only hierarchy delimiter; malformed URL escapes and encoded or repeatedly\nencoded separators/traversal are rejected. Attach related files to the same\nowning element; do not invent a generic board-wide resource task or write\npayloads into `.task-board/.resources/` manually.\n\nDirectories are structural nodes, not payloads. Resource CRUD, validation, and\nhandoff evidence recognize regular files only; a directory named like a report\ncannot be adopted or used to satisfy outcome evidence.\n\nLocal and board-server filesystem element deletion is journaled and\ncrash-recoverable. Direct resource CRUD first resolves any pending staged\nelement delete under the board mutation lock. MinIO element/subtree deletion\ncurrently fails before mutation with the typed\n`ErrAtomicResourceDeleteUnsupported` capability error; ordinary resource CRUD\nremains available.\n\nReferences are tracked in `progress.md` as markdown links:\n```markdown\n## Precondition Resources\n- [specs/auth/design.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/precondition/specs/auth/design.md) — Architecture design\n\n## Outcome Resources\n- [reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/outcome/reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md) — Research findings\n```\n\n## Spawn Integration\n\nPrecondition resources are automatically injected into agent prompts by `task-board spawn`. Full prompts preserve normal text inlining. Lite prompts use the resolved `agent_context.prompt.preconditions` policy: by default, text below `inline_bytes` is rendered inline, while larger text and binary resources are written read-only under `.temp/resources/` and represented by their name, description, byte count, SHA-256 digest, and local path. This means:\n\n1. Attach instructions/specs as precondition resources to a task\n2. Run `task-board spawn TASK-XX --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — agent gets everything automatically\n3. Agent saves results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write, then `update_resource(...)` for revisions\n\n## Best Practices\n\n- **Use preconditions for agent instructions** — attach design docs, specs, or step-by-step instructions before spawning\n- **Use outcomes for work products** — research findings, analysis, implementation notes\n- **Attach to the owning element** — pass the Task, Bug, Story, or Epic ID that owns the artifact; the storage layer groups its payloads under that element directory automatically\n- **Use explicit create/update/delete** — `add_resource` / `resource add` must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` when replacing an existing payload\n- **Use adopt only for local repair** — `resource adopt` registers bytes already stored at `.resources/<elementID>/<relative-resource-path>` without rewriting them; a missing payload returns resource-not-found and an existing declaration returns resource-exists\n- **Choose a useful hierarchy inside the owning element** — `findings/`, `artifacts/`, `reports/`, and `revisions/` are conventions, not a fixed schema; nest further when it makes the handoff easier to navigate\n- **Use safe relative resource paths** — `/` separates validated path segments; empty, dot/traversal, absolute/drive-qualified, backslash, control-character, malformed escape, and encoded/repeatedly encoded separator or traversal forms are rejected in local and remote storage\n- **Attach files, not folders** — directories only organize resource files and never satisfy resource existence or handoff evidence\n- **Always use board commands** — never create/edit resource files or progress.md resource sections manually\n- **Name handoff evidence with task scope** — keep the task ID in the resource path, for example `reports/TASK-260218-abc_architecture-design.md`, so `task-board handoff` can recognize it\n- **Add descriptions** — the `description=` param adds a one-line description shown in spawn prompts\n\n---\n\n# Bugs\n\nBugs are elements of the board, living inside stories alongside tasks.\n\n```bash\n# Create bug\ntask-board m 'create(type=bug, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=crash-on-start, description=\"App crashes when starting recording\")'\n\n# Track like any element\ntask-board m 'set_status(BUG-260218-abc, status=development)'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Reproduce the issue\")'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Find root cause\")'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Write fix\")'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Add regression test\")'\n```\n# Negative Evidence Contract\n\nA passing suite means nothing unless something in it would have failed. This\nfile is the single source of truth for the rules that make a green check mean\nsomething, and for the incident record behind each one. The operative wording\nthat reaches every spawned child lives in the spawn template section\n`Evidence That Counts`; the reviewer's licence lives in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`;\nthe repeated-variant heuristic lives in `.roles/orchestrator/role.md`. Read this\nfile when you need the shapes, the failure vocabulary, or the incidents that\nproduced a rule — do not restate these rules in other documents.\n\n| Rule | Operative statement | Evidence record |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Negative tests for gates | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Narrowing, not deleting | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Production call site | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Absence vs failure to read | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Prove, or report nothing | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Reviewer licence to defeat a gate | `.roles/reviewer/role.md` | this file |\n| Many variants, one unowned decision | `.roles/orchestrator/role.md` | this file |\n\n## Negative Tests For Gates\n\nAny feature that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS must ship\nwith negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it is supposed to\nreject. A positive test proving the happy path passes is not evidence that the\ngate works; it is evidence that the gate is reachable.\n\nThis is the dominant defect class in this repository's 2026-07-28..08-03 waves.\nEvery instance below was found because a reviewer deliberately attacked the\ngate, not because anyone read the code:\n\n- the fact-assay attested a COLD child that never inherited the context, because\n  it checked inventory, session id, digests, and output shape, but never\n  inheritance;\n- the assay fork had repository tool access, so it could answer from disk while\n  the base was false;\n- the builder attestation accepted a correctly-shaped forged digest that the\n  caller minted itself;\n- clearance enforcement existed but no production path called it, and counting\n  tests around a fake proved nothing;\n- an unknown assay event preserved a false zero-tool observation;\n- a provider capability was declared absent to disable a flaky check, and the\n  claim did not reproduce (9/11 success).\n\n## Standard Negative Shapes\n\nUse these names in checklists, verdicts, and test names. They are the shapes a\nnegative test must cover and the shapes a reviewer looks for first.\n\n| Shape | What it looks like when nobody tests for it | What the negative test must do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Forged or self-minted evidence | The caller supplies the proof that the gate checks, and a correctly-shaped value passes. | Mint the evidence in the test the way a caller could, and require refusal. |\n| Absent evidence treated as satisfied | Missing input takes the permissive branch; the suite only ever supplies the input. | Remove the evidence entirely and require refusal, not a default-pass. |\n| Check present but uncalled from production | The guard is implemented and unit-tested by direct call; no production path invokes it. | Drive the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. |\n| Bypass path around the check | One entry point enforces; a second, older, or retry/renewal path does not. | Exercise every entry point that reaches the protected state, including retry, renewal, resume, and recovery, and every delivery surface that carries the rule — a contract clause reaching one context profile and not the other is this shape. |\n| Capability claim that does not reproduce | A capability is declared absent or present to make a flaky check go away. | Re-run the claim enough times to state a real rate, or report unknown. |\n\n## Prove A Bound By Narrowing, Not By Deleting\n\nA test that removes a gate entirely proves only that the gate exists. It says\nnothing about whether the gate covers the right class. Restrict the gate to a\nnarrower condition instead and require a named test to fail.\n\nTwo repository incidents where a narrowed gate survived a green suite:\n\n- **BUG-260801-3kiloe F1** — the recovery successor cap was proven only by total\n  removal. Narrowing it to provider-limit signals left every other recoverable\n  failure unbounded, with the whole suite still green.\n- **TASK-260802-17wetr F16** — thirteen cycles later, the same shape: the\n  probe-lease cap survived deletion tests while the renewal path escaped it.\n- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 1** — this contract itself. The reviewer clauses\n  landed in the role body and the shipped test checked the role file and the\n  lite template separately, so both halves were green while the composed lite\n  prompt carried neither. Assert on the composed production artifact, not on the\n  pieces it is built from.\n- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 2** — the same contract, one level up, and the\n  clearest statement of the difference between the two shapes. The cycle-1 fix\n  did assert on the composed prompt, but it called the composition helper\n  directly and handed it the role, task-class filter, and profile itself.\n  Production builds those inputs inside the spawn entry point, so a mutant\n  narrowing only the real lite-reviewer routing just before composition left the\n  test green while an actual lite reviewer lost its gate-defeat licence. A test\n  that supplies the inputs is testing the helper, not the path. Obtain the\n  artifact the way the consumer does — here, read the prompt file recorded on\n  the run manifest after driving the entry point — so role resolution, the\n  task-class filter, and profile resolution are all under test rather than\n  assumed.\n\nThree traps when running the mutants themselves, all hit while proving this file:\n\n- **The mutant must be re-run with `-count=1`.** `go test` caches on package\n  sources; a test that reads `.roles/*.md` or any file outside its package at\n  runtime returns a stale cached PASS after that file is mutated. A cached green\n  is not a surviving mutant, it is an unexecuted test.\n- **A passing assertion may be satisfied by the wrong source.** Two mutants of\n  the producer DoD entries survived because the phrase asserted also appears,\n  differently worded, in the shared spawn template. Choose assertion wording\n  that only the intended source can supply, then narrow that source and require\n  the named test to fail.\n- **Restore the mutant from a copy, not from git.** `git checkout <file>`\n  restores from the index, which silently discards uncommitted work in that file\n  — during this task it reverted the whole cycle-2 change, not just the mutant.\n  Copy the file aside before mutating, restore from the copy, and verify the\n  restoration by diffing against it before rerunning the gates.\n\n## An Absence And A Failure To Read Are Different Facts\n\nAbsence is a legitimate state with a defined fallback. A failed, partial, or\nmalformed read is evidence of a problem. Treating the second as the first lets\nthe fallback launder a fault into a normal-looking value, and a fallback defined\nfor absence must never fire on a read failure.\n\nEach of these was a separate defect in this repository:\n\n- a non-empty value read as verified;\n- an unknown scanner version read as clean;\n- a partial provenance record read as absent;\n- a zero pid start time read as alive;\n- a malformed `api_error_status` read as missing;\n- an unparsed envelope read as no envelope — this one produced a false provider\n  suppression that would have hidden a healthy provider for hours.\n\n## Prove, Or Report Nothing\n\nWhere a property cannot be established, report unparsed or unknown rather than\ninferring it from a proxy signal. A plausible guess is worse than an explicit\nunknown, because callers act on it.\n\nIncident: headless top-level inference in the Claude classifier was unsound in\nprinciple and produced six variants before it was deleted rather than\nstrengthened.\n\n## Many Variants, One Unowned Decision\n\nA long run of same-shaped variants usually means a decision has more than one\nowner, not that there are many bugs. Eleven framing variants stopped reproducing\nonly when structure got a single scanner and a single state type; every per-site\npatch before that produced the next variant. The operative wording is in\n`.roles/orchestrator/role.md`: unify the decision, do not patch each site.\n\n## Reviewer Licence\n\nReviewers are explicitly licensed to attempt to defeat a gate and to reject a\nchange whose only evidence is positive-path. Grep for callers before believing a\nguard is in force. The operative wording is in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`.\nMM .gitignore\nMM README.md\nMM go.mod\nD  go.sum\nD  internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go\nD  internal/canonicaljson/canonical_test.go\nMM internal/catalog/catalog.go\nMM internal/catalog/catalog.v0.5.0.json\nMM internal/catalog/catalog_gen.go\nMM internal/catalog/catalog_test.go\nMM internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go\nMM internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main_test.go\nMM internal/cataloggen/generate.go\nMM internal/cataloggen/generate_test.go\nD  internal/scalar/integer.go\nD  internal/scalar/names.go\nD  internal/scalar/path.go\nD  internal/scalar/scalar.go\nD  internal/scalar/scalar_test.go\nD  internal/scalar/time_digest.go\nD  internal/scalar/uuid.go\nMM internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go\nMM internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json\nMM internal/traceability/traceability.go\nMM internal/traceability/traceability_test.go\nMM task-board.config.json\n?? go.sum\n?? internal/canonicaljson/\n?? internal/scalar/\ntask-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e\nfb61f3d (HEAD -> task-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e) TASK-260830-236x9n: TASK-260830-236x9n: implement-jcs-and-self-identities\n006ba4e TASK-260830-1pbx0c: TASK-260830-1pbx0c: implement-common-scalar-types\nc9e5290 (origin/story/normative-pin-scope-completion, story/normative-pin-scope-completion) Record STORY-260831-2xdhn9 board state\n60c1b62 STORY-260831-2xdhn9: STORY-260831-2xdhn9: normative-pin-scope-completion\n3679514 (origin/task/close-landing-gate-task, task/close-landing-gate-task, task-board/story/STORY-260831-2xdhn9) Close landing gate configuration task\nREADME.md\ninternal/scalar/scalar_test.go\ninternal/scalar/path.go\ninternal/scalar/uuid.go\ninternal/scalar/names.go\ninternal/scalar/integer.go\ninternal/scalar/scalar.go\ninternal/scalar/time_digest.go\ninternal/scalar/fuzz_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/golden_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/validation_config_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/canonical.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/canonical_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/fuzz_test.go\ninternal/traceability/traceability.go\ninternal/traceability/traceability_test.go\ninternal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go\ninternal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main.go\ninternal/catalog/catalog_gen.go\ninternal/catalog/catalog_test.go\ninternal/catalog/catalog.go\ninternal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main_test.go\ninternal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go\ninternal/cataloggen/generate_test.go\ninternal/cataloggen/generate.go\ninternal/specpin/pin_test.go\ninternal/specpin/pin.go\ninternal/specpin/scope_test.go\ninternal/specpin/sections.go\ngo.mod\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1708},"formatted_output":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 10954)\nTotal output lines: 750\n\n# Statuses\n\n9 statuses organized in a workflow:\n\n| Status | Meaning | Who sets |\n|--------|---------|----------|\n| **backlog** | Captured, no work yet | anyone |\n| **analysis** | Researching, decomposing | anyone |\n| **to-dev** | Ready for development | orchestrator |\n| **development** | Writing code | sub-agent |\n| **to-review** | Code ready for review | sub-agent |\n| **reviewing** | Reviewer child is checking the work | reviewer |\n| **done** | Reviewed and accepted leaf work, or computed container completion | reviewer / orchestrator after reviewer verdict; parent aggregation |\n| **closed** | Explicit administrative disposition: cancelled, obsolete, or won't do (reason in notes) | anyone |\n| **blocked** | Concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision (full evidence packet in notes/resource) | anyone |\n\n## Status Flow\n\n**Happy path:**\n```\nbacklog → analysis → to-dev → development → to-review → reviewing → done\n```\n\n**Returns from reviewing:**\n- `reviewing → analysis` — need more research\n- `reviewing → to-dev` — code issues, needs rework\n\n**Terminal from any status:**\n- `any → closed` — won't do / cancelled\n- `any → blocked` — concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision\n\n**Note on closed:** Use `closed` for cancelled, obsolete, or won't-do items. Don't delete — close with a note explaining why. History is preserved, and the item can be reopened if needed.\n\nFor a leaf task or bug, `done` means its delivery was accepted and `closed`\nmeans it was explicitly disposed without accepted delivery. A leaf may be\nclosed from any lifecycle state.\n\nFor a story or epic, `done` is computed completion: every direct child is\n`done` or `closed`. `closed` is an explicit administrative disposition of the\ncontainer itself. A container may be closed only when every direct and\ntransitive descendant is already `done` or `closed`; an empty container also\nsatisfies this rule. Close or complete descendant work first, then close\ncontainers from the inside out. `task-board validate` reports\n`PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` for stored closed containers that still contain active\ndescendants.\n\nValidation is strict by default for empty containers too: an empty Story/Epic\nstored as `done` aggregates to `backlog` and is reported. A migrated local\nboard may explicitly set\n`task-board.config.json -> validation.allow_legacy_empty_done_containers` to\n`true` to suppress only that historical empty-`done` mismatch. Empty active\ncontainers and every container with one or more children remain strict.\n\n**Don't delete elements.** Use `closed` instead of `delete`. Deletion loses history and context. The only exception is removing test/junk data during board setup.\n\n**Orchestrator review routing flow:** When a producer child sets `to-review`:\n1. Orchestrator confirms the producer attached task-scoped outcome evidence.\n2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer child with explicit selection, for example `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.\n3. Reviewer child sets/keeps `reviewing`, reads artifacts/code, and writes the verdict.\n4. If accepted → `done`. If changes are requested → `to-dev` (code rework) or `analysis` (needs more research), with notes/outcome evidence.\n4a. When the reviewer was launched against a Change Request (a managed Story workspace run), acceptance is recorded by `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=...)` instead. That mutation sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, and parks the element at `to-review` as the **accepted handoff** — accepted work never stays in `reviewing`. The Orchestrator then runs `task-board worktree checkpoint <ID>` for a **non-final** leaf, which commits the accepted candidate onto the Story branch and makes the `done` transition with `commit_ack=scope_committed`. A Change Request with `repository_delta=empty` skips the commit and still reaches `done` by the same command. `accept_cr` accepts no `commit_ack` argument, its `evidence` must be a task-scoped outcome resource this reviewer run attached or updated (naming the producer's own `_results.md` is refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`), only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may call it (anything else is refused with `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`), and the live task/reviewer checklist must be complete. All refusals precede CR, status, and activity writes.\n\n4b. **`checkpoint` never closes the Story.** When the leaf is the Story's last open child, closing it would promote the Story to `done` in the same mutation and cover that transition with `commit_ack=scope_committed` — attesting that the Story's scope is committed while its branch has never reached trunk. `worktree checkpoint` refuses that with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint` and names `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>`, which lands the branch before making the transition. The refusal is a scope rule, not an acknowledgement rule: it applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it leaves the branch, the accepted record and the board untouched so the integration path can still use them.\n5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.\n6. If a child exits early or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, the Orchestrator inspects the run/outcomes and retries or reroutes through a focused child. Recoverable work is never handed to the human.\n7. A `blocked` verdict stops routing only when it identifies a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision and records the full evidence packet; otherwise retry or reroute.\n\n`reviewing` is a real state, not a pass-through. A reviewer child must do actual review work between the producer handoff and `done`; the orchestrator routes and monitors that review instead of performing the verdict inline.\n\n`reviewing` is never a verdict, including under the Change Request flow. A reviewer run that ends at `reviewing` with no accepted Change Request is cancelled and a successor reviewer is spawned — that is what distinguishes \"the reviewer decided nothing\" from \"the reviewer decided\", and `accept_cr` does not change it. The accepted branch is derived from the Change Request record, not from the `reviewing` status.\n\n`to-review` therefore carries two meanings that the record disambiguates: a producer handoff awaiting review, and an accepted Change Request awaiting orchestrator checkpoint or integration. `task-board worktree status` and the Change Request state say which.\n\nA tracked spawned producer cannot use direct `set_status(..., status=to-review)`\nfor its assigned element. That workflow guardrail returns a typed remediation to\nrun `task-board handoff ELEMENT --role ROLE`, which verifies checklist and\noutcome evidence. Untracked operator status administration is unchanged.\n\n`to-review` is an internal routing trigger, never a handoff of routine review to the human. Human input is reserved for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision.\n\nHuman-facing wording follows the board state. `to-review` means \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\"; it is not \"done\", \"complete\", \"finished\", \"final\", or \"готово\". Say a task is done only after the board item is actually `done`.\n\n## Per-Element Review Policy\n\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with\n`create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with\n`set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` query\nprojection. New elements and legacy elements without the field default to\n`required`.\n\n- `required` keeps the producer -> reviewer -> rework/reviewer loop. Only an\n  accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but selection should favor a cheaper suitable effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates.\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff from `to-review`\n  directly to `done` only after checking acceptance criteria, checklist,\n  validation results, and outcome evidence. Missing evidence returns to the\n  producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\n\nThis policy changes reviewer routing, not the need for board-owned evidence, and\nnever introduces routine human review.\n\n## blocked vs is_blocked\n\nTwo different concepts:\n\n- **blocked** (status) — explicit stop-the-line status for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Notes or an outcome resource must record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.\n- **is_blocked** (computed) — from `blocked-by` dependencies; if dependency is not `done`/`closed`, element is blocked\n\nCLI display:\n```\nTASK-05: to-dev [BLOCKED by TASK-02]     ← computed from dependency\nTASK-07: blocked                          ← explicit status\n```\n\n## Automatic Parent Status Aggregation\n\nAfter a child status mutation, the CLI recomputes every ancestor from its direct\nchildren. It is derived state: an unassigned story or epic may follow an\nassigned child without implying ownership of the container.\n\n- If every child is `done` or `closed`, a non-closed parent becomes `done`.\n  An already explicit `closed` parent stays `closed`; terminal child updates do\n  not silently normalize it to `done`.\n- Otherwise the parent adopts the highest-priority active child status:\n  `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, then `backlog`.\n  `blocked` children do not select a parent status; a parent with only blocked\n  children keeps its current manual status.\n- The recomputation can promote, escalate, reopen, or demote a parent, then\n  continues through all ancestors. A parent that is `blocked` explicitly is not\n  recomputed.\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=development)'\n# TASK-260205-abc123 → development\n# STORY-260205-def456 → development (auto-escalated)\n# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → development (auto-escalated)\n```\n\nFor Task/Bug records created with the current format, the first transition into\n`development` also requires an explicit estimated value. Set it first with\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The refusal\nis a typed estimate-required error, parallel to the unfinished-dependency start\nrefusal. Records whose legacy `progress.md` has no Estimate section are\ngrandfathered and are not retroactively blocked; an idempotent status write for\nan element already in `development` is not treated as a new start.\n\n### Commit confirmation for accepted work\n\nThis gate exists so the commit-owning mover lays out its own delivered scope\nas atomic, project-perspective commits and honestly acknowledges that scope is\ncommitted — it is not a human checkpoint. An Orchestrator (or other\ncommit-owning mover) must not stop autonomous work to ask the human how to\nsplit commits, or whether to acknowledge, on every commit or close. The mover\ndecides its own commit decomposition — how many commits, per-story or\nfeature-clustered, ordering, buildability — and commits that scope itself,\nbackdated per the project's commit-time policy when one is configured. Human\nauthorization is the standing grant to commit work, not a per-commit\napproval.\n\nThis section is the single source for that contract. Role bodies, the spawn\ntemplates, and the router carry only the ownership statement and a link back\nhere; they must not restate the mechanics below.\n\nTop-level `task-board.config.json -> version_control.confirm` defaults to\n`false`. When it is `true`, `desired_commit_time` is required. Any mutation\nthat would move a Bug or Story to `done` must then include the exact mover-local\nacknowledgement `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This covers a direct Bug/Story\ntransition and a Task/Bug terminal transition that automatically promotes its\nparent Story.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version_control\": {\n    \"confirm\": true,\n    \"desired_commit_time\": \"evening MSK\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260205-def456, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'\n```\n\nThe error reports the transition-causing element, every enforced Bug/Story\ntransition, the owner message, and the configured desired time. The\nacknowledgement covers only the mover's scope; foreign uncommitted work is\nallowed and task-board performs no git clean-tree check. Never acknowledge\n`commit_ack=scope_committed` when the mover's own scope is genuinely\nuncommitted — commit it first, then acknowledge. Local evaluation and\nstatus writes share one mutation lock. In remote mode an enforcing CLI first\nrequires the server's `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1` capability, then\nthe server evaluates the acknowledgement and writes the child/ancestor statuses\nunder one serialized mutation.\n\nA reviewer-archetype spawned run must not supply `commit_ack`. It records\nacceptance evidence and hands the accepted verdict to the mover that owns the\nscoped commit (typically the Orchestrator) — never to the human, and it must\nnot escalate the commit-or-acknowledge decision to the human itself; that\nmover commits first, then makes the final `done` transition with\n`commit_ack=scope_committed`.\n\nBoard structure decides how often this gate fires on an unfinished parent; the\nrule that keeps it honest lives with the other decomposition rules in\n[planning workflow](planning-workflow.md).\n\nReactivation follows one deterministic rule: adding a descendant creates it in\n`backlog`, and adding or changing any descendant to an aggregating active status\nruns ordinary aggregation through every ancestor. Any `done` or `closed`\ncontainer on that path reopens to the highest-priority active aggregate status;\nexplicit closure is not sticky once descendant work becomes active. The target\nis the aggregate child status, not a fixed `development` state:\n\n```bash\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=to-dev)'\n# TASK-260205-abc123 → to-dev\n# STORY-260205-def456 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\n# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\n```\n\nAutomatic container transitions do not require an assignee. Hard dependencies\nstill gate automatic changes to `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, and\n`done`: if a container has an unfinished blocker, its derived status is left\nunchanged and the child mutation succeeds. Direct manual transitions use the\nsame dependency gate; they additionally require an assignee for `to-dev`,\n`development`, and `to-review`.\n\n`blocked` remains the existing non-aggregating exception: blocked children do\nnot select a parent status. Closed ancestors remain closed rather than being\nsilently normalized to `done`, so validation reports every affected container\nthat still has a blocked descendant. The operator must reopen the container or\nresolve/close that descendant work.\n\n## Story Activation Reminders\n\nAn `agent_reminders` array is added to the JSON envelope of a production\nmutation whenever that mutation's persisted changes include a Story\ntransitioning out of `backlog`. It is **additive advisory output**: it never\nfetches Git, never mutates the board beyond what the mutation already did, and\nnever replaces the authoritative freshness gate documented in\n[task-board config](task-board-config.md#trunk-discovery-authority-and-refusals) —\na reminder can render every authority field `null` and the mutation still\nsucceeds.\n\n### Trigger\n\nA change qualifies when all of the following hold:\n\n- `element_id` starts with `STORY-` (case-insensitive).\n- `field` is `status` and the change's recorded `old_value` is exactly\n  `backlog`.\n- The new status is `analysis`, `to-dev`, or `development`. Every other\n  target status — `to-review`, `reviewing`, `blocked`, `done`, `closed`, or a\n  `backlog → backlog` no-op — does not qualify, and neither does a\n  reactivation from `done` or `closed` (only `backlog` is the qualifying old\n  value).\n\nBoth the mutation's primary change and every side effect are scanned, so a\nTask/Bug mutation that auto-escalates its containing Story from `backlog`\nstill emits the Story's reminder even though the Task/Bug itself never\nqualifies on its own ID prefix.\n\n### Deduplication and order\n\nWithin one mutation's full change list (primary change first, then side\neffects in their recorded order), only the first qualifying change per Story\nID produces a reminder — a batch that touches the same Story's status twice\n(for example a reopen side effect followed by the requested transition) still\nemits exactly one reminder for it. Distinct qualifying Story IDs each get\ntheir own reminder, in first-seen order.\n\n### Payload shape\n\n```json\n{\n  \"code\": \"story_worktree_base_preflight_required\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-29T12:34:56.123456789Z\",\n  \"message\": \"Resolve and refresh the configured integration branch upstream, compare exact OIDs, and record the selected Story worktree base before provisioning or trusting it.\",\n  \"story_id\": \"STORY-260218-bbbbbb\",\n  \"trunk_ref\": \"refs/heads/main\",\n  \"trunk_oid\": \"8c3f1a9...\",\n  \"upstream_ref\": \"refs/remotes/protected/main\",\n  \"upstream_oid\": \"5e2b7d0...\",\n  \"selected_base_oid\": null\n}\n```\n\n`code`, `message`, and the RFC3339Nano UTC `timestamp` are stable across every\nreminder in one mutation (one clock read per mutation, not per Story).\n`trunk_ref`, `trunk_oid`, `upstream_ref`, `upstream_oid`, and\n`selected_base_oid` are always present as JSON keys and are explicit `null`,\nnever omitted, whenever that specific piece of evidence is not known. The\n`agent_reminders` key itself is only added to the result when at least one\nchange qualified; a mutation with no qualifying activation adds no key at all\n(not an empty array).\n\n### Authority evidence: local versus remote\n\nThe five nullable fields come from a cache-only projection that is read after\nthe mutation persists and never fails the mutation on error:\n\n- **Local CLI** — prefers a persisted workspace freshness record from a prior\n  `task-board spawn` for that Story (`trunk_ref`/`trunk_oid` = the recorded\n  integration branch and its local OID at spawn time, `upstream_ref`/\n  `upstream_oid` = the recorded upstream ref/OID, `selected_base_oid` = the\n  OID the workspace actually branched from). When no such record exists, it\n  falls back to a cache-only Git discovery (`DiscoverCachedTrunk`) against the\n  configured `integration_base_branch` — the same discovery order as the\n  freshness gate, but it never fetches, so `upstream_oid` reflects whatever\n  the last fetch already cached — and `selected_base_oid` is `null`, because\n  a cache-only projection names no provisioned workspace. Any read failure\n  (unreadable tracking config, unreadable ref) renders every field `null`\n  rather than failing the mutation.\n- **Remote (board-server)** — the reminder is produced by the identical shared\n  `pkg/board/query` mutation code, so it fires on the same triggers with the\n  same dedup, but the server wires no authority source (there is no local Git\n  checkout to read), so every one of the five fields is always `null`; only\n  `code`, `timestamp`, `message`, and `story_id` are populated.\n\n### Mutation coverage\n\n`agent_reminders` is decorated by one shared boundary that a subset of\nproduction mutations route their persisted changes through:\n\n| Covered — can emit a reminder | Not wired — structurally cannot emit |\n| --- | --- |\n| `set_status` | `normalize_legacy_status` |\n| `handoff` | `set_estimate` |\n| `create` (only via its ancestor-reopen side effects) | `set_det…954 tokens truncated…gation files on its chain. Everything else\n  under `.task-board/` — `.task-board/.goals/**` and every other Story's\n  subtree — stays exactly as it was: ordinary uncommitted working-tree state\n  that this command neither examines nor stages.\n- **Foreign uncommitted work is untouched**, on the same rule the section above\n  states for a hand-typed acknowledgement.\n\n**The two commits, in order.** First the squash commit carrying the accepted\ncandidate tree; then the `done` transition; then a second, board-only commit\ntitled `Record <STORY-ID> board state`. A Story whose accepted candidate has no\nrepository delta produces no first commit and still produces the second one —\nits board record is the whole of its work product.\n\n**Recovery is re-running the same command.** Integration keeps one durable\ntransaction record per Story, so a crashed or interrupted run resumes the\nrecorded phase instead of starting a second integration, and a completed one is\na no-op rather than a duplicate landing. A rollback is offered in exactly one\nphase — nothing landed yet, trunk unmoved — and is requested explicitly with\n`--rollback`; no later phase undoes a commit that is already on trunk. Partial\nintegration is therefore a state to resume, never a state to clean up by hand.\n# Resources\n\nResources are owned by the board element they are attached to. Use explicit\nCRUD operations against that element ID. Local payloads are grouped under\n`.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/`; remote board-server uses the equivalent\nelement-scoped filesystem or MinIO object-key prefix.\n\n## Resource Types\n\n| Type | Purpose | Example |\n|------|---------|---------|\n| **precondition** | Inputs needed before work starts | Design docs, specs, instructions, API schemas |\n| **outcome** | Results produced by work | Research findings, implementation notes, analysis |\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\n# Create a resource\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_design.md, type=precondition, content=\"...\", description=\"Architecture design\")'\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content=\"...\", description=\"Research findings\")'\n\n# Replace an existing resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content=\"revised findings\", description=\"Research findings v2\")'\ntask-board resource update TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_results.md --name reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome\n\n# Adopt an existing orphaned local payload without rewriting it\ntask-board resource adopt TASK-260218-abc revisions/2/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome --description \"Recovered result\"\n\n# Delete a resource (destructive — requires --confirm)\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md)'\ntask-board resource delete TASK-260218-abc artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md --confirm\n\n# View resources on an element\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { preconditionResources outcomeResources }'\n```\n\n## Element-Owned Storage\n\nResources from different tasks are not stored in one shared file pile. Each\nelement gets its own directory, and every resource attached to that element is\nstored below it:\n\n```\n.task-board/.resources/\n├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/\n│   ├── findings/\n│   │   └── investigation.md\n│   ├── artifacts/\n│   │   └── run-1/\n│   │       └── output.json\n│   ├── reports/\n│   │   └── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md\n│   └── revisions/\n│       └── 2/\n│           └── review.md\n└── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\n    └── research.md         # outcome\n```\n\nThe resource `name` is a safe forward-slash-relative path. Agents may choose\nthe hierarchy that fits the artifact and nest it further as needed. Every\nsegment must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and must not be `.`, `..`, an\nabsolute/drive-qualified path, or contain a backslash. Raw forward slashes are\nthe only hierarchy delimiter; malformed URL escapes and encoded or repeatedly\nencoded separators/traversal are rejected. Attach related files to the same\nowning element; do not invent a generic board-wide resource task or write\npayloads into `.task-board/.resources/` manually.\n\nDirectories are structural nodes, not payloads. Resource CRUD, validation, and\nhandoff evidence recognize regular files only; a directory named like a report\ncannot be adopted or used to satisfy outcome evidence.\n\nLocal and board-server filesystem element deletion is journaled and\ncrash-recoverable. Direct resource CRUD first resolves any pending staged\nelement delete under the board mutation lock. MinIO element/subtree deletion\ncurrently fails before mutation with the typed\n`ErrAtomicResourceDeleteUnsupported` capability error; ordinary resource CRUD\nremains available.\n\nReferences are tracked in `progress.md` as markdown links:\n```markdown\n## Precondition Resources\n- [specs/auth/design.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/precondition/specs/auth/design.md) — Architecture design\n\n## Outcome Resources\n- [reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/outcome/reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md) — Research findings\n```\n\n## Spawn Integration\n\nPrecondition resources are automatically injected into agent prompts by `task-board spawn`. Full prompts preserve normal text inlining. Lite prompts use the resolved `agent_context.prompt.preconditions` policy: by default, text below `inline_bytes` is rendered inline, while larger text and binary resources are written read-only under `.temp/resources/` and represented by their name, description, byte count, SHA-256 digest, and local path. This means:\n\n1. Attach instructions/specs as precondition resources to a task\n2. Run `task-board spawn TASK-XX --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — agent gets everything automatically\n3. Agent saves results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write, then `update_resource(...)` for revisions\n\n## Best Practices\n\n- **Use preconditions for agent instructions** — attach design docs, specs, or step-by-step instructions before spawning\n- **Use outcomes for work products** — research findings, analysis, implementation notes\n- **Attach to the owning element** — pass the Task, Bug, Story, or Epic ID that owns the artifact; the storage layer groups its payloads under that element directory automatically\n- **Use explicit create/update/delete** — `add_resource` / `resource add` must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` when replacing an existing payload\n- **Use adopt only for local repair** — `resource adopt` registers bytes already stored at `.resources/<elementID>/<relative-resource-path>` without rewriting them; a missing payload returns resource-not-found and an existing declaration returns resource-exists\n- **Choose a useful hierarchy inside the owning element** — `findings/`, `artifacts/`, `reports/`, and `revisions/` are conventions, not a fixed schema; nest further when it makes the handoff easier to navigate\n- **Use safe relative resource paths** — `/` separates validated path segments; empty, dot/traversal, absolute/drive-qualified, backslash, control-character, malformed escape, and encoded/repeatedly encoded separator or traversal forms are rejected in local and remote storage\n- **Attach files, not folders** — directories only organize resource files and never satisfy resource existence or handoff evidence\n- **Always use board commands** — never create/edit resource files or progress.md resource sections manually\n- **Name handoff evidence with task scope** — keep the task ID in the resource path, for example `reports/TASK-260218-abc_architecture-design.md`, so `task-board handoff` can recognize it\n- **Add descriptions** — the `description=` param adds a one-line description shown in spawn prompts\n\n---\n\n# Bugs\n\nBugs are elements of the board, living inside stories alongside tasks.\n\n```bash\n# Create bug\ntask-board m 'create(type=bug, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=crash-on-start, description=\"App crashes when starting recording\")'\n\n# Track like any element\ntask-board m 'set_status(BUG-260218-abc, status=development)'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Reproduce the issue\")'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Find root cause\")'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Write fix\")'\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\"Add regression test\")'\n```\n# Negative Evidence Contract\n\nA passing suite means nothing unless something in it would have failed. This\nfile is the single source of truth for the rules that make a green check mean\nsomething, and for the incident record behind each one. The operative wording\nthat reaches every spawned child lives in the spawn template section\n`Evidence That Counts`; the reviewer's licence lives in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`;\nthe repeated-variant heuristic lives in `.roles/orchestrator/role.md`. Read this\nfile when you need the shapes, the failure vocabulary, or the incidents that\nproduced a rule — do not restate these rules in other documents.\n\n| Rule | Operative statement | Evidence record |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Negative tests for gates | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Narrowing, not deleting | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Production call site | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Absence vs failure to read | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Prove, or report nothing | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\n| Reviewer licence to defeat a gate | `.roles/reviewer/role.md` | this file |\n| Many variants, one unowned decision | `.roles/orchestrator/role.md` | this file |\n\n## Negative Tests For Gates\n\nAny feature that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS must ship\nwith negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it is supposed to\nreject. A positive test proving the happy path passes is not evidence that the\ngate works; it is evidence that the gate is reachable.\n\nThis is the dominant defect class in this repository's 2026-07-28..08-03 waves.\nEvery instance below was found because a reviewer deliberately attacked the\ngate, not because anyone read the code:\n\n- the fact-assay attested a COLD child that never inherited the context, because\n  it checked inventory, session id, digests, and output shape, but never\n  inheritance;\n- the assay fork had repository tool access, so it could answer from disk while\n  the base was false;\n- the builder attestation accepted a correctly-shaped forged digest that the\n  caller minted itself;\n- clearance enforcement existed but no production path called it, and counting\n  tests around a fake proved nothing;\n- an unknown assay event preserved a false zero-tool observation;\n- a provider capability was declared absent to disable a flaky check, and the\n  claim did not reproduce (9/11 success).\n\n## Standard Negative Shapes\n\nUse these names in checklists, verdicts, and test names. They are the shapes a\nnegative test must cover and the shapes a reviewer looks for first.\n\n| Shape | What it looks like when nobody tests for it | What the negative test must do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Forged or self-minted evidence | The caller supplies the proof that the gate checks, and a correctly-shaped value passes. | Mint the evidence in the test the way a caller could, and require refusal. |\n| Absent evidence treated as satisfied | Missing input takes the permissive branch; the suite only ever supplies the input. | Remove the evidence entirely and require refusal, not a default-pass. |\n| Check present but uncalled from production | The guard is implemented and unit-tested by direct call; no production path invokes it. | Drive the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. |\n| Bypass path around the check | One entry point enforces; a second, older, or retry/renewal path does not. | Exercise every entry point that reaches the protected state, including retry, renewal, resume, and recovery, and every delivery surface that carries the rule — a contract clause reaching one context profile and not the other is this shape. |\n| Capability claim that does not reproduce | A capability is declared absent or present to make a flaky check go away. | Re-run the claim enough times to state a real rate, or report unknown. |\n\n## Prove A Bound By Narrowing, Not By Deleting\n\nA test that removes a gate entirely proves only that the gate exists. It says\nnothing about whether the gate covers the right class. Restrict the gate to a\nnarrower condition instead and require a named test to fail.\n\nTwo repository incidents where a narrowed gate survived a green suite:\n\n- **BUG-260801-3kiloe F1** — the recovery successor cap was proven only by total\n  removal. Narrowing it to provider-limit signals left every other recoverable\n  failure unbounded, with the whole suite still green.\n- **TASK-260802-17wetr F16** — thirteen cycles later, the same shape: the\n  probe-lease cap survived deletion tests while the renewal path escaped it.\n- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 1** — this contract itself. The reviewer clauses\n  landed in the role body and the shipped test checked the role file and the\n  lite template separately, so both halves were green while the composed lite\n  prompt carried neither. Assert on the composed production artifact, not on the\n  pieces it is built from.\n- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 2** — the same contract, one level up, and the\n  clearest statement of the difference between the two shapes. The cycle-1 fix\n  did assert on the composed prompt, but it called the composition helper\n  directly and handed it the role, task-class filter, and profile itself.\n  Production builds those inputs inside the spawn entry point, so a mutant\n  narrowing only the real lite-reviewer routing just before composition left the\n  test green while an actual lite reviewer lost its gate-defeat licence. A test\n  that supplies the inputs is testing the helper, not the path. Obtain the\n  artifact the way the consumer does — here, read the prompt file recorded on\n  the run manifest after driving the entry point — so role resolution, the\n  task-class filter, and profile resolution are all under test rather than\n  assumed.\n\nThree traps when running the mutants themselves, all hit while proving this file:\n\n- **The mutant must be re-run with `-count=1`.** `go test` caches on package\n  sources; a test that reads `.roles/*.md` or any file outside its package at\n  runtime returns a stale cached PASS after that file is mutated. A cached green\n  is not a surviving mutant, it is an unexecuted test.\n- **A passing assertion may be satisfied by the wrong source.** Two mutants of\n  the producer DoD entries survived because the phrase asserted also appears,\n  differently worded, in the shared spawn template. Choose assertion wording\n  that only the intended source can supply, then narrow that source and require\n  the named test to fail.\n- **Restore the mutant from a copy, not from git.** `git checkout <file>`\n  restores from the index, which silently discards uncommitted work in that file\n  — during this task it reverted the whole cycle-2 change, not just the mutant.\n  Copy the file aside before mutating, restore from the copy, and verify the\n  restoration by diffing against it before rerunning the gates.\n\n## An Absence And A Failure To Read Are Different Facts\n\nAbsence is a legitimate state with a defined fallback. A failed, partial, or\nmalformed read is evidence of a problem. Treating the second as the first lets\nthe fallback launder a fault into a normal-looking value, and a fallback defined\nfor absence must never fire on a read failure.\n\nEach of these was a separate defect in this repository:\n\n- a non-empty value read as verified;\n- an unknown scanner version read as clean;\n- a partial provenance record read as absent;\n- a zero pid start time read as alive;\n- a malformed `api_error_status` read as missing;\n- an unparsed envelope read as no envelope — this one produced a false provider\n  suppression that would have hidden a healthy provider for hours.\n\n## Prove, Or Report Nothing\n\nWhere a property cannot be established, report unparsed or unknown rather than\ninferring it from a proxy signal. A plausible guess is worse than an explicit\nunknown, because callers act on it.\n\nIncident: headless top-level inference in the Claude classifier was unsound in\nprinciple and produced six variants before it was deleted rather than\nstrengthened.\n\n## Many Variants, One Unowned Decision\n\nA long run of same-shaped variants usually means a decision has more than one\nowner, not that there are many bugs. Eleven framing variants stopped reproducing\nonly when structure got a single scanner and a single state type; every per-site\npatch before that produced the next variant. The operative wording is in\n`.roles/orchestrator/role.md`: unify the decision, do not patch each site.\n\n## Reviewer Licence\n\nReviewers are explicitly licensed to attempt to defeat a gate and to reject a\nchange whose only evidence is positive-path. Grep for callers before believing a\nguard is in force. The operative wording is in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`.\nMM .gitignore\nMM README.md\nMM go.mod\nD  go.sum\nD  internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go\nD  internal/canonicaljson/canonical_test.go\nMM internal/catalog/catalog.go\nMM internal/catalog/catalog.v0.5.0.json\nMM internal/catalog/catalog_gen.go\nMM internal/catalog/catalog_test.go\nMM internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go\nMM internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main_test.go\nMM internal/cataloggen/generate.go\nMM internal/cataloggen/generate_test.go\nD  internal/scalar/integer.go\nD  internal/scalar/names.go\nD  internal/scalar/path.go\nD  internal/scalar/scalar.go\nD  internal/scalar/scalar_test.go\nD  internal/scalar/time_digest.go\nD  internal/scalar/uuid.go\nMM internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go\nMM internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json\nMM internal/traceability/traceability.go\nMM internal/traceability/traceability_test.go\nMM task-board.config.json\n?? go.sum\n?? internal/canonicaljson/\n?? internal/scalar/\ntask-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e\nfb61f3d (HEAD -> task-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e) TASK-260830-236x9n: TASK-260830-236x9n: implement-jcs-and-self-identities\n006ba4e TASK-260830-1pbx0c: TASK-260830-1pbx0c: implement-common-scalar-types\nc9e5290 (origin/story/normative-pin-scope-completion, story/normative-pin-scope-completion) Record STORY-260831-2xdhn9 board state\n60c1b62 STORY-260831-2xdhn9: STORY-260831-2xdhn9: normative-pin-scope-completion\n3679514 (origin/task/close-landing-gate-task, task/close-landing-gate-task, task-board/story/STORY-260831-2xdhn9) Close landing gate configuration task\nREADME.md\ninternal/scalar/scalar_test.go\ninternal/scalar/path.go\ninternal/scalar/uuid.go\ninternal/scalar/names.go\ninternal/scalar/integer.go\ninternal/scalar/scalar.go\ninternal/scalar/time_digest.go\ninternal/scalar/fuzz_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/golden_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/validation_config_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/canonical.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/canonical_test.go\ninternal/canonicaljson/fuzz_test.go\ninternal/traceability/traceability.go\ninternal/traceability/traceability_test.go\ninternal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go\ninternal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main.go\ninternal/catalog/catalog_gen.go\ninternal/catalog/catalog_test.go\ninternal/catalog/catalog.go\ninternal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main_test.go\ninternal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go\ninternal/cataloggen/generate_test.go\ninternal/cataloggen/generate.go\ninternal/specpin/pin_test.go\ninternal/specpin/pin.go\ninternal/specpin/scope_test.go\ninternal/specpin/sections.go\ngo.mod\n"},"started_at_ms":1788201369559,"completed_at_ms":1788201369559}}
33:{"timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:36:09.565Z","ordinal":32,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0591b-7fdd-73a2-bfb4-e6630025a2ed","call_id":"call_XDqxZ2N4WtzFRsiMVfm1Myh7","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 11191)\nTotal output lines: 1\n\n{\"chunk_id\":\"9de48b\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000001708,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":10954,\"output\":\"# Statuses\\n\\n9 statuses organized in a workflow:\\n\\n| Status | Meaning | Who sets |\\n|--------|---------|----------|\\n| **backlog** | Captured, no work yet | anyone |\\n| **analysis** | Researching, decomposing | anyone |\\n| **to-dev** | Ready for development | orchestrator |\\n| **development** | Writing code | sub-agent |\\n| **to-review** | Code ready for review | sub-agent |\\n| **reviewing** | Reviewer child is checking the work | reviewer |\\n| **done** | Reviewed and accepted leaf work, or computed container completion | reviewer / orchestrator after reviewer verdict; parent aggregation |\\n| **closed** | Explicit administrative disposition: cancelled, obsolete, or won't do (reason in notes) | anyone |\\n| **blocked** | Concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision (full evidence packet in notes/resource) | anyone |\\n\\n## Status Flow\\n\\n**Happy path:**\\n```\\nbacklog → analysis → to-dev → development → to-review → reviewing → done\\n```\\n\\n**Returns from reviewing:**\\n- `reviewing → analysis` — need more research\\n- `reviewing → to-dev` — code issues, needs rework\\n\\n**Terminal from any status:**\\n- `any → closed` — won't do / cancelled\\n- `any → blocked` — concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision\\n\\n**Note on closed:** Use `closed` for cancelled, obsolete, or won't-do items. Don't delete — close with a note explaining why. History is preserved, and the item can be reopened if needed.\\n\\nFor a leaf task or bug, `done` means its delivery was accepted and `closed`\\nmeans it was explicitly disposed without accepted delivery. A leaf may be\\nclosed from any lifecycle state.\\n\\nFor a story or epic, `done` is computed completion: every direct child is\\n`done` or `closed`. `closed` is an explicit administrative disposition of the\\ncontainer itself. A container may be closed only when every direct and\\ntransitive descendant is already `done` or `closed`; an empty container also\\nsatisfies this rule. Close or complete descendant work first, then close\\ncontainers from the inside out. `task-board validate` reports\\n`PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` for stored closed containers that still contain active\\ndescendants.\\n\\nValidation is strict by default for empty containers too: an empty Story/Epic\\nstored as `done` aggregates to `backlog` and is reported. A migrated local\\nboard may explicitly set\\n`task-board.config.json -> validation.allow_legacy_empty_done_containers` to\\n`true` to suppress only that historical empty-`done` mismatch. Empty active\\ncontainers and every container with one or more children remain strict.\\n\\n**Don't delete elements.** Use `closed` instead of `delete`. Deletion loses history and context. The only exception is removing test/junk data during board setup.\\n\\n**Orchestrator review routing flow:** When a producer child sets `to-review`:\\n1. Orchestrator confirms the producer attached task-scoped outcome evidence.\\n2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer child with explicit selection, for example `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.\\n3. Reviewer child sets/keeps `reviewing`, reads artifacts/code, and writes the verdict.\\n4. If accepted → `done`. If changes are requested → `to-dev` (code rework) or `analysis` (needs more research), with notes/outcome evidence.\\n4a. When the reviewer was launched against a Change Request (a managed Story workspace run), acceptance is recorded by `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=...)` instead. That mutation sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, and parks the element at `to-review` as the **accepted handoff** — accepted work never stays in `reviewing`. The Orchestrator then runs `task-board worktree checkpoint <ID>` for a **non-final** leaf, which commits the accepted candidate onto the Story branch and makes the `done` transition with `commit_ack=scope_committed`. A Change Request with `repository_delta=empty` skips the commit and still reaches `done` by the same command. `accept_cr` accepts no `commit_ack` argument, its `evidence` must be a task-scoped outcome resource this reviewer run attached or updated (naming the producer's own `_results.md` is refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`), only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may call it (anything else is refused with `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`), and the live task/reviewer checklist must be complete. All refusals precede CR, status, and activity writes.\\n\\n4b. **`checkpoint` never closes the Story.** When the leaf is the Story's last open child, closing it would promote the Story to `done` in the same mutation and cover that transition with `commit_ack=scope_committed` — attesting that the Story's scope is committed while its branch has never reached trunk. `worktree checkpoint` refuses that with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint` and names `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>`, which lands the branch before making the transition. The refusal is a scope rule, not an acknowledgement rule: it applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it leaves the branch, the accepted record and the board untouched so the integration path can still use them.\\n5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.\\n6. If a child exits early or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, the Orchestrator inspects the run/outcomes and retries or reroutes through a focused child. Recoverable work is never handed to the human.\\n7. A `blocked` verdict stops routing only when it identifies a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision and records the full evidence packet; otherwise retry or reroute.\\n\\n`reviewing` is a real state, not a pass-through. A reviewer child must do actual review work between the producer handoff and `done`; the orchestrator routes and monitors that review instead of performing the verdict inline.\\n\\n`reviewing` is never a verdict, including under the Change Request flow. A reviewer run that ends at `reviewing` with no accepted Change Request is cancelled and a successor reviewer is spawned — that is what distinguishes \\\"the reviewer decided nothing\\\" from \\\"the reviewer decided\\\", and `accept_cr` does not change it. The accepted branch is derived from the Change Request record, not from the `reviewing` status.\\n\\n`to-review` therefore carries two meanings that the record disambiguates: a producer handoff awaiting review, and an accepted Change Request awaiting orchestrator checkpoint or integration. `task-board worktree status` and the Change Request state say which.\\n\\nA tracked spawned producer cannot use direct `set_status(..., status=to-review)`\\nfor its assigned element. That workflow guardrail returns a typed remediation to\\nrun `task-board handoff ELEMENT --role ROLE`, which verifies checklist and\\noutcome evidence. Untracked operator status administration is unchanged.\\n\\n`to-review` is an internal routing trigger, never a handoff of routine review to the human. Human input is reserved for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision.\\n\\nHuman-facing wording follows the board state. `to-review` means \\\"ready for review\\\" or \\\"handed off to review\\\"; it is not \\\"done\\\", \\\"complete\\\", \\\"finished\\\", \\\"final\\\", or \\\"готово\\\". Say a task is done only after the board item is actually `done`.\\n\\n## Per-Element Review Policy\\n\\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with\\n`create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with\\n`set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` query\\nprojection. New elements and legacy elements without the field default to\\n`required`.\\n\\n- `required` keeps the producer -> reviewer -> rework/reviewer loop. Only an\\n  accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but selection should favor a cheaper suitable effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates.\\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff from `to-review`\\n  directly to `done` only after checking acceptance criteria, checklist,\\n  validation results, and outcome evidence. Missing evidence returns to the\\n  producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\\n\\nThis policy changes reviewer routing, not the need for board-owned evidence, and\\nnever introduces routine human review.\\n\\n## blocked vs is_blocked\\n\\nTwo different concepts:\\n\\n- **blocked** (status) — explicit stop-the-line status for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Notes or an outcome resource must record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.\\n- **is_blocked** (computed) — from `blocked-by` dependencies; if dependency is not `done`/`closed`, element is blocked\\n\\nCLI display:\\n```\\nTASK-05: to-dev [BLOCKED by TASK-02]     ← computed from dependency\\nTASK-07: blocked                          ← explicit status\\n```\\n\\n## Automatic Parent Status Aggregation\\n\\nAfter a child status mutation, the CLI recomputes every ancestor from its direct\\nchildren. It is derived state: an unassigned story or epic may follow an\\nassigned child without implying ownership of the container.\\n\\n- If every child is `done` or `closed`, a non-closed parent becomes `done`.\\n  An already explicit `closed` parent stays `closed`; terminal child updates do\\n  not silently normalize it to `done`.\\n- Otherwise the parent adopts the highest-priority active child status:\\n  `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, then `backlog`.\\n  `blocked` children do not select a parent status; a parent with only blocked\\n  children keeps its current manual status.\\n- The recomputation can promote, escalate, reopen, or demote a parent, then\\n  continues through all ancestors. A parent that is `blocked` explicitly is not\\n  recomputed.\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=development)'\\n# TASK-260205-abc123 → development\\n# STORY-260205-def456 → development (auto-escalated)\\n# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → development (auto-escalated)\\n```\\n\\nFor Task/Bug records created with the current format, the first transition into\\n`development` also requires an explicit estimated value. Set it first with\\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The refusal\\nis a typed estimate-required error, parallel to the unfinished-dependency start\\nrefusal. Records whose legacy `progress.md` has no Estimate section are\\ngrandfathered and are not retroactively blocked; an idempotent status write for\\nan element already in `development` is not treated as a new start.\\n\\n### Commit confirmation for accepted work\\n\\nThis gate exists so the commit-owning mover lays out its own delivered scope\\nas atomic, project-perspective commits and honestly acknowledges that scope is\\ncommitted — it is not a human checkpoint. An Orchestrator (or other\\ncommit-owning mover) must not stop autonomous work to ask the human how to\\nsplit commits, or whether to acknowledge, on every commit or close. The mover\\ndecides its own commit decomposition — how many commits, per-story or\\nfeature-clustered, ordering, buildability — and commits that scope itself,\\nbackdated per the project's commit-time policy when one is configured. Human\\nauthorization is the standing grant to commit work, not a per-commit\\napproval.\\n\\nThis section is the single source for that contract. Role bodies, the spawn\\ntemplates, and the router carry only the ownership statement and a link back\\nhere; they must not restate the mechanics below.\\n\\nTop-level `task-board.config.json -> version_control.confirm` defaults to\\n`false`. When it is `true`, `desired_commit_time` is required. Any mutation\\nthat would move a Bug or Story to `done` must then include the exact mover-local\\nacknowledgement `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This covers a direct Bug/Story\\ntransition and a Task/Bug terminal transition that automatically promotes its\\nparent Story.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"version_control\\\": {\\n    \\\"confirm\\\": true,\\n    \\\"desired_commit_time\\\": \\\"evening MSK\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260205-def456, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'\\n```\\n\\nThe error reports the transition-causing element, every enforced Bug/Story\\ntransition, the owner message, and the configured desired time. The\\nacknowledgement covers only the mover's scope; foreign uncommitted work is\\nallowed and task-board performs no git clean-tree check. Never acknowledge\\n`commit_ack=scope_committed` when the mover's own scope is genuinely\\nuncommitted — commit it first, then acknowledge. Local evaluation and\\nstatus writes share one mutation lock. In remote mode an enforcing CLI first\\nrequires the server's `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1` capability, then\\nthe server evaluates the acknowledgement and writes the child/ancestor statuses\\nunder one serialized mutation.\\n\\nA reviewer-archetype spawned run must not supply `commit_ack`. It records\\nacceptance evidence and hands the accepted verdict to the mover that owns the\\nscoped commit (typically the Orchestrator) — never to the human, and it must\\nnot escalate the commit-or-acknowledge decision to the human itself; that\\nmover commits first, then makes the final `done` transition with\\n`commit_ack=scope_committed`.\\n\\nBoard structure decides how often this gate fires on an unfinished parent; the\\nrule that keeps it honest lives with the other decomposition rules in\\n[planning workflow](planning-workflow.md).\\n\\nReactivation follows one deterministic rule: adding a descendant creates it in\\n`backlog`, and adding or changing any descendant to an aggregating active status\\nruns ordinary aggregation through every ancestor. Any `done` or `closed`\\ncontainer on that path reopens to the highest-priority active aggregate status;\\nexplicit closure is not sticky once descendant work becomes active. The target\\nis the aggregate child status, not a fixed `development` state:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=to-dev)'\\n# TASK-260205-abc123 → to-dev\\n# STORY-260205-def456 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\\n# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\\n```\\n\\nAutomatic container transitions do not require an assignee. Hard dependencies\\nstill gate automatic changes to `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, and\\n`done`: if a container has an unfinished blocker, its derived status is left\\nunchanged and the child mutation succeeds. Direct manual transitions use the\\nsame dependency gate; they additionally require an assignee for `to-dev`,\\n`development`, and `to-review`.\\n\\n`blocked` remains the existing non-aggregating exception: blocked children do\\nnot select a parent status. Closed ancestors remain closed rather than being\\nsilently normalized to `done`, so validation reports every affected container\\nthat still has a blocked descendant. The operator must reopen the container or\\nresolve/close that descendant work.\\n\\n## Story Activation Reminders\\n\\nAn `agent_reminders` array is added to the JSON envelope of a production\\nmutation whenever that mutation's persisted changes include a Story\\ntransitioning out of `backlog`. It is **additive advisory output**: it never\\nfetches Git, never mutates the board beyond what the mutation already did, and\\nnever replaces the authoritative freshness gate documented in\\n[task-board config](task-board-config.md#trunk-discovery-authority-and-refusals) —\\na reminder can render every authority field `null` and the mutation still\\nsucceeds.\\n\\n### Trigger\\n\\nA change qualifies when all of the following hold:\\n\\n- `element_id` starts with `STORY-` (case-insensitive).\\n- `field` is `status` and the change's recorded `old_value` is exactly\\n  `backlog`.\\n- The new status is `analysis`, `to-dev`, or `development`. Every other\\n  target status — `to-review`, `reviewing`, `blocked`, `done`, `closed`, or a\\n  `backlog → backlog` no-op — does not qualify, and neither does a\\n  reactivation from `done` or `closed` (only `backlog` is the qualifying old\\n  value).\\n\\nBoth the mutation's primary change and every side effect are scanned, so a\\nTask/Bug mutation that auto-escalates its containing Story from `backlog`\\nstill emits the Story's reminder even though the Task/Bug itself never\\nqualifies on its own ID prefix.\\n\\n### Deduplication and order\\n\\nWithin one mutation's full change list (primary change first, then side\\neffects in their recorded order), only the first qualifying change per Story\\nID produces a reminder — a batch that touches the same Story's status twice\\n(for example a reopen side effect followed by the requested transition) still\\nemits exactly one reminder for it. Distinct qualifying Story IDs each get\\ntheir own reminder, in first-seen order.\\n\\n### Payload shape\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"code\\\": \\\"story_worktree_base_preflight_required\\\",\\n  \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-08-29T12:34:56.123456789Z\\\",\\n  \\\"message\\\": \\\"Resolve and refresh the configured integration branch upstream, compare exact OIDs, and record the selected Story worktree base before provisioning or trusting it.\\\",\\n  \\\"story_id\\\": \\\"STORY-260218-bbbbbb\\\",\\n  \\\"trunk_ref\\\": \\\"refs/heads/main\\\",\\n  \\\"trunk_oid\\\": \\\"8c3f1a9...\\\",\\n  \\\"upstream_ref\\\": \\\"refs/remotes/protected/main\\\",\\n  \\\"upstream_oid\\\": \\\"5e2b7d0...\\\",\\n  \\\"selected_base_oid\\\": null\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`code`, `message`, and the RFC3339Nano UTC `timestamp` are stable across every\\nreminder in one mutation (one clock read per mutation, not per Story).\\n`trunk_ref`, `trunk_oid`, `upstream_ref`, `upstream_oid`, and\\n`selected_base_oid` are always present as JSON keys and are explicit `null`,\\nnever omitted, whenever that specific piece of evidence is not known. The\\n`agent_reminders` key itself is only added to the result when at least one\\nchange qualified; a mutation with no qualifying activation adds no key at all\\n(not an empty array).\\n\\n### Authority evidence: local versus remote\\n\\nThe five nullable fields come from a cache-only projection that is read after\\nthe mutation persists and never fails the mutation on error:\\n\\n- **Local CLI** — prefers a persisted workspace freshness record from a prior\\n  `task-board spawn` for that Story (`trunk_ref`/`trunk_oid` = the recorded\\n  integration branch and its local OID at spawn time, `upstream_ref`/\\n  `upstream_oid` = the recorded upstream ref/OID, `selected_base_oid` = the\\n  OID the workspace actually branched from). When no such record exists, it\\n  falls back to a cache-only Git discovery (`DiscoverCachedTrunk`) against the\\n  configured `integration_base_branch` — the same discovery order as the\\n  freshness gate, but it never fetches, so `upstream_oid` reflects whatever\\n  the last fetch already cached — and `selected_base_oid` is `null`, because\\n  a cache-only projection names no provisioned workspace. Any read failure\\n  (unreadable tracking config, unreadable ref) renders every field `null`\\n  rather than failing the mutation.\\n- **Remote (board-server)** — the reminder is produced by the identical shared\\n  `pkg/board/query` mutation code, so it fires on the same triggers with the\\n  same dedup, but the server wires no authority source (there is no local Git\\n  checkout to read), so every one of the five f…1191 tokens truncated…o commits, in order.** First the squash commit carrying the accepted\\ncandidate tree; then the `done` transition; then a second, board-only commit\\ntitled `Record <STORY-ID> board state`. A Story whose accepted candidate has no\\nrepository delta produces no first commit and still produces the second one —\\nits board record is the whole of its work product.\\n\\n**Recovery is re-running the same command.** Integration keeps one durable\\ntransaction record per Story, so a crashed or interrupted run resumes the\\nrecorded phase instead of starting a second integration, and a completed one is\\na no-op rather than a duplicate landing. A rollback is offered in exactly one\\nphase — nothing landed yet, trunk unmoved — and is requested explicitly with\\n`--rollback`; no later phase undoes a commit that is already on trunk. Partial\\nintegration is therefore a state to resume, never a state to clean up by hand.\\n# Resources\\n\\nResources are owned by the board element they are attached to. Use explicit\\nCRUD operations against that element ID. Local payloads are grouped under\\n`.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/`; remote board-server uses the equivalent\\nelement-scoped filesystem or MinIO object-key prefix.\\n\\n## Resource Types\\n\\n| Type | Purpose | Example |\\n|------|---------|---------|\\n| **precondition** | Inputs needed before work starts | Design docs, specs, instructions, API schemas |\\n| **outcome** | Results produced by work | Research findings, implementation notes, analysis |\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create a resource\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_design.md, type=precondition, content=\\\"...\\\", description=\\\"Architecture design\\\")'\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content=\\\"...\\\", description=\\\"Research findings\\\")'\\n\\n# Replace an existing resource\\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content=\\\"revised findings\\\", description=\\\"Research findings v2\\\")'\\ntask-board resource update TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_results.md --name reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome\\n\\n# Adopt an existing orphaned local payload without rewriting it\\ntask-board resource adopt TASK-260218-abc revisions/2/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome --description \\\"Recovered result\\\"\\n\\n# Delete a resource (destructive — requires --confirm)\\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md)'\\ntask-board resource delete TASK-260218-abc artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md --confirm\\n\\n# View resources on an element\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { preconditionResources outcomeResources }'\\n```\\n\\n## Element-Owned Storage\\n\\nResources from different tasks are not stored in one shared file pile. Each\\nelement gets its own directory, and every resource attached to that element is\\nstored below it:\\n\\n```\\n.task-board/.resources/\\n├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/\\n│   ├── findings/\\n│   │   └── investigation.md\\n│   ├── artifacts/\\n│   │   └── run-1/\\n│   │       └── output.json\\n│   ├── reports/\\n│   │   └── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md\\n│   └── revisions/\\n│       └── 2/\\n│           └── review.md\\n└── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\\n    └── research.md         # outcome\\n```\\n\\nThe resource `name` is a safe forward-slash-relative path. Agents may choose\\nthe hierarchy that fits the artifact and nest it further as needed. Every\\nsegment must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and must not be `.`, `..`, an\\nabsolute/drive-qualified path, or contain a backslash. Raw forward slashes are\\nthe only hierarchy delimiter; malformed URL escapes and encoded or repeatedly\\nencoded separators/traversal are rejected. Attach related files to the same\\nowning element; do not invent a generic board-wide resource task or write\\npayloads into `.task-board/.resources/` manually.\\n\\nDirectories are structural nodes, not payloads. Resource CRUD, validation, and\\nhandoff evidence recognize regular files only; a directory named like a report\\ncannot be adopted or used to satisfy outcome evidence.\\n\\nLocal and board-server filesystem element deletion is journaled and\\ncrash-recoverable. Direct resource CRUD first resolves any pending staged\\nelement delete under the board mutation lock. MinIO element/subtree deletion\\ncurrently fails before mutation with the typed\\n`ErrAtomicResourceDeleteUnsupported` capability error; ordinary resource CRUD\\nremains available.\\n\\nReferences are tracked in `progress.md` as markdown links:\\n```markdown\\n## Precondition Resources\\n- [specs/auth/design.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/precondition/specs/auth/design.md) — Architecture design\\n\\n## Outcome Resources\\n- [reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/outcome/reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md) — Research findings\\n```\\n\\n## Spawn Integration\\n\\nPrecondition resources are automatically injected into agent prompts by `task-board spawn`. Full prompts preserve normal text inlining. Lite prompts use the resolved `agent_context.prompt.preconditions` policy: by default, text below `inline_bytes` is rendered inline, while larger text and binary resources are written read-only under `.temp/resources/` and represented by their name, description, byte count, SHA-256 digest, and local path. This means:\\n\\n1. Attach instructions/specs as precondition resources to a task\\n2. Run `task-board spawn TASK-XX --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — agent gets everything automatically\\n3. Agent saves results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write, then `update_resource(...)` for revisions\\n\\n## Best Practices\\n\\n- **Use preconditions for agent instructions** — attach design docs, specs, or step-by-step instructions before spawning\\n- **Use outcomes for work products** — research findings, analysis, implementation notes\\n- **Attach to the owning element** — pass the Task, Bug, Story, or Epic ID that owns the artifact; the storage layer groups its payloads under that element directory automatically\\n- **Use explicit create/update/delete** — `add_resource` / `resource add` must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` when replacing an existing payload\\n- **Use adopt only for local repair** — `resource adopt` registers bytes already stored at `.resources/<elementID>/<relative-resource-path>` without rewriting them; a missing payload returns resource-not-found and an existing declaration returns resource-exists\\n- **Choose a useful hierarchy inside the owning element** — `findings/`, `artifacts/`, `reports/`, and `revisions/` are conventions, not a fixed schema; nest further when it makes the handoff easier to navigate\\n- **Use safe relative resource paths** — `/` separates validated path segments; empty, dot/traversal, absolute/drive-qualified, backslash, control-character, malformed escape, and encoded/repeatedly encoded separator or traversal forms are rejected in local and remote storage\\n- **Attach files, not folders** — directories only organize resource files and never satisfy resource existence or handoff evidence\\n- **Always use board commands** — never create/edit resource files or progress.md resource sections manually\\n- **Name handoff evidence with task scope** — keep the task ID in the resource path, for example `reports/TASK-260218-abc_architecture-design.md`, so `task-board handoff` can recognize it\\n- **Add descriptions** — the `description=` param adds a one-line description shown in spawn prompts\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Bugs\\n\\nBugs are elements of the board, living inside stories alongside tasks.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create bug\\ntask-board m 'create(type=bug, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=crash-on-start, description=\\\"App crashes when starting recording\\\")'\\n\\n# Track like any element\\ntask-board m 'set_status(BUG-260218-abc, status=development)'\\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\\\"Reproduce the issue\\\")'\\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\\\"Find root cause\\\")'\\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\\\"Write fix\\\")'\\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text=\\\"Add regression test\\\")'\\n```\\n# Negative Evidence Contract\\n\\nA passing suite means nothing unless something in it would have failed. This\\nfile is the single source of truth for the rules that make a green check mean\\nsomething, and for the incident record behind each one. The operative wording\\nthat reaches every spawned child lives in the spawn template section\\n`Evidence That Counts`; the reviewer's licence lives in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`;\\nthe repeated-variant heuristic lives in `.roles/orchestrator/role.md`. Read this\\nfile when you need the shapes, the failure vocabulary, or the incidents that\\nproduced a rule — do not restate these rules in other documents.\\n\\n| Rule | Operative statement | Evidence record |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Negative tests for gates | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\\n| Narrowing, not deleting | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\\n| Production call site | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\\n| Absence vs failure to read | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\\n| Prove, or report nothing | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |\\n| Reviewer licence to defeat a gate | `.roles/reviewer/role.md` | this file |\\n| Many variants, one unowned decision | `.roles/orchestrator/role.md` | this file |\\n\\n## Negative Tests For Gates\\n\\nAny feature that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS must ship\\nwith negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it is supposed to\\nreject. A positive test proving the happy path passes is not evidence that the\\ngate works; it is evidence that the gate is reachable.\\n\\nThis is the dominant defect class in this repository's 2026-07-28..08-03 waves.\\nEvery instance below was found because a reviewer deliberately attacked the\\ngate, not because anyone read the code:\\n\\n- the fact-assay attested a COLD child that never inherited the context, because\\n  it checked inventory, session id, digests, and output shape, but never\\n  inheritance;\\n- the assay fork had repository tool access, so it could answer from disk while\\n  the base was false;\\n- the builder attestation accepted a correctly-shaped forged digest that the\\n  caller minted itself;\\n- clearance enforcement existed but no production path called it, and counting\\n  tests around a fake proved nothing;\\n- an unknown assay event preserved a false zero-tool observation;\\n- a provider capability was declared absent to disable a flaky check, and the\\n  claim did not reproduce (9/11 success).\\n\\n## Standard Negative Shapes\\n\\nUse these names in checklists, verdicts, and test names. They are the shapes a\\nnegative test must cover and the shapes a reviewer looks for first.\\n\\n| Shape | What it looks like when nobody tests for it | What the negative test must do |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Forged or self-minted evidence | The caller supplies the proof that the gate checks, and a correctly-shaped value passes. | Mint the evidence in the test the way a caller could, and require refusal. |\\n| Absent evidence treated as satisfied | Missing input takes the permissive branch; the suite only ever supplies the input. | Remove the evidence entirely and require refusal, not a default-pass. |\\n| Check present but uncalled from production | The guard is implemented and unit-tested by direct call; no production path invokes it. | Drive the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. |\\n| Bypass path around the check | One entry point enforces; a second, older, or retry/renewal path does not. | Exercise every entry point that reaches the protected state, including retry, renewal, resume, and recovery, and every delivery surface that carries the rule — a contract clause reaching one context profile and not the other is this shape. |\\n| Capability claim that does not reproduce | A capability is declared absent or present to make a flaky check go away. | Re-run the claim enough times to state a real rate, or report unknown. |\\n\\n## Prove A Bound By Narrowing, Not By Deleting\\n\\nA test that removes a gate entirely proves only that the gate exists. It says\\nnothing about whether the gate covers the right class. Restrict the gate to a\\nnarrower condition instead and require a named test to fail.\\n\\nTwo repository incidents where a narrowed gate survived a green suite:\\n\\n- **BUG-260801-3kiloe F1** — the recovery successor cap was proven only by total\\n  removal. Narrowing it to provider-limit signals left every other recoverable\\n  failure unbounded, with the whole suite still green.\\n- **TASK-260802-17wetr F16** — thirteen cycles later, the same shape: the\\n  probe-lease cap survived deletion tests while the renewal path escaped it.\\n- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 1** — this contract itself. The reviewer clauses\\n  landed in the role body and the shipped test checked the role file and the\\n  lite template separately, so both halves were green while the composed lite\\n  prompt carried neither. Assert on the composed production artifact, not on the\\n  pieces it is built from.\\n- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 2** — the same contract, one level up, and the\\n  clearest statement of the difference between the two shapes. The cycle-1 fix\\n  did assert on the composed prompt, but it called the composition helper\\n  directly and handed it the role, task-class filter, and profile itself.\\n  Production builds those inputs inside the spawn entry point, so a mutant\\n  narrowing only the real lite-reviewer routing just before composition left the\\n  test green while an actual lite reviewer lost its gate-defeat licence. A test\\n  that supplies the inputs is testing the helper, not the path. Obtain the\\n  artifact the way the consumer does — here, read the prompt file recorded on\\n  the run manifest after driving the entry point — so role resolution, the\\n  task-class filter, and profile resolution are all under test rather than\\n  assumed.\\n\\nThree traps when running the mutants themselves, all hit while proving this file:\\n\\n- **The mutant must be re-run with `-count=1`.** `go test` caches on package\\n  sources; a test that reads `.roles/*.md` or any file outside its package at\\n  runtime returns a stale cached PASS after that file is mutated. A cached green\\n  is not a surviving mutant, it is an unexecuted test.\\n- **A passing assertion may be satisfied by the wrong source.** Two mutants of\\n  the producer DoD entries survived because the phrase asserted also appears,\\n  differently worded, in the shared spawn template. Choose assertion wording\\n  that only the intended source can supply, then narrow that source and require\\n  the named test to fail.\\n- **Restore the mutant from a copy, not from git.** `git checkout <file>`\\n  restores from the index, which silently discards uncommitted work in that file\\n  — during this task it reverted the whole cycle-2 change, not just the mutant.\\n  Copy the file aside before mutating, restore from the copy, and verify the\\n  restoration by diffing against it before rerunning the gates.\\n\\n## An Absence And A Failure To Read Are Different Facts\\n\\nAbsence is a legitimate state with a defined fallback. A failed, partial, or\\nmalformed read is evidence of a problem. Treating the second as the first lets\\nthe fallback launder a fault into a normal-looking value, and a fallback defined\\nfor absence must never fire on a read failure.\\n\\nEach of these was a separate defect in this repository:\\n\\n- a non-empty value read as verified;\\n- an unknown scanner version read as clean;\\n- a partial provenance record read as absent;\\n- a zero pid start time read as alive;\\n- a malformed `api_error_status` read as missing;\\n- an unparsed envelope read as no envelope — this one produced a false provider\\n  suppression that would have hidden a healthy provider for hours.\\n\\n## Prove, Or Report Nothing\\n\\nWhere a property cannot be established, report unparsed or unknown rather than\\ninferring it from a proxy signal. A plausible guess is worse than an explicit\\nunknown, because callers act on it.\\n\\nIncident: headless top-level inference in the Claude classifier was unsound in\\nprinciple and produced six variants before it was deleted rather than\\nstrengthened.\\n\\n## Many Variants, One Unowned Decision\\n\\nA long run of same-shaped variants usually means a decision has more than one\\nowner, not that there are many bugs. Eleven framing variants stopped reproducing\\nonly when structure got a single scanner and a single state type; every per-site\\npatch before that produced the next variant. The operative wording is in\\n`.roles/orchestrator/role.md`: unify the decision, do not patch each site.\\n\\n## Reviewer Licence\\n\\nReviewers are explicitly licensed to attempt to defeat a gate and to reject a\\nchange whose only evidence is positive-path. Grep for callers before believing a\\nguard is in force. The operative wording is in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`.\\nMM .gitignore\\nMM README.md\\nMM go.mod\\nD  go.sum\\nD  internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go\\nD  internal/canonicaljson/canonical_test.go\\nMM internal/catalog/catalog.go\\nMM internal/catalog/catalog.v0.5.0.json\\nMM internal/catalog/catalog_gen.go\\nMM internal/catalog/catalog_test.go\\nMM internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go\\nMM internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main_test.go\\nMM internal/cataloggen/generate.go\\nMM internal/cataloggen/generate_test.go\\nD  internal/scalar/integer.go\\nD  internal/scalar/names.go\\nD  internal/scalar/path.go\\nD  internal/scalar/scalar.go\\nD  internal/scalar/scalar_test.go\\nD  internal/scalar/time_digest.go\\nD  internal/scalar/uuid.go\\nMM internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go\\nMM internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json\\nMM internal/traceability/traceability.go\\nMM internal/traceability/traceability_test.go\\nMM task-board.config.json\\n?? go.sum\\n?? internal/canonicaljson/\\n?? internal/scalar/\\ntask-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e\\nfb61f3d (HEAD -> task-board/story/STORY-260830-2wdm5e) TASK-260830-236x9n: TASK-260830-236x9n: implement-jcs-and-self-identities\\n006ba4e TASK-260830-1pbx0c: TASK-260830-1pbx0c: implement-common-scalar-types\\nc9e5290 (origin/story/normative-pin-scope-completion, story/normative-pin-scope-completion) Record STORY-260831-2xdhn9 board state\\n60c1b62 STORY-260831-2xdhn9: STORY-260831-2xdhn9: normative-pin-scope-completion\\n3679514 (origin/task/close-landing-gate-task, task/close-landing-gate-task, task-board/story/STORY-260831-2xdhn9) Close landing gate configuration task\\nREADME.md\\ninternal/scalar/scalar_test.go\\ninternal/scalar/path.go\\ninternal/scalar/uuid.go\\ninternal/scalar/names.go\\ninternal/scalar/integer.go\\ninternal/scalar/scalar.go\\ninternal/scalar/time_digest.go\\ninternal/scalar/fuzz_test.go\\ninternal/canonicaljson/golden_test.go\\ninternal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go\\ninternal/canonicaljson/validation_config_test.go\\ninternal/canonicaljson/canonical.go\\ninternal/canonicaljson/canonical_test.go\\ninternal/canonicaljson/fuzz_test.go\\ninternal/traceability/traceability.go\\ninternal/traceability/traceability_test.go\\ninternal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go\\ninternal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main.go\\ninternal/catalog/catalog_gen.go\\ninternal/catalog/catalog_test.go\\ninternal/catalog/catalog.go\\ninternal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main_test.go\\ninternal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go\\ninternal/cataloggen/generate_test.go\\ninternal/cataloggen/generate.go\\ninternal/specpin/pin_test.go\\ninternal/specpin/pin.go\\ninternal/specpin/scope_test.go\\ninternal/specpin/sections.go\\ngo.mod\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0591b-218e-7791-8c1d-c09fdfe98b6c","create_time":1788201369.565206}}}
